% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% CHATSV - the chat room server, as a SINTRAN RT program.
%
% COMPILED AND RUN ON D100, 2026-08-18. It served a real room to two real
% clients on two terminals, and the round trip both ways is in the log:
%
%     routed letter    rcv st= 0  t= 2  len= 25   hdr ends= 16   parse k=1 nlen=5
%     ordinary msg     rcv st= 0  t= 1  len= 14   parse k=4 nlen=5   slot now= 1
%
% Those two lines came from diagnostics that have since been taken out; what
% each of them proved is written beside the code it proved it about.
%
% Every XMSG routine below, and the way it is CALLED, is taken from the routine
% reference and worked examples in
%     Operations/Cosmos/ND-60164-3-EN COSMOS Programmer Guide.md
% rather than from analogy. Four things that reference corrected, each of which
% would have cost a compile or worse:
%
%   XMPOPNM -> XMPOPCN   a room has SEATS, and the free-connection counter
%                        belongs to a CONNECTION port. With XMPOPNM the limit is
%                        silently absent while the C# side still enforces it.
%   XMPFFRE -> XMPFREL   XMPFFRE does not exist.
%   XMPFREA/XMPFWRI      take NO message identifier - they act on the CURRENT
%                        message, as XMPFSND sends it.
%   ADDR(buf)            is not enough. The guide's own examples write
%                        addr(buf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS.
%
% And every call RETURNS its status - the examples all end "=: returnStatus".
% That is how the statuses below are checked; XMOK is zero, from XMP-B02:DEFS.
%
% THE MESSAGE FORMAT IS OURS. We chose it; no ND ever sent one, so no capture
% will ever confirm it and Xmsg.Chat/ChatMessage.cs plus its tests are its only
% specification. The rules below are a transcription of Xmsg.Chat/ChatRoom.cs,
% which has the tests that say what "correct" means.
%
%     byte 0      kind          1 Join 2 Welcome 3 Reject 4 Say 5 Said
%                               6 Leave 7 Joined 8 Left 9 Rename 10 Renamed
%     byte 1      nickname length
%     bytes 2..   nickname
%     then        2 bytes text length, big endian
%     then        the text
%
% THE BYTE LAYOUT IS PINNED IN C#, and this file must agree with it by hand.
% SRC/Xmsg.Chat.Tests/ChatMessageGoldenBytesTests.cs holds the exact bytes of a
% Said, a Renamed, a Join and a 300-character line. There is no shared code
% between that and this - only those goldens - so if the layout ever changes,
% both sides change in the SAME commit or they disagree in silence.
%
%
% THREE CORRECTIONS FROM APPENDIX G, the guide's own sample CLIENT and SERVER:
%
%   flags are 2**BIT      XFWTF is a BIT POSITION (15), not a flags word. The
%                         samples both write "2**XFWTF =: FLAGS". Passing 15
%                         would set bits 0,1,2,3 - four unrelated options - and
%                         the wait would simply not happen.
%   XMMSGIDENTIFIER       the message-identifier type, with two M's. Written
%                         "XMMSGIDENTIFIER: MSGIDENT" in the sample.
%   XMUSERADDRESS         and the address idiom is
%                         ADDR(BUF(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS. ROUND brackets -
%                         the guide prints square ones and PLANC F rejects
%                         them with EXPECTS ")" ILLEGAL SYNTAX "[".
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

MODULE chatsv

% THE REAL COSMOS BINDING, from ND-10609, and INSIDE the module. Above MODULE
% the declarations land in the outer scope, the body cannot see them, and every
% call fails NOT PREVIOUSLY DECLARED while the include's own lines draw no
% diagnostic at all - so they look accepted and simply are not there.
%
% Do NOT also include XMSG-PL-VALUES-L:INCL: it shares 184 constant names with
% XMP-B02:DEFS. DEFS is the authority for this library layer, VALUES-L for the
% raw MON 200B interface underneath it.
$INCLUDE XMP-B02:DEFS
$INCLUDE XMP-B02:IMPT

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% FILE MONITOR CALLS, for the log. Same three CHAT.PLNC uses for CHAT:CNFG,
% declared the same way, because that pair has been working on D100 since
% 2026-08-19 and a second spelling of a proven call is a second thing to get
% wrong.
%
%   MON50  OpenFile   name, type, access -> device number. RAISES ROUTINEERROR
%                     on failure, and leaves 46 in the device number when the
%                     file is not there - NOT zero. Never test the number.
%   MON2   OutByte    the byte is the IN-VALUE: "b MON2(dev)".
%   MON43  CloseFile  and it is 43, not 54 - 54 is DELETE.
%
% They come from MON-CALL-1B-A00, which this program already links for
% MONITOR_CALL - see the LOAD line in CHATSV:MODE.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES, BYTES, INTEGER) : MON50 )
IMPORT ( ROUTINE BYTE, VOID (INTEGER) : MON2 )
IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER) : MON43 )

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% RANDOM BLOCK ACCESS, for the per-room history ring.
%
%   MN117  ReadFromFile  (RFILE 117B)
%   MN120  WriteToFile   (WFILE 120B)
%
%     (dev, zero, dadr, bl, words)
%      dev    the device number MON50 gave back
%      zero   the WAIT flag: 0 suspends until the transfer is done, which is
%             what we want - anything else means checking with AwaitFileTransfer
%      dadr   the buffer, an INTEGER ARRAY. WORDS, not bytes.
%      bl     the block to start at. The FIRST BLOCK IS 0, and -1 means "the
%             next one".
%      words  how many words to move.
%
% THE NAME LOSES ITS "O" ABOVE 76, and this is not a typo. Asked the library
% itself rather than guessed:
%
%   Brl: LIST-BRF-ENTRIES PLANC-1BANK-F00,,
%        ... MON73 MON74 MON75 MON76  MN104 MN113 MN114 MN117 MN120 MN122 ...
%
% "MON104" would be six characters and an ND symbol is five, so the O goes.
% The PLANC manual's appendix looks self-contradictory for the same reason -
% its HEADING says "MON117" and the routine it declares is "MN117". The
% routine name is the one to import. One command settled it; the alternative
% was a guessed name and a four-minute compile to find out.
%
% WHY RANDOM AND NOT THE SEQUENTIAL PAIR ABOVE: MON1/MON2 move one byte and
% cannot overwrite in place, so a ring built on them means rewriting the whole
% file - on every message, or on every N with a compaction step. One message
% per 512-byte block makes an append a SINGLE MN120 at block (id MOD N): no
% read-modify-write, no compaction, and a file whose size is fixed the day the
% room is created.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER ARRAY, INTEGER, &
    INTEGER) : MN117 )
IMPORT ( ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER ARRAY, INTEGER, &
    INTEGER) : MN120 )

    % ----- message kinds, mirroring ChatMessageKind.cs ----------------------
    INTEGER : kJoin    := 1
    INTEGER : kWelcome := 2
    INTEGER : kReject  := 3
    INTEGER : kSay     := 4
    INTEGER : kSaid    := 5
    INTEGER : kLeave   := 6
    INTEGER : kJoined  := 7
    INTEGER : kLeft    := 8
    INTEGER : kRename  := 9
    INTEGER : kRenamed := 10

    % ONE KIND, BOTH DIRECTIONS: the member asks with an empty text, the room
    % answers with the same kind and the names in the text. See buildWho.
    INTEGER : kWho     := 11

    % ALSO ONE KIND BOTH DIRECTIONS, same shape as kWho.
    %
    % kTopic  ask: empty text.  set: the new topic in the text.
    %         answer: the ROOM NAME in the name field, the topic in the text.
    %         A SET is broadcast to the room; an ASK goes to the asker alone.
    % kRooms  ask: empty text. answer: "NAME(count)" per room, space separated.
    %
    % Room kinds live in 1..31 and admin starts at 32 - see the note there.
    % These MUST match DOC/protocols/chat-wire.json and ChatMessageKind.cs, and
    % all three move in the same commit.
    % 12 IS NOT FREE - it is Map, reserved for /map (task #77). The registry
    % had already allocated all three when this was written, and taking 12 for
    % the topic would have put /topic and /map on the same number. Read
    % DOC/protocols/chat-wire.json BEFORE picking a kind, not after.
    % /map - the machines and their trunks, for an ordinary member. A ROOM kind
    % and not an admin one on purpose: SEEING how the network is wired is not
    % the same right as REWIRING it. Reserved here since 2026-08-20; built
    % 2026-08-22. See buildMap.
    INTEGER : kMap     := 12

    INTEGER : kRooms   := 13
    INTEGER : kTopic   := 14

    % THE ANSWER TO /who WHEN THE ASKER IS IN NO ROOM.
    %
    % Ronny, 2026-08-22: "running /who when not in a room should list all rooms
    % and all people in the rooms." A person who has left, or who was refused a
    % nickname, has no seat and therefore no room to filter by - and the useful
    % answer to them is the whole house, not silence.
    %
    % A SEPARATE KIND, NOT kWho, because the client LABELS a kWho answer with
    % the room it thinks it is in - "in (LOBBY): ..." - and putting every room's
    % people under that label would print something FALSE, which is the exact
    % mistake /who itself made until 2026-08-22 (see buildWho). A different kind
    % costs one number and makes the label impossible to get wrong.
    %
    % ONE DIRECTION ONLY, unlike its neighbours: the client never sends 15. It
    % sends kWho and the SERVER decides which answer fits, because the server is
    % the one that knows whether the asker has a seat.
    INTEGER : kAllWho  := 15

    % REPLAYED HISTORY - one of these per remembered message, sent to a joiner
    % before the room hears they arrived.
    %
    % A DISTINCT KIND AND NOT kSaid, decided 2026-08-22 and written into the
    % registry with the reason. Replaying kSaid would be less code and the
    % client already renders it - but then a client CANNOT TELL history from
    % live traffic, and the difference matters the moment it timestamps, beeps
    % or counts unread. Taking the cheap option here would be choosing to make
    % those features wrong later.
    %
    % Same shape as kSaid otherwise: the speaker in the name field, what they
    % said in the text.
    INTEGER : kHist    := 16

    % ----- DIRECT MESSAGES, one person to one person -----------------------
    %
    % These four are 17..20, inside the ROOM block (1..31), because a direct
    % message is ordinary member traffic on the service port - it configures
    % nothing, so it is not an admin kind.
    %
    % kDirect   client to server. The TARGET rides in the TEXT before a '/',
    %           exactly as kSay's room does on a trunk - one layout for the
    %           whole family rather than a special case per direction. The
    %           target is either a bare alias (RONNY) or one qualified by
    %           machine (D102!RONNY).
    % kDirected server to the TARGET. The name field holds the sender
    %           QUALIFIED - D102!KARI, never a bare KARI - because what is
    %           shown has to be what can be typed back, and a bare name is
    %           ambiguous the moment two machines each hold one. The text is
    %           the message alone: the target is whoever is reading it.
    % kDirSent  server to the SENDER, naming who it actually went to. Sent on
    %           EVERY delivery and not only on ambiguous ones - refusing an
    %           ambiguous alias protects the sender when the collision is
    %           visible, this protects them when it is not, and it costs one
    %           line to make a wrong delivery visible at once.
    % kDirBad   server to the SENDER: not delivered, and why. The name field
    %           holds the target that was tried, the text the reason - and for
    %           an ambiguous alias the CANDIDATES, so they can be retyped
    %           straight off the screen.
    %
    % THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO STORE AND FORWARD. "not logged in" is a refusal,
    % not a queue. A message that waits for somebody is a mailbox - a different
    % product with different rules, and SINTRAN already has mail for that.
    INTEGER : kDirect   := 17
    INTEGER : kDirected := 18
    INTEGER : kDirSent  := 19
    INTEGER : kDirBad   := 20

    % ADMIN kinds - 32 and up, and they belong on the command port. These MUST
    % match DOC/protocols/chat-wire.json and ChatMessageKind.cs; all three move
    % in the same commit, which is constraint 5 of the federation design.
    INTEGER : kAdmStat := 32
    INTEGER : kAdmRepl := 33
    INTEGER : kAdmStop := 34

    % Trunk configuration, from CHAT-MON on the command port. These CONFIGURE the
    % network, which is why they are admin kinds and not room kinds - seeing how
    % the network is wired (/map, kind 12) is not the same right as rewiring it.
    % All three answer with kAdmRepl, so CHAT-MON needs no new decoding.
    INTEGER : kAdmTrunk   := 35
    INTEGER : kAdmUntrnk  := 36

    % INITIALIZE <room> <n> - how many messages a room remembers.
    %
    % An ADMIN kind because it configures the server, and it is the one thing
    % the history design asked for that the ring itself did not provide: "a
    % CONSTANT, default around 50 messages per room, CONFIGURABLE PER ROOM ...
    % not a hard-coded 50 buried in the source".
    INTEGER : kAdmInit    := 38
    INTEGER : kAdmTrunks  := 37

    % SERVER TO SERVER, on the trunk port. 48 and up, left clear of the admin
    % block so a future admin verb cannot collide with a trunk one.
    %
    % ONE KIND, BOTH DIRECTIONS, and the TEXT'S FIRST BYTE says which way: 0 is
    % "I am here, are you?" and 1 is "I am here, and I am answering you". Without
    % that byte two servers answering each other's HELLO would say hello for
    % ever. An ANSWER is never answered.
    INTEGER : kTrkHello   := 48
    INTEGER : trkAsk      := 0
    INTEGER : trkAnswer   := 1

    % Phase C - "tell me who is on you" and the answer.
    %
    % TWO KINDS, NOT ONE, unlike the hello. The hello could share a kind because
    % its two directions carry the same thing; these do not - the ask is empty
    % and the answer is a list - so a receiver would have to look at the length
    % to know which it had. A kind per direction is cheaper to read and cannot
    % be got wrong.
    %
    % THE ANSWER'S TEXT is "NAME/ROOM NAME/ROOM ...", space between people and a
    % slash between a person and their room. Readable on a trace, and it needs
    % no length table - the same shape /who already sends to a client.
    INTEGER : kTrkWho     := 49
    INTEGER : kTrkMbrs    := 50

    % Phase D - somebody spoke on another machine.
    %
    % NAME field = the speaker, unqualified, as their own machine knows them.
    % TEXT = "ROOM/what they said". The receiving machine adds the "@Dnnn",
    % because only IT knows which system the letter came from - taken from the
    % magic, not from anything the sender claimed. A speaker cannot forge the
    % machine they are on.
    %
    % NOT RELAYED ONWARD. A message received on a trunk is delivered to local
    % members and stops. With two machines that is complete; with three it will
    % need a hop count and an origin, which is the rest of phase D and is not
    % built. Forwarding blindly now would loop the moment a third machine
    % appeared.
    INTEGER : kTrkSaid    := 51
    % A line being RELAYED onward. Same shape as kTrkSaid with three bytes in
    % front: the origin system, big-endian, then how many hops it may still
    % take. A SEPARATE kind rather than extra fields on kTrkSaid so a server
    % that has not been upgraded keeps handling its direct trunks unchanged -
    % these machines are upgraded one at a time.
    INTEGER : kTrkRelay   := 52
    % How far a line may travel from the machine it was typed on. Three is
    % ample for D100/D102/D103 and bounds the damage if a future mesh ever
    % loops: the count is what makes the loop terminate, not the topology.
    INTEGER : maxHops     := 3
    % A relayed line that also carries the number its ORIGIN gave it. Same
    % shape as kTrkRelay with two more bytes after the hop count.
    %
    % THE ID MUST COME FROM THE ORIGIN. It cannot be handed out by a relay: a
    % machine with two peers sends one line to both, each would stamp its own
    % number, and a machine further out would see two names for one line and
    % show it twice - which is the very thing this exists to stop.
    %
    % A SEPARATE kind rather than a longer kTrkRelay header, for the same
    % reason kTrkRelay is separate from kTrkSaid: a server that has not been
    % upgraded would read the two extra bytes as a name length and a name and
    % show its users rubbish. A kind it does not know it just ignores.
    INTEGER : kTrkRelId   := 53

    % A DIRECT MESSAGE CROSSING A TRUNK. Same six-byte header as kTrkRelId -
    % origin system, hops remaining, line id - so it is relayed and de-duplicated
    % by the machinery that already exists rather than by a second mechanism.
    %
    % The difference is what the text holds: kTrkRelId packs ROOM/message, this
    % packs TARGET/message, which leaves the name field free for the SENDER -
    % the one thing the far end has to display.
    INTEGER : kTrkDirect  := 54
    % How many recent lines we remember in order to spot a repeat. A second
    % copy arrives within a hop or two of the first, so a short window is
    % enough, and a FIXED one cannot be made to grow by a peer sending us
    % traffic.
    INTEGER : maxSeen     := 64

    % Set by an AdminStop. Checked at the bottom of the loop rather than acted
    % on where it arrives, so the reply path and the reaping finish first - a
    % stop that abandons a half-sent message is not a clean stop.
    BOOLEAN : stopWanted := FALSE

    % THERE IS NO ELEVENTH KIND, and the reason is worth keeping.
    %
    % A kHello was invented here on 2026-08-18 to answer the connect on its own,
    % on the belief that a connection port is handed "4 bytes with byte 0 = 0"
    % and that the joiner's nickname could only follow as a second, ordinary
    % message. That belief was wrong, and it was wrong because this program was
    % reading the WRONG BYTES: the connect arrives with the XSLET letter header
    % still in front of it, and byte 0 of that header is the serial number.
    %
    % The nickname was in the very first arrival all along. See the header skip
    % in the receive loop for the layout and the manual reference. The join is
    % ONE step, exactly as the Programmer Guide describes it: the letter carries
    % the user data, the server answers it, and "thereby gives away its own
    % magic number" - the welcome IS the answer, so no extra kind is needed.

    % XFWTF is the BIT POSITION (15). The flags word is 2 to that power - the
    % sample programs in Appendix G both write "2**XFWTF =: FLAGS". Passing 15
    % itself would set bits 0 to 3, four unrelated options, and the wait would
    % not happen at all.
    % XFWTF COMES FROM XMP-B02:DEFS - do NOT declare it here. Declaring our own
    % drew IDENTIFIER ALREADY SPECIFIED/DECLARED "XFWTF", and the compiler then
    % fell over with ASSERT VIOLATION AT 136747B and took the batch job with it.
    % A redeclaration is not a warning you can carry on past in this compiler.
    % ZERO, and it stays zero - see the PROGRAM body for the measurement. Flags
    % zero is XMSG's own no-wait: XFRCV returns at once whether or not a message
    % was there, which is the only way one loop can poll TWO ports.
    INTEGER : waitFlags := 0

    % 2**XFSEC, computed at start. SECURE, and the reason is a measurement.
    %
    % These sends used to carry flags ZERO, and the guide is explicit about what
    % that costs: "Non-secure messages are discarded and released by XMSG if they
    % cannot be delivered." So a member whose terminal died was written to for
    % ever, in silence.
    %
    % THE OBVIOUS ALTERNATIVE WAS TRIED FIRST AND IS REFUTED. XMPSEND is handed a
    % magic number, and a magic carries a generation, so it looked as though XMSG
    % would refuse one naming a closed port on the spot - XMXEIMA, "Invalid magic
    % number". MEASURED 2026-08-18 on D100: two clients in the room, one killed
    % with ESC ("USER BREAK AT 17202B"), the other spoke. The broadcast to the
    % dead port returned XMOK. Not one status printed, the seat stayed spent, and
    % LIST-NAMES held at 14. XMPSEND DOES NOT VALIDATE THE DESTINATION.
    %
    % So the return has to come back as a MESSAGE, which is what XFSEC buys:
    % "The message will be returned to the sending port if it cannot be
    % delivered, or if the receiving port is closed (e.g., if the receiving task
    % terminates)". It arrives as msgType XMTRE and is handled in the receive
    % loop.
    %
    % MEASURED WITH THE FLAG SET, same two clients, same kill: the bounce came
    % back carrying reason 16915 - which is XMXEIMA, the very code the refuted
    % attempt was watching for. It was never the send's status; it is the reason
    % on the return. CHAT-LOBBY went 16 free, 14 with both joined, back to 15
    % once the dead one was reaped.
    INTEGER : sendFlags := 0

    % ----- the room ---------------------------------------------------------
    % maxSeats is what XMPOPCN is given, so XROUT turns the SEVENTEENTH joiner
    % away before this program sees them. The array bounds and that argument are
    % the same number on purpose.
    %
    % THE CLIENT DOES NOT KNOW THIS NUMBER AND MUST NOT. Neither CHAT.PLNC nor
    % ChatClient.cs mentions it anywhere - XROUT does the admission itself, before
    % this program is woken, so a client that is turned away simply gets a refusal
    % it has to handle in any case. The room's size is the room's business, and
    % raising it is this file alone. The C# ChatRoom has no seat limit at all.
    %
    % Raised from 8 to 16 on 2026-08-18. Nothing else has to move with it: the
    % four arrays below and every FOR loop are written in terms of maxSeats, and
    % the only literal bounds are the array declarations, which are next to it.
    INTEGER : maxSeats := 16
    INTEGER : nameLen  := 16

    INTEGER4 ARRAY : memberMagic(1:16)

    % A two-dimensional BYTES array: sixteen names of sixteen bytes. Legal PLANC -
    % the reference gives BYTES ARRAY : b1(1:2,0:3) directly.
    %
    % Note that memberName(slot) with the LAST subscript omitted refers to the
    % WHOLE string, which is tempting for copying a name in one statement. It is
    % NOT used here, because a name is variable length and a whole-row copy would
    % carry the sixteen bytes including whatever follows the name. The explicit
    % loops are longer and right.
    % SIXTEEN BYTES PER NAME, and the number is named because the wire decides
    % how many bytes arrive. A nickname length is one byte, so a peer can ask for
    % up to 255 - and PLANC checks no array bound. Copying an unchecked length in
    % here writes straight through the other seven members' names and out the far
    % side of the array. See the guard at the top of handleMessage.
    INTEGER        : maxNameLen := 16
    % NAMES ARE UNIQUE IN THEIR FIRST TEN CHARACTERS, and that is not a style
    % note - it is what the compiler enforces. memberName and memberNameLen both
    % start MEMBERNAME, so the second silently became a redeclaration of the
    % first: IDENTIFIER ALREADY SPECIFIED/DECLARED "MEMBERNAME", and then every
    % memberName(slot, i) drew MORE SUBSCRIPTS THAN IN THE ARRAY DECLARATION,
    % because the name now referred to the one-dimensional array. Four errors,
    % one cause, and none of them mention the length. The listing gives it away:
    % it prints the TRUNCATED names - BUILDRENAM, BUILDFROMS, REASONNICK.
    BYTES ARRAY    : memberName(1:16, 1:16)
    INTEGER ARRAY  : memberLen(1:16)
    INTEGER ARRAY  : memberUsed(1:16)

    % Seats whose member has VANISHED - the terminal was killed, the client was
    % aborted with ESC, the task died. Marked by sendTo when a send comes back
    % saying the port is gone, and cleared by reapDead.
    %
    % A MARK IS NOT AN EVICTION, and the separation is the whole point of this
    % array rather than a nicety. sendTo is called from inside broadcast's loop,
    % and evicting there would have to announce the departure - which means
    % building a message into outBuf while broadcast is still part way through
    % sending the outBuf it built. One shared buffer, so the message everybody
    % else is waiting for would be overwritten mid-broadcast and the room would
    % receive the departure notice instead of what was said. Marking is the only
    % thing safe to do at that depth; the acting happens back at the top.
    INTEGER ARRAY  : memberDead(1:16)

    % ---- WHICH ROOM EACH MEMBER IS IN ------------------------------------
    %
    % The room used to be a PORT NAME - the client built "CHAT-" plus the room
    % and addressed that - so the server never had to know: whoever reached
    % this port was, by definition, in this room. One port now, so the room is
    % a field in the Join and the server has to remember it per member.
    %
    % Sixteen characters, same as a nickname. A room is a name a person types.
    BYTES ARRAY    : mbrRoom(1:16, 1:16)
    INTEGER ARRAY  : mbrRoomLen(1:16)

    % Where a member goes when the Join names no room - see copyRoomIn.
    BYTES          : homeRoom := 'LOBBY'
    INTEGER        : lenHomeRoom := 5

    % ---- ROOM TOPICS -----------------------------------------------------
    %
    % A SEPARATE TABLE, NOT A COLUMN IN THE MEMBER TABLE, and the reason is
    % that a topic OUTLIVES the people who set it. Hanging it off a seat would
    % throw it away the moment the last person left, which is exactly when a
    % topic is doing its job: telling the next person what the room is for.
    %
    % Keyed by ROOM NAME, because that is all a room is here - there is no room
    % id and no room record. An entry is made the first time somebody sets a
    % topic, and once made it stays for the life of the server.
    %
    % EIGHT rooms, not sixteen. Sixteen seats could in principle be sixteen
    % rooms of one person each, so this table CAN fill up - and when it does,
    % setTopic refuses in the open rather than silently dropping the topic. A
    % refusal a user can read beats a feature that quietly does nothing.
    INTEGER        : maxTopics := 8
    BYTES ARRAY    : topicRoom(1:8, 1:16)
    INTEGER ARRAY  : topicRoomLen(1:8)

    % 64 characters. The whole message must fit outBuf's 250-byte text field
    % alongside the room name, and a topic is a headline, not an essay.
    INTEGER        : maxTopicLen := 64
    BYTES ARRAY    : topicText(1:8, 1:64)
    INTEGER ARRAY  : topicTextLen(1:8)

    % ----- the per-room history ring ---------------------------------------
    %
    % Ronny, 2026-08-20: somebody joining should see what was just said instead
    % of an empty screen, and it should survive the server being stopped.
    %
    % IT SHARES THE TOPIC TABLE'S SLOTS, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE STRUCTURE. The
    % design note used to say this needed a room table first, because rooms are
    % DERIVED from the seats and there is no per-room anything. There already
    % is one: topicRoom/topicRoomLen with findTopic and claimTopic is a room
    % table under another name. Using the same index for history means the two
    % can never disagree about what a room is - and a room that has neither a
    % topic nor a history needs no entry at all.
    %
    % ONE 512-BYTE BLOCK PER MESSAGE, block = id MOD histSize. An append is a
    % single MN120 - no read-modify-write, no compaction, and the file's size is
    % fixed the day the room is created. See the note beside the imports.
    %
    % THE FILE IS NAMED FROM THE INDEX, NEVER THE ROOM NAME: CHATH1:DATA up to
    % CHATH8:DATA. Room names are chosen by users and a SINTRAN file name is
    % short, case-folded and must be unique - deriving one from arbitrary text
    % invites collisions and illegal names with no good failure mode. The room
    % NAME is written into block 0 so the file is still self-describing.
    INTEGER        : maxHistRooms := 8

    % The next id to be written, per room. IT ONLY EVER GOES UP: it is what
    % makes "block = id MOD size" a ring, and it is the same number the unread
    % count will need - the server reports the room's latest, the client
    % subtracts its own anchor. One number doing two jobs, so it has to be right.
    INTEGER ARRAY  : histNext(1:8)

    % How many messages this room keeps. Per room, not one constant for the
    % whole server, because that is what was asked for. 50 until CHAT-MON can
    % set it.
    % 200, Ronny 2026-08-23. It is also maxHistSize, so the default IS the
    % ceiling and INITIALIZE can only ever lower a room - which is the right way
    % round: the limit exists to stop a typo asking for megabytes, not to stop
    % anybody keeping what they meant to keep.
    %
    % 200 messages is 200 blocks is 100KB a room, and eight rooms is 800KB -
    % the same figure the no-total decision was taken against, so the bound has
    % not moved. It is now the ordinary case rather than the worst one.
    INTEGER        : histDefault := 200

    % AND A CEILING, because INITIALIZE takes a number from an operator and a
    % ring of 30000 blocks is 15 megabytes of disk asked for by a typo. 200 is
    % well past anything a room needs to show a new arrival and still only
    % 100KB a room.
    INTEGER        : maxHistSize := 200
    INTEGER ARRAY  : histSize(1:8)

    % CreateFile's page count. INTEGER4 because that is what 221B's parameter
    % is - startAddr beside it is INTEGER4 for the same reason, and passing a
    % plain INTEGER to a call that wants a double word is the kind of mistake
    % that compiles and then writes somewhere unintended.
    INTEGER4       : histPages := 0

    % The open file, or 0 for "not opened yet". Opened once and left open: a
    % room is written to on every message, and opening per message would put a
    % file open and close in the path of every line anybody types.
    INTEGER ARRAY  : histDev(1:8)

    % One block, as WORDS, because that is what MN117 and MN120 move.
    % 256 words = 512 bytes = one SINTRAN block.
    INTEGER        : histWords := 256
    INTEGER ARRAY  : histBlock(0:255)

    % The file name being built.
    %
    % IT NAMES THE USER, AND THAT IS NOT DECORATION: "(SYSTEM)CHATH1".
    %
    % MEASURED ON D100, 2026-08-23. With a bare "CHATH1" the server created
    % nothing and opened nothing - and after (SYSTEM)CHATH1:DATA was created BY
    % HAND from the terminal, 50 pages, FILE-STATISTICS still said
    % "OPENED 0 TIMES". The program was looking somewhere that is not SYSTEM.
    %
    % Which somewhere is not settled: an RT program does not run as the user
    % who loaded it, and "CREATE-FILE (RT)CHATH1:DATA,50" typed as SYSTEM is
    % refused with NOT DIRECTORY ACCESS, so that guess cannot even be tested
    % from a terminal.
    %
    % NAMING THE USER REMOVES THE QUESTION rather than answering it. A server
    % that keeps its files somewhere definite is better than one that depends
    % on whatever user SINTRAN decides an RT program is - and the files then
    % sit beside CHATSV:PROG where anybody looking for them would look.
    %
    % 22 bytes: "(SYSTEM)CHATH1" is 14, and the quoted form that means CREATE
    % is 16. Room to spare rather than a count that has to be re-derived if the
    % stem ever changes.
    BYTES          : histName(0:21)
    BYTES          : histUser := '(SYSTEM)'
    INTEGER        : lenHistUser := 8
    BYTES          : histStem := 'CHATH'
    BYTES          : histType := 'DATA'

    % Is there anything to reap? Kept so the common case - nothing died - costs
    % one compare per message instead of a walk of the whole room.
    INTEGER        : deadFound := 0

    % ----- knowing when to give up ------------------------------------------
    % How many receives in a ROW have failed. Cleared by any good one, so this
    % counts a run of trouble rather than trouble over a lifetime.
    INTEGER        : badRuns := 0

    % Twenty in a row is not a bad moment, it is a broken machine. Small enough
    % that a spin is caught in under a second, large enough that a burst of
    % something transient is ridden out rather than treated as fatal.
    INTEGER        : maxBadRuns := 20

    % Print at most this many of them. The fault that made all this necessary
    % was ONE message repeated as fast as the terminal could take it, and a
    % diagnostic with no limit buries the line that says what happened.
    INTEGER        : maxNoisy := 5

    INTEGER ARRAY : stack(0:1000)

    % SIZE OF inBuf, named because the read must clamp against it. XMSG reports
    % the SENDER'S message size; nothing stops that being larger than this
    % array, and PLANC checks no bound.
    INTEGER : inBufSize := 256
    BYTES : inBuf(0:255)
    BYTES : outBuf(0:255)

    % ONE SPACE, AS A BYTE. buildWho puts a separator between names, and
    % "' ' =: outBuf(at)" does not compile: 'x' is a STRING and an element of a
    % BYTES array is a BYTE. Held as a one-element BYTES so the copy is
    % byte-to-byte.
    BYTES : spaceByte := ' '

    % Same trick, for the room counts /list writes - "LOBBY(2)". Brackets are
    % held as one-element BYTES for exactly the reason spaceByte is.
    BYTES : openParen  := '('
    BYTES : closeParen := ')'

    % '@', for qualifying a remote member - "ANNA@D102". Two machines can each
    % have an ANNA, and a bare name would make them look like one person.
    BYTES : atByte     := '@'

    % '0' as a NUMBER, because a digit is computed rather than copied: the
    % count is arithmetic, so the character is digitZero + the value. Held as
    % an INTEGER and not a BYTES for that reason - it is added to, not copied.
    INTEGER : digitZero := 48

    % Same reason as spaceByte: a string literal cannot be stored into a BYTES
    % element, so every literal character this program writes into a buffer
    % lives in a one-element BYTES and is copied byte to byte.
    % A LOCAL CANNOT CARRY AN INITIALISER. "BYTES : oneByte := ' '" inside
    % showName drew *** ERROR - INITIAL VALUE ILLEGAL HERE, while the identical
    % line at MODULE level compiles - which is why the same declaration works in
    % CHATMON and failed here. Module level it is, beside the others.
    BYTES : oneByte   := ' '
    BYTES : slashByte := '/'
    BYTES : zeroByte  := '0'

    % '!', between a MACHINE and a PERSON in a direct message - D102!RONNY.
    %
    % NOT '@', and the choice is deliberate rather than a matter of taste. '@'
    % is the client's short command prefix, so @RONNY@D100 would need a parser
    % that counts at-signs and decides which one it meant. 'machine!alias'
    % reads left to right as route-then-person and has precedent in the UUCP
    % addressing of the same era. The byte is pinned as 0x21 by the C# test
    % AQualifiedTargetKeepsItsMachineSeparator, so the two ends cannot drift.
    %
    % Note that '@' still qualifies a name for DISPLAY - "ANNA@D102" in /who.
    % Display and addressing are different jobs: one is read by a person, the
    % other is typed by one.
    BYTES : bangByte  := '!'

    % ':', for the all-rooms /who - "LOBBY: TESTER OLAV". Same reason as every
    % other single character in this list: a string literal cannot be stored
    % into a BYTES element, so it lives in a one-element BYTES and is copied.
    BYTES : colonByte := ':'

    % '"', and it is not decoration: quoting a file name is how you say CREATE
    % to MON50, which would otherwise require the file to exist already. See
    % histOpen, where the plain name is tried first and the quoted one only if
    % that fails.
    BYTES : quoteByte := '"'

    % The fixed part of the status line. Held as bytes for the same reason.
    BYTES : statusWord := 'SEATS '

    INTEGER  : myPort
    INTEGER  : msgType

    % ---- COUNTED, NOT PRINTED ---------------------------------------------
    % These three used to print a line each, every time, straight to terminal 1.
    % Two of them are EXPECTED events, so the terminal filled with them and the
    % rare interesting line was buried:
    %
    %   nEmptyLet  a routed letter with no payload. XROUT's ANSWER to a seat
    %              return arrives on our own port looking exactly like this, so
    %              there is one per seat returned - see the note by the drop.
    %   nBounced   a message that came home because nobody was there. One per
    %              dead client, and clients die in bursts when a terminal goes.
    %   nRefused   a join we turned away, whatever the reason.
    %
    % A count answers the question the flood was trying to answer - "is this
    % happening more than it should" - and STATUS shows all three on demand.
    %
    % WHAT A RISING nEmptyLet ACTUALLY MEANS - measured 2026-08-24 from a hub
    % capture, and it is NOT a fault. The trunk exchanges a burst of about 24
    % frames every 2 to 3 minutes when nobody is typing at all: kTrkHello, then
    % kTrkWho, then kTrkMbrs, both directions. Every letter we send that way
    % draws an XROUT answer back onto our own port with no payload, so the count
    % tracks the TRUNK's idle cycle, not the clients. 712 on D100 after an
    % afternoon with one idle client is normal.
    %
    % So read it as a liveness number: climbing steadily means the trunk is up.
    % FLAT while a trunk is supposed to be up is the interesting case.
    INTEGER  : nEmptyLet
    INTEGER  : nBounced
    INTEGER  : nRefused
    % How many lines this machine has passed ON to a further peer. A machine in
    % the MIDDLE of a chain should show this climbing; an end machine should
    % show it flat at zero, and a non-zero count on an end machine means the
    % topology is not what somebody thinks it is.
    INTEGER  : nRelayed

    % How many repeats we recognised and threw away. On a CHAIN this stays at
    % ZERO for ever - there is only one path between any two machines, so no
    % line can arrive twice. It only starts moving once somebody wires a
    % second path, and that is exactly when you want to see it moving.
    INTEGER  : nDupes

    % The number we put on the next line typed HERE. Only this machine ever
    % sets it; a relay passes an id on untouched.
    %
    % It wraps at 65536 and that is harmless: a number can only be mistaken
    % for a live one if we sent 65536 lines while the first was still inside
    % a 64-entry window, which cannot happen.
    INTEGER  : myLineId

    % The lines we have already seen, as two parallel rows - the machine it
    % came from and the number that machine gave it. BOTH are needed: every
    % machine numbers from its own counter, so id 1 exists on all of them at
    % once, and matching on the id alone would silently swallow other
    % machines' lines.
    INTEGER ARRAY  : seenSys(1:64)
    INTEGER ARRAY  : seenId(1:64)
    % Where the next one goes, 1..maxSeen, and how many rows are real.
    % seenUsed matters because a fresh table is all zeros, and (0,0) is a pair
    % a real line could otherwise be mistaken for.
    INTEGER  : seenAt
    INTEGER  : seenUsed

    % ---- THE COMMAND PORT, AND WHY THE LOOP HAD TO CHANGE ------------------
    %
    % XFRCV RECEIVES ON ONE NAMED PORT. There is no receive-on-any-port form -
    % ND-60.134.2 section 4.3.2.10. This program used to block on myPort with
    % XFWTF, and a server that keeps doing that NEVER SEES a message on a
    % second port. Not failing, not logging: sitting in the lobby wait while
    % CHATMON waits for an answer that cannot come.
    %
    % The manual's answer is XFWAK: a non-blocking receive arms a wake-up
    % instead, and "this 'wake up' option can be enabled on more than one port
    % at a time". MON 100B is StartRTProgram, so that wake-up RT-STARTS this
    % program - which means there is no wait to choose at all. Drain both
    % ports, and when both are empty, TERMINATE. A transmission to either one
    % starts us again.
    %
    % AND THE TRAP FROM THE SAME PARAGRAPH: "when the 'wake up' is done, the
    % message is not received, and so the receive must be repeated." The
    % wake-up is a DOORBELL, NOT A DELIVERY.
    INTEGER  : adminPort

    % CHAT-ADMIN is a NORMAL named port (XMPOPNM), NOT a connection port.
    % CHAT-LOBBY is a connection port and every arrival there spends one of the
    % free connections - a seat a human then cannot have. An operator asking
    % for STATUS must not cost the room a seat, and must still get in when the
    % room is full, which is exactly when they would want to ask.
    BYTES    : admName := 'CHAT-ADMIN'
    INTEGER  : lenAdmName := 10

    % ---- THE TRUNK PORT, AND WHY IT IS A THIRD PORT --------------------------
    %
    % Server-to-server traffic. Also XMPOPNM, and for the SAME reason the command
    % port is: a trunk must cost the room NO SEATS. A peer that is switched off
    % gets a HELLO every so often for as long as it is away, and if that spent a
    % free connection each time the room would fill up with machines that are not
    % there. The design calls this constraint 1: the trunk must never point at
    % the room port.
    %
    % A TRUNK IS NOT A CONNECTION. XMSG named ports are message passing - there is
    % no session to open, nothing to hold, and nothing that drops. A trunk is a
    % BELIEF that a peer is reachable, held as soft state and refreshed. Neither
    % side is the client: each is a server when a letter arrives and a client when
    % it sends one.
    BYTES    : trkName := 'CHAT-TRUNK'
    INTEGER  : lenTrkName := 10
    INTEGER  : trunkPort

    % The XSLET letter header XMPBLET builds when this server ORIGINATES a
    % message to another machine. Replies do not need it - they go straight to
    % the magic the message arrived with - so this exists only for trunks.
    BYTES    : letterBuf(0:63)

    % The peer's SYSTEM NAME, built at runtime from its number: 'D' and the
    % decimal digits. XMPBLET's system parameter is a NAME as BYTES, never a
    % number - see the long note on sendToName for the three compile cycles that
    % established it. Declared with explicit BOUNDS because it is filled at
    % runtime, which is also why it is passed as a subarray.
    BYTES    : peerName(0:15)
    BYTES    : dByte := 'D'

    % ---- THE PEER TABLE ------------------------------------------------------
    %
    % One row per trunked machine. SOFT STATE: nothing here is a connection, it
    % is what we currently BELIEVE about a peer, and the belief is refreshed by
    % anything arriving from it.
    %
    % EIGHT is plenty and the bound is deliberate. Trunks are configured by an
    % operator one at a time, not discovered, so a table that cannot overflow
    % quietly is worth more than a big one. TRUNK refuses out loud when it is
    % full.
    INTEGER        : maxPeers := 8
    INTEGER ARRAY  : peerSystem(1:8)      % XMSG system number, 0 = free row
    INTEGER ARRAY  : peerState(1:8)       % see the three constants below
    INTEGER ARRAY  : peerHeard(1:8)       % loop ticks since we last heard from it

    % Peer states. NOT a BOOLEAN pair, because "we have never heard from it" and
    % "we heard from it and then it went quiet" are different things to an
    % operator reading LIST-TRUNKS: the first is probably a configuration
    % mistake, the second is a machine that went away.
    INTEGER        : peerUnknown := 0
    INTEGER        : peerUp      := 1
    INTEGER        : peerDown    := 2

    % How many idle loop ticks before a peer we have heard nothing from is
    % declared Down, and how often we say HELLO to one that is not Up.
    %
    % BACKOFF IS REQUIRED, NOT OPTIONAL - a machine that is switched off must not
    % be probed every few seconds for a week. This first cut uses a flat retry;
    % the design asks for 15s/30s/60s/5min and that is worth doing once the flat
    % one is proved on the wire. Written down here so it is not forgotten.
    % Seconds of silence before a peer we HAD is declared Down.
    INTEGER        : peerDeadAfter := 60

    % ---- BACKOFF, AND WHY IT IS NOT A FIXED INTERVAL ------------------------
    %
    % "Backoff is required, not optional" - the design, and it is right: a
    % machine that is switched off must not be probed every few seconds for a
    % week. Two peers left down over a weekend at a flat 20s would be about
    % 30,000 pointless letters.
    %
    % So the wait DOUBLES after every unanswered try, from 15 seconds up to a
    % cap of five minutes: 15, 30, 60, 120, 240, 300, 300, ... A machine that
    % comes back is found within five minutes at worst, and usually far sooner
    % because ITS OWN hello reaches us the moment it starts - the retry is the
    % fallback, not the mechanism.
    %
    % RESET ON EVERY transition to Up, not just the first, so a peer that flaps
    % does not inherit a five-minute wait from its last absence.
    INTEGER        : peerFirstWait := 15
    INTEGER        : peerMaxWait   := 300
    INTEGER ARRAY  : peerWait(1:8)        % seconds to wait before the next try
    INTEGER ARRAY  : peerCount(1:8)       % seconds left of that wait

    % ---- WHO IS ON THE OTHER MACHINES ---------------------------------------
    %
    % Phase C. A copy of what each peer last told us about its members, so /who
    % can answer across machines.
    %
    % ENTIRELY DERIVED, NEVER AUTHORITATIVE. Every row here was sent by the
    % machine that owns it, and is replaced wholesale the next time that machine
    % speaks. We never add, edit or reason about another machine's members - if
    % this disagrees with the far end, the far end is right and this is stale.
    %
    % KEYED BY THE OWNING SYSTEM so a peer going away can have all of its rows
    % dropped in one pass. A peer that is Down must not leave people apparently
    % chatting from a machine that is not there.
    INTEGER        : maxRemote := 32
    INTEGER ARRAY  : rmtSystem(1:32)      % owning machine, 0 = free row
    BYTES ARRAY    : rmtName(1:32, 1:16)
    INTEGER ARRAY  : rmtNameLen(1:32)
    BYTES ARRAY    : rmtRoom(1:32, 1:16)
    INTEGER ARRAY  : rmtRoomLen(1:32)

    % TRUE once the ports are open. MODULE data lives in the RT segment and
    % SURVIVES the terminate above - the member table, the port numbers and the
    % magic are all still here on the next start. INISTACK is not: it runs
    % every time. So the opening work must happen ONCE, or the second start
    % tries to re-open a name this program already holds and fails.
    BOOLEAN  : portsOpen := FALSE

    % Which port the message being handled arrived on. Admin kinds are refused
    % on the lobby and room kinds on the command port - sharing one vocabulary
    % is not sharing the authority to use any of it anywhere.
    BOOLEAN  : fromAdmin := FALSE

    % Same idea for the trunk port. A kTrkHello arriving on the ROOM port is not
    % a trunk message, it is a member sending something they should not, and the
    % handler drops it rather than trusting it.
    BOOLEAN  : fromTrunk := FALSE

    % Which SYSTEM the message being handled came from, taken apart from the
    % sender's magic number with XMPFM2P. The trunk needs it because a peer is
    % identified by system number, and because an unsolicited HELLO has to be
    % answerable without anything having been configured first.
    INTEGER  : senderSystem := 0
    INTEGER  : senderPortNo := 0
    INTEGER  : senderRtInfo := 0
    INTEGER  : senderExtra  := 0

    % OUR OWN system number, taken apart from our own magic at startup.
    %
    % NEEDED BECAUSE WE HEAR OURSELVES. A hello addressed to a machine that has
    % no CHAT-TRUNK name comes straight BACK, arrives on our own trunk port
    % carrying OUR magic, and without this test the server adds a peer row for
    % itself and calls it up. MEASURED on D100 2026-08-22, the first time a
    % trunk was ever configured:
    %
    %     C-M: START-TRUNK 102
    %     C-M: LIST-TRUNKS   ->   102 unknown 100 up
    %
    % 100 is this machine. Nothing was up; a letter had bounced.
    INTEGER  : mySystem := 0

    % Direct messages delivered, and direct messages refused. On the STATUS
    % line beside the other counters, because a screen cannot tell you either
    % of these - a refusal is seen only by the one person who got it, and a
    % delivery is seen only by two.
    %
    % A CLIMBING refuse count with a flat sent count is the shape of a client
    % or a user getting the address form wrong, which is worth knowing without
    % having to ask anybody.
    INTEGER  : dmSent := 0
    INTEGER  : dmBad  := 0

    % WHAT AN ALIAS RESOLVED TO. Four answers, so they are variables and not a
    % return value - PLANC returns one thing.
    %
    %   dirCount   how many people matched. 0 refuses, 2 or more refuses with
    %              the candidates, exactly 1 is delivered.
    %   dirLocal   the local seat, or 0
    %   dirRemote  the row in the remote table, or 0
    %   dirSystem  the machine the one match is on
    INTEGER  : dirCount  := 0
    INTEGER  : dirLocal  := 0
    INTEGER  : dirRemote := 0
    INTEGER  : dirSystem := 0

    % For the idle sleep - see the empty-drain branch of the main loop.
    % sleepWhy comes back 0 when the time simply elapsed, 1 when a break
    % restarted us. Neither changes what this loop does next, but the call
    % needs somewhere to put it.
    INTEGER  : sleepUnits
    INTEGER  : sleepKind
    INTEGER  : sleepWhy

    % XMROU, "routed message (via XROUT)", is what msgType carries when a letter
    % arrives. It is the ONLY thing that says a SEAT was spent: XROUT takes one
    % free connection to forward a letter, before this program has seen a byte
    % of it.
    %
    % SETTLED FROM ND'S OWN SAMPLE SERVERS, both of them, in the COSMOS
    % Programmer Guide (ND-60.164.3) appendix of sample programs. Each waits for
    % the forwarded letter and then refuses anything else:
    %
    %   PLANC     IF MSGTYPE >< XMROU THEN
    %                 OUTPUT(1, 'A', 'WRONG MESSAGE TYPE ')
    %
    %   FORTRAN   IF (MSGTYPE .NE. XMROU) THEN
    %                 WRITE(1,*)'WRONG MESSAGE TYPE'
    %
    % Both then read the CLIENT's magic number out of the arrived message to
    % answer it directly - which is the other half of the same fact, and the
    % reason the sender field is the client rather than XROUT.
    %
    % The name is used bare here, not declared as a literal, because the PLANC
    % sample uses it bare under the same two includes this file has.

    % Was the sender in the room BEFORE this message was handled? The seat
    % accounting below needs the answer from before, so it is taken early.
    INTEGER  : wasMember
    % Is the sender a member AFTER the letter was handled? That single question
    % decides whether the seat XROUT spent on the arrival goes back.
    INTEGER  : nowMember
    % Byte 1 of a routed arrival, kept BEFORE the payload is shifted down. 65
    % (XSLET) means a client sent it and a seat was spent; anything else is
    % XROUT answering one of our own calls, which spent nothing.
    INTEGER  : letterService

    % XSLET (65) IS NOT DECLARED HERE. It is already a CONSTANT in XMP-B02:DEFS
    % - "CONSTANT XSLET=65  % Send a letter" - and declaring our own would be a
    % redeclaration, which in this compiler is not a warning: it drew
    % IDENTIFIER ALREADY SPECIFIED and then ASSERT VIOLATION AT 136747B and took
    % the whole batch job with it. Checked against the listing before writing
    % this, not assumed.
    INTEGER  : remotePortHash
    INTEGER  : nBytes
    % What XMPFRCV put in nBytes, kept before XMPFMST overwrites it. It is the
    % message length normally and the REASON FOR RETURN on an XMTRE.
    INTEGER  : receiveStat
    INTEGER  : readLength
    INTEGER  : readAmount
    % An INTEGER dumpAmount clamped the 48-byte arrival dump. Both are gone;
    % see the note in the receive loop for what that dump found.
    % Where the chat message starts inside a letter that came via XROUT - that
    % is, the first byte after the XSLET header. Derived from the header's own
    % length word on every arrival, never assumed.
    INTEGER  : payloadAt
    INTEGER  : writtenLength
    INTEGER  : returnStatus
    INTEGER4 : senderMagic

    % OUR OWN port's magic number, read once with XMPFP2M after the port opens.
    % Kept only so an arrival can be tested against it, because the thing that
    % actually happened - our own answer arriving back at this very port - is not
    % something any amount of reading the guide would have suggested, and without
    % this comparison it cannot be told apart from a real peer's message.
    INTEGER4 : myMagic := 0
    BOOLEAN  : uniqueName := TRUE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % THE LOG. An RT program has no terminal, so every OUTPUT(1, ...) in this
    % file goes NOWHERE once CHATSER is started with @RT.
    %
    % That is not a tidiness problem, it is why a whole afternoon was spent
    % guessing on 2026-08-20. Two clients sat in one room and neither heard
    % the other; the one line that would have said why - "SV: snd failed st="
    % from sendTo - was printed to a device that is not there. The fault was
    % looked for in the room table, the wire, the seat counter and the build,
    % in that order, because the program's own account of itself was thrown
    % away.
    %
    % SO EVERY DIAGNOSTIC GOES TO A FILE AS WELL AS THE TERMINAL. Run as
    % @CHATSV you see it on screen AND in the file; run as @RT CHATSER the
    % screen half goes nowhere and the file is all there is.
    %
    % OPEN, WRITE, CLOSE PER LINE - and that is deliberate, not laziness.
    % A crash leaves an open file's block buffer unwritten, so anything still
    % in it is lost - which is exactly the moment the log has to survive. One
    % line per open costs a file system round trip, and these lines are only
    % written on errors and at startup, so the cost is paid when something is
    % already wrong.
    %
    % ACCESS 5 IS SEQUENTIAL WRITE APPEND, from the OpenFile table in
    % ND-860228-2-EN. Access 0 would truncate at CLOSE and each line would
    % erase the one before it - and it truncates at CLOSE rather than at OPEN,
    % which is the trap CHAT.PLNC already carries a note about.
    %
    % AN RT PROGRAM'S DEFAULT USER IS RT, NOT SYSTEM. The file is named
    % without a user, so it resolves against RT first and then SYSTEM. If it
    % is never created, logging simply does nothing - see logBroken.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % CHATLG2, NOT CHATLOG, AND THE REASON MATTERS. The first log this server
    % ever wrote went out as plain 7-bit ASCII, before logPut existed. Access 5
    % APPENDS, so the parity-correct lines written afterwards landed BEHIND 107
    % bytes SINTRAN's own utilities refuse to read, and LIST-FILE stopped at the
    % first of them without a word - the whole file reads as empty. The file
    % belongs to user RT and SYSTEM has no directory access to delete it, so the
    % only way out is a name that has never been written the wrong way.
    % Measured on D100, 2026-08-20.
    BYTES   : logName := 'CHATLG2'
    BYTES   : logType := 'SYMB'

    % Sequential write APPEND. See above - 0 would truncate.
    INTEGER : logAppend := 5

    % Set once the log has failed, and never cleared. A server that cannot
    % write its log must not spend a file system call per diagnostic finding
    % that out again, and must certainly not fail because of it.
    BOOLEAN : logBroken := FALSE

    % Digits, LEAST significant first as they are produced, written out in
    % reverse. Ten is enough for any 16-bit value with room to spare.
    BYTES   : logDigits(0:9)

    % Single characters the log writes, as NUMBERS. A carriage return has no
    % spelling inside a PLANC string literal, and CHAT.PLNC already carries
    % its carriageRet the same way, so all three are written alike rather
    % than two one way and one the other.
    INTEGER : logMinus := 45
    INTEGER : logCr    := 13
    INTEGER : logLf    := 10

    % The full filespec, for the create. CreateFile takes the WHOLE thing in
    % one 64-byte buffer, unlike MON50 which takes name and type separately.
    BYTES   : logFull     := 'CHATLG2:SYMB'
    INTEGER : lenLogFull  := 12
    INTEGER : nameBufSize := 64
    BYTES   : nameBuf(0:63)
    BYTES   : blankByte   := ' '
    INTEGER4 : startAddr  := 0
    INTEGER4 : noOfPages  := 0

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Ask SINTRAN to create CHATLOG:SYMB.
    %
    % MON50 CANNOT CREATE A FILE - measured in CHAT.PLNC, where quoting the
    % name did nothing at all. CreateFile is 221B and it is a separate call.
    % It has no MONn routine, which is why it goes through MONITOR_CALL; that
    % works here because MON-CALL-1B-A00 supplies 5MON_P, and this program
    % already relies on it for TimeOut.
    %
    % The buffer is filled with SPACES first and the filespec copied over the
    % front, so whatever was in the rest is never read as part of the name.
    % Both size parameters are zero: an ordinary indexed file that grows as
    % it is written, which is what a log wants.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : makeLog
        INTEGER : i
        FOR i IN 1:nameBufSize DO
            blankByte(0) =: nameBuf(i - 1)
        ENDFOR
        FOR i IN 1:lenLogFull DO
            logFull(i - 1) =: nameBuf(i - 1)
        ENDFOR
        MONITOR_CALL(221B, nameBuf, startAddr, noOfPages)
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Open the log for append, creating it the first time.
    %
    % Returns the device number, or 0 when the log is not usable. THE CALLER
    % MUST TEST THE BOOLEAN, not the number - a failed MON50 leaves 46 in it,
    % SINTRAN's "no such file", which is the defect CHAT.PLNC records at
    % length. This routine turns that into a plain 0 so no caller can repeat
    % it.
    %
    % THE FLAG IS CLEARED AFTER THE ON BLOCK, NOT BEFORE. Measured in
    % CHAT.PLNC on 2026-08-19: cleared before, the handler body had already
    % run and every open reported failure while returning a good device.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER : logOpen
        INTEGER : dev
        BOOLEAN : openFailed

        IF logBroken THEN
            0 RETURN
        ENDIF

        0 =: dev
        ON ROUTINEERROR DO
            TRUE =: openFailed
        ENDON
        FALSE =: openFailed

        MON50(logName, logType, logAppend) =: dev

        IF openFailed THEN
            % Not there yet. Make it and open it for real. A create that
            % fails needs no message of its own - the second open fails too
            % and that is what sets logBroken.
            ON ROUTINEERROR DO
            ENDON
            makeLog

            ON ROUTINEERROR DO
                TRUE =: openFailed
            ENDON
            FALSE =: openFailed
            MON50(logName, logType, logAppend) =: dev
        ENDIF

        IF openFailed THEN
            TRUE =: logBroken
            0 RETURN
        ENDIF

        dev RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Write one line: a tag, a number, and an end of line.
    %
    % The tag is passed as a slice of a BYTES with its length, the same shape
    % every other routine here uses, because PLANC's OUTPUT needs the width in
    % the format string and a routine cannot have a literal one.
    %
    % NOTHING HERE IS ALLOWED TO KILL THE SERVER. Every failure sets the flag
    % and returns. A logger that can take down the thing it is watching is
    % worse than no logger.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Write one byte to the log. PLAIN 7-BIT ASCII, and the parity version of
    % this routine has been TAKEN OUT.
    %
    % IT USED TO SET EVEN PARITY IN BIT 7, on a conclusion that was WRONG. The
    % reasoning was: the log filled up correctly - 107 bytes, five opens, every
    % size right - and then
    %
    %   @LIST-FILE SVLOG:SYMB,,     printed the header and NOTHING ELSE
    %   @QED  R SVLOG:SYMB          PARITY ERROR AT LINE 1, thirteen times
    %
    % which reads as "the bytes are there and the tools refuse them", so the
    % bytes must be at fault.
    %
    % THE CONTROL RUN THAT WAS NEVER DONE. The same two commands against
    % CHATSV:MODE - the build file @MODE compiles this whole server from, which
    % is unquestionably good - answer EXACTLY THE SAME WAY: LIST-FILE prints the
    % header and nothing else, QED cries PARITY ERROR thirteen times. Both tools
    % do this to every file our FA push puts on the machine. They were never
    % evidence about the log at all.
    %
    % And the parity code did not even do what it claimed: measured on the wire,
    % EVERY byte came back with bit 7 set - S as 0xD3, T as 0xD4, space as 0xA0,
    % CR as 0x8D - so the "is the count odd" test was true every time. It was
    % wrong code justifying itself with a wrong measurement.
    %
    % TO READ THIS LOG, PULL IT OFF THE MACHINE AND LOOK AT THE BYTES:
    %
    %   @COPY-FILE "SVLOG2:SYMB",(RT)CHATLG2:SYMB     (RT owns it; SYSTEM cannot
    %                                                  delete or chmod it)
    %   Xmsg.Live.Runner --config topology-d19999-hdlc-server.json \
    %       --pull 'SVLOG2:SYMB' --pull-from 100 --pull-to svlog2.raw \
    %       --originate-from-seed
    %
    % That works, and it is how the broadcast question was finally answered.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER) : logPut(dev, value)
        BYTE : b

        % Masked to seven bits so a stray high bit in a caller's value cannot
        % put a character in the file that nothing will render.
        value AND 127 =: b
        b MON2(dev)
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (BYTES, INTEGER, INTEGER) : logLine(tag, tagLen, value)
        INTEGER : dev, i, left, rest, digit, digitCount
        BYTE    : b
        BOOLEAN : writeFailed

        logOpen =: dev
        IF dev = 0 THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % ONE FLAG FOR THE WHOLE LINE, not a message per byte. CHAT.PLNC
        % learned that the hard way: a handler round a write loop that prints
        % inside it says the same thing once per character.
        ON ROUTINEERROR DO
            TRUE =: writeFailed
        ENDON
        FALSE =: writeFailed

        FOR i IN 1:tagLen DO
            tag(i - 1) =: b
            logPut(dev, b)
        ENDFOR

        % The number, most significant digit first. Built backwards into
        % logDigits and then written forwards, because dividing gives the
        % LAST digit first and a log line read by a person wants them the
        % other way round.
        %
        % Negative values are printed with a minus and then their magnitude.
        % A status word can be negative on this wire and "-34" is readable
        % where the unsigned form is not.
        value =: left
        IF left < 0 THEN
            logPut(dev, logMinus)
            0 - left =: left
        ENDIF

        % NO "MOD" - it appears nowhere in this file and is not going to be
        % introduced on a guess. putNumber above gets its digits by dividing
        % and subtracting, so this does the same.
        0 =: digitCount
        % A do-while shape, so zero prints as "0" rather than as nothing.
        DO
            left / 10 =: rest
            left - rest * 10 =: digit
            zeroByte(0) + digit =: logDigits(digitCount)
            digitCount + 1 =: digitCount
            rest =: left
            WHILE left > 0
        ENDDO

        FOR i IN 1:digitCount DO
            logDigits(digitCount - i) =: b
            logPut(dev, b)
        ENDFOR

        % THE CARRIAGE RETURN MATTERS - a SINTRAN text file is expected to
        % have one, and without it every line runs into the next. It carries
        % parity like every other byte; QED counts it as part of the line.
        logPut(dev, logCr)
        logPut(dev, logLf)

        % CLOSE, ALWAYS, including after a failed write - see the note on the
        % open. An unclosed file keeps its old contents.
        MON43(dev)

        IF writeFailed THEN
            TRUE =: logBroken
        ENDIF
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Compare a name in the room against one in a buffer, ignoring case.
    %
    % ChatRoom.cs compares ignoring case, so this does too - the two doors into
    % one room must not disagree about whether ANNA and anna are the same
    % person.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : sameName(slot, at, length)
        INTEGER : j, a, b
        IF memberLen(slot) >< length THEN
            0 RETURN
        ENDIF
        FOR j IN 1:length DO
            memberName(slot, j) =: a
            inBuf(at + j - 1) =: b
            IF a > 96 THEN a - 32 =: a ENDIF        % fold lower case to upper
            IF b > 96 THEN b - 32 =: b ENDIF
            IF a >< b THEN
                0 RETURN
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        1 RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER4) : findByMagic(magic)
        INTEGER : i
        FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
            IF memberUsed(i) >< 0 THEN
                IF memberMagic(i) = magic THEN
                    i RETURN
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        0 RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : findByName(at, length)
        INTEGER : i
        FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
            IF memberUsed(i) >< 0 THEN
                IF sameName(i, at, length) = 1 THEN
                    i RETURN
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        0 RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER : freeSlot
        INTEGER : i
        FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
            IF memberUsed(i) = 0 THEN
                i RETURN
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        0 RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Build a message into outBuf. Returns its total length.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            buildMessage(kind, nameAt, nameLength, textAt, textLength)
        INTEGER : i, at
        kind =: outBuf(0)
        nameLength =: outBuf(1)
        2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:nameLength DO
            inBuf(nameAt + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        0 =: outBuf(at)                       % text length, high byte
        textLength =: outBuf(at + 1)          % text length, low byte
        at + 2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
            inBuf(textAt + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Print a name that arrived in inBuf, one byte at a time.
    %
    % Byte by byte because 'ALn' formats a STRING: handing it a single byte
    % prints the byte's NUMBER, not the character. A one-element BYTES is the
    % way across, the same trick the space separator in buildWho uses.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Write a small number into outBuf as decimal digits, advancing at.
    %
    % By hand because there is no formatter that writes into a BYTES array -
    % OUTPUT goes to a device, not a buffer. Handles 0..999, which covers a
    % seat count and every counter this status line is likely to grow.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : putNumber(value, at)
        INTEGER : left, digit, p
        BOOLEAN : started

        % RETURNS THE NEW CURSOR rather than writing through an INTEGER WRITE
        % parameter. The WRITE form was assumed to advance the CALLER's variable
        % and did not: the status line came out "SEATS 016" instead of
        % "SEATS 0/16" - the separator was written where the previous digit had
        % gone, and the length came out short. MEASURED on D100 2026-08-20.
        %
        % Returning the cursor is the idiom buildMessage and buildWho already
        % use in this file ("at RETURN"), and it cannot be got wrong by the
        % caller in the same silent way.
        at =: p
        value =: left
        FALSE =: started

        % Hundreds and tens are skipped until a non-zero one appears, so 7
        % prints as "7" and not "007". Zero itself still has to print, which
        % the final digit below always does.
        left / 100 =: digit
        IF digit > 0 THEN
            zeroByte(0) + digit =: outBuf(p)
            p + 1 =: p
            TRUE =: started
            left - digit * 100 =: left
        ENDIF

        left / 10 =: digit
        IF digit > 0 OR started THEN
            zeroByte(0) + digit =: outBuf(p)
            p + 1 =: p
            left - digit * 10 =: left
        ENDIF

        zeroByte(0) + left =: outBuf(p)
        p + 1 =: p
        p RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Write a system number into peerName and return the new cursor.
    %
    % A SEPARATE ROUTINE FROM putNumber, and not a tidiness failure: putNumber
    % writes into outBuf, which is the message being built. A system name is not
    % part of any message - it goes to XMPBLET - so it needs its own buffer, and
    % a routine that took the buffer as a parameter would be handing a BYTES
    % formal parameter around, which this compiler is fussy about in exactly the
    % area that already cost three cycles here.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : putPeerDigits(value, at)
        INTEGER : left, digit, p
        BOOLEAN : started

        at =: p
        value =: left
        FALSE =: started

        left / 10000 =: digit
        IF digit > 0 THEN
            zeroByte(0) + digit =: peerName(p)
            p + 1 =: p
            TRUE =: started
            left - digit * 10000 =: left
        ENDIF

        left / 1000 =: digit
        IF digit > 0 OR started THEN
            zeroByte(0) + digit =: peerName(p)
            p + 1 =: p
            TRUE =: started
            left - digit * 1000 =: left
        ENDIF

        left / 100 =: digit
        IF digit > 0 OR started THEN
            zeroByte(0) + digit =: peerName(p)
            p + 1 =: p
            TRUE =: started
            left - digit * 100 =: left
        ENDIF

        left / 10 =: digit
        IF digit > 0 OR started THEN
            zeroByte(0) + digit =: peerName(p)
            p + 1 =: p
            left - digit * 10 =: left
        ENDIF

        zeroByte(0) + left =: peerName(p)
        p + 1 =: p
        p RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Write a fixed word into outBuf and return the new cursor.
    %
    % Same "at RETURN" idiom as putNumber, and for the same reason: a WRITE
    % parameter looks like it advances the caller's cursor and does not.
    %
    % SUBSCRIPT FROM ZERO - a BYTES from a literal starts at index 0. See
    % buildAdmText.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES, INTEGER, INTEGER) : putWord(text, textLen, at)
        INTEGER : i, p
        at =: p
        FOR i IN 1:textLen DO
            text(i - 1) =: outBuf(p)
            p + 1 =: p
        ENDFOR
        p RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER) : showName(nameAt, nameLength)
        INTEGER : i
        FOR i IN 1:nameLength DO
            inBuf(nameAt + i - 1) =: oneByte
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneByte)
        ENDFOR
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Build the answer to an AdminStatus: how the room stands, as readable
    % text.
    %
    % TEXT, NOT A STRUCT OF COUNTERS, and that is a decision rather than
    % laziness. What is worth reporting grows with every phase of federation -
    % peers, trunk states, hop counts - and a text answer lets it grow without
    % changing the wire and re-pinning the golden bytes each time.
    %
    % The name field is EMPTY: the server is answering, not a member.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER : buildStatus
        INTEGER : i, j, at, seated, textAt, textLen
        % kbTotal counts BLOCKS while it is being summed and is turned into KB
        % once, at the end. held is how many messages a room actually has.
        INTEGER : kbTotal, held

        0 =: seated
        FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
            IF memberUsed(i) = 1 THEN
                seated + 1 =: seated
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        kAdmRepl =: outBuf(0)
        0 =: outBuf(1)                        % no name: the SERVER is answering
        2 =: at
        0 =: outBuf(at)                       % text length, high byte
        0 =: outBuf(at + 1)                   % text length, low byte, filled below
        at + 2 =: textAt
        textAt =: at

        % "SEATS n/m" - taken and offered. Two numbers, because "3 seats used"
        % does not say whether the room is full, and full is the case an
        % operator is asking about.
        FOR j IN 0:5 DO
            statusWord(j) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        putNumber(seated, at) =: at
        slashByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
        at + 1 =: at
        putNumber(maxSeats, at) =: at

        % ---- AND WHAT THE HISTORY IS DOING ---------------------------------
        %
        % Ronny asked for three things here, 2026-08-23: what each room keeps
        % and holds, what a NEW room would get, and how much disk it all comes
        % to. He chose reporting over a total that refuses - eight rooms at the
        % 200 ceiling is 800KB, which is nothing on this disk, and a second
        % limit is a second thing that can turn down a reasonable request.
        %
        %   SEATS 2/16  default 50  disk 31KB  LOBBY 50/12 GENERAL 20/3
        %
        % BLOCKS ARE COUNTED, NOT FILE SIZES. A ring's file is its full size
        % from the day it is made, so asking the file system would report what
        % was RESERVED. What an operator wants to know is what it will cost,
        % and that is size x 512 whether the ring is full yet or not.
        %
        % HELD is "how many are actually in there" - histNext when the ring has
        % not wrapped, the size once it has. Two numbers because 50/12 says a
        % room is a quarter full, where 12 alone does not say what of.
        putWord('  default ', 10, at) =: at
        putNumber(histDefault, at) =: at

        0 =: kbTotal
        FOR i IN 1:maxHistRooms DO
            IF topicRoomLen(i) > 0 THEN
                kbTotal + histSize(i) =: kbTotal
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        putWord('  disk ', 7, at) =: at
        putNumber(kbTotal / 2, at) =: at        % blocks of 512 bytes -> KB
        putWord('KB', 2, at) =: at

        % ---- THE THREE COUNTS THAT USED TO BE A FLOOD ----------------------
        % empty  routed letters with no payload. One per seat returned, because
        %        XROUT's answer comes back on our own port looking like that.
        %        Climbing while the room is quiet is the seat feedback loop.
        % bounce messages that came home with nobody there - dead clients.
        % refuse joins turned away, whatever the reason.
        % relay  lines passed ON to a further peer. A machine in the MIDDLE of
        %        a chain shows this climbing; an END machine shows it flat at
        %        zero, so a non-zero count on an end machine means the topology
        %        is not what somebody believes it is.
        %
        % Before this they printed a line each, every time, and the terminal
        % filled with the two that are expected. A number you can ask for beats
        % a line you cannot avoid.
        putWord('  empty ', 8, at) =: at
        putNumber(nEmptyLet, at) =: at
        putWord(' bounce ', 8, at) =: at
        putNumber(nBounced, at) =: at
        putWord(' relay ', 7, at) =: at
        putNumber(nRelayed, at) =: at
        putWord(' dupe ', 6, at) =: at
        putNumber(nDupes, at) =: at
        putWord(' refuse ', 8, at) =: at
        putNumber(nRefused, at) =: at

        FOR i IN 1:maxHistRooms DO
            IF topicRoomLen(i) > 0 THEN
                % Bounded like every other builder here: a name plus two
                % numbers is at most 25 bytes, and outBuf's text field is 250.
                IF at - textAt < 215 THEN
                    spaceByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                    at + 1 =: at
                    FOR j IN 1:topicRoomLen(i) DO
                        topicRoom(i, j) =: outBuf(at)
                        at + 1 =: at
                    ENDFOR
                    spaceByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                    at + 1 =: at
                    putNumber(histSize(i), at) =: at
                    slashByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                    at + 1 =: at

                    histNext(i) =: held
                    IF held > histSize(i) THEN
                        histSize(i) =: held     % wrapped: it holds a full ring
                    ENDIF
                    putNumber(held, at) =: at
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        at - textAt =: textLen
        textLen =: outBuf(3)                  % low byte; it cannot reach 256
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Build a kAdmRepl carrying one fixed sentence.
    %
    % SUBSCRIPT FROM ZERO. A BYTES declared from a literal has its first
    % character at index 0, so "text(i)" for i in 1:len drops the first letter
    % and reads one byte past the end. That defect turned RONNY into ONNY on the
    % wire once already; see the note in refuse().
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES, INTEGER) : buildAdmText(text, textLen)
        INTEGER : i, at
        kAdmRepl =: outBuf(0)
        0 =: outBuf(1)                        % no name: the SERVER is answering
        2 =: at
        0 =: outBuf(at)
        textLen =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:textLen DO
            text(i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Read a decimal system number out of an arrived message. 0 means "that was
    % not a number", which the caller reports rather than acting on.
    %
    % NO SIGN AND NO SPACES. An operator types "TRUNK 102"; anything else is a
    % mistake worth refusing out loud rather than interpreting generously.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : trunkNumber(at, length)
        INTEGER : i, d, value
        IF length < 1 THEN
            0 RETURN
        ENDIF
        0 =: value
        FOR i IN 1:length DO
            inBuf(at + i - 1) =: d
            IF d < 48 THEN
                0 RETURN
            ENDIF
            IF d > 57 THEN
                0 RETURN
            ENDIF
            (value * 10) + (d - 48) =: value
        ENDFOR
        value RETURN
    ENDROUTINE


    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % LIST-TRUNKS: every configured peer and what we believe about it.
    %
    % "102 up" / "103 down" / "200 unknown". UNKNOWN and DOWN are kept apart
    % deliberately - the first usually means the trunk was never answered and is
    % probably a configuration mistake, the second means a machine that was
    % there and went away. Collapsing them would hide the difference exactly
    % when an operator needs it.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER : buildTrunks
        INTEGER : i, at, textAt, textLen, st

        kAdmRepl =: outBuf(0)
        0 =: outBuf(1)
        2 =: at
        0 =: outBuf(at)
        0 =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: textAt
        textAt =: at

        FOR i IN 1:maxPeers DO
            IF peerSystem(i) >< 0 THEN
                IF at - textAt > 0 THEN
                    spaceByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                    at + 1 =: at
                ENDIF
                putNumber(peerSystem(i), at) =: at
                spaceByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                at + 1 =: at
                peerState(i) =: st
                IF st = peerUp THEN
                    putWord('up', 2, at) =: at
                ENDIF
                IF st = peerDown THEN
                    putWord('down', 4, at) =: at
                ENDIF
                IF st = peerUnknown THEN
                    putWord('unknown', 7, at) =: at
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        IF at = textAt THEN
            putWord('no trunks', 9, at) =: at
        ENDIF

        at - textAt =: textLen
        textLen =: outBuf(3)
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % /map - the same picture, for somebody who is not an operator.
    %
    %     this is D100 - 102 up  103 down
    %     this is D100 - on its own
    %
    % Ronny, 2026-08-20: end users should be able to see how the network is
    % configured. The registry has held kind 12 for it ever since.
    %
    % WHY IT IS NOT JUST LIST-TRUNKS WITH A DIFFERENT NAME, and this is the
    % whole reason it is a ROOM kind rather than an admin one:
    %
    %   SEEING how the network is wired is not the same right as REWIRING it.
    %   LIST-TRUNKS arrives on the ADMIN port, from CHAT-MON, alongside
    %   START-TRUNK and STOP-TRUNK - the verbs that change things. /map arrives
    %   on the room port from an ordinary member and can change nothing.
    %
    % IT NAMES THIS MACHINE FIRST. A trunk list on its own is ambiguous the
    % moment there is more than one machine: "102 up" read on D102 would be
    % nonsense, and a user who has just been told they are "ANNA@D102" needs to
    % know which end they are looking from. mySystem comes from xmpfm2p on our
    % own magic at start-up, so it is what the KERNEL says we are, not what a
    % configuration file claims.
    %
    % Peers are two spaces apart and the number and state one, the same shape
    % the all-rooms /who uses, so the groups read apart at a glance.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER : buildMap
        INTEGER : i, at, textAt, textLen, st, headAt

        kMap =: outBuf(0)
        0 =: outBuf(1)                        % no name: the SERVER is answering
        2 =: at
        0 =: outBuf(at)
        0 =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: textAt
        textAt =: at

        putWord('this is D', 9, at) =: at
        putNumber(mySystem, at) =: at
        putWord(' - ', 3, at) =: at

        % REMEMBER WHERE THE HEADER ENDED, do not compute it.
        %
        % This was first written as "IF at - textAt > 15", 15 being the length
        % of "this is D100 - ". That is only true for a THREE-DIGIT system
        % number: on D19999 the header is 17 and the test would put two spaces
        % before the first peer and then still claim there were none. A length
        % that depends on data is not a constant, however constant it looks in
        % the machine you are sitting at.
        at =: headAt

        FOR i IN 1:maxPeers DO
            IF peerSystem(i) >< 0 THEN
                IF at > headAt THEN
                    spaceByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                    at + 1 =: at
                    spaceByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                    at + 1 =: at
                ENDIF
                putNumber(peerSystem(i), at) =: at
                spaceByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                at + 1 =: at
                peerState(i) =: st
                IF st = peerUp THEN
                    putWord('up', 2, at) =: at
                ENDIF
                IF st = peerDown THEN
                    putWord('down', 4, at) =: at
                ENDIF
                IF st = peerUnknown THEN
                    putWord('unknown', 7, at) =: at
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        % NO TRUNKS IS A REAL ANSWER AND IS SAID IN WORDS. "on its own" is what
        % a person wants to read; an empty list after a dash is what a program
        % would print. Same reason /topic spells out an absent topic.
        IF at = headAt THEN
            putWord('on its own', 10, at) =: at
        ENDIF

        at - textAt =: textLen
        textLen =: outBuf(3)
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Forget everything a peer told us. Called when it goes Down, and before
    % storing a fresh list from it.
    %
    % WHOLESALE, NEVER MERGED. A peer that comes back has almost certainly
    % restarted, so anything we remember about its members is stale - the design
    % says treat Up as "start again". Merging would leave ghosts of people who
    % left while the machine was away, and nothing would ever remove them.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER) : forgetRemote(system)
        INTEGER : i
        FOR i IN 1:maxRemote DO
            IF rmtSystem(i) = system THEN
                0 =: rmtSystem(i)
                0 =: rmtNameLen(i)
                0 =: rmtRoomLen(i)
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Build the answer to a peer's "who is on you": our own seated members as
    % "NAME/ROOM NAME/ROOM ...".
    %
    % OUR OWN MEMBERS ONLY. What we know about OTHER machines is theirs to
    % report, not ours to relay - passing it on would let a wrong entry echo
    % around a ring of machines with nothing to stop it. Every machine speaks
    % only for itself; that is what makes the state converge.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER : buildMembers
        INTEGER : i, j, at, textAt, textLen, need

        kTrkMbrs =: outBuf(0)
        0 =: outBuf(1)                        % no name: a MACHINE is answering
        2 =: at
        0 =: outBuf(at)
        0 =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: textAt
        textAt =: at
        0 =: textLen

        FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
            IF memberUsed(i) = 1 THEN
                % name + '/' + room, and a space if anything is there already
                memberLen(i) + mbrRoomLen(i) + 1 =: need
                IF textLen > 0 THEN
                    need + 1 =: need
                ENDIF

                IF textLen + need <= 250 THEN
                    IF textLen > 0 THEN
                        spaceByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                        at + 1 =: at
                        textLen + 1 =: textLen
                    ENDIF
                    FOR j IN 1:memberLen(i) DO
                        memberName(i, j) =: outBuf(at)
                        at + 1 =: at
                    ENDFOR
                    slashByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                    at + 1 =: at
                    FOR j IN 1:mbrRoomLen(i) DO
                        mbrRoom(i, j) =: outBuf(at)
                        at + 1 =: at
                    ENDFOR
                    textLen + need =: textLen
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        textLen =: outBuf(3)
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Store what a peer just told us. Replaces everything we held for it.
    %
    % Parses "NAME/ROOM NAME/ROOM ..." out of inBuf. A malformed entry - no
    % slash, or a name longer than a name can be - is DROPPED and the parse
    % carries on. One bad entry from a peer must not cost us the rest of its
    % list, and must certainly not write past the end of a table.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % NO LABEL AND NO GO. A "skip this entry" jump would read more naturally,
    % but PLANC's label form is not something this file has ever used and a
    % guess costs a ten-minute build cycle. A BOOLEAN says the same thing and
    % cannot be got wrong.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            takeMembers(system, at, length)
        INTEGER : p, stop, nameAt, nameLen2, roomAt, roomLen2, row, i
        BOOLEAN : good

        forgetRemote(system)

        at =: p
        at + length =: stop

        DO
            WHILE p < stop

            % skip separators
            DO
                WHILE p < stop AND inBuf(p) = spaceByte(0)
                p + 1 =: p
            ENDDO

            % the name, up to a slash or a space
            p =: nameAt
            DO
                WHILE p < stop AND inBuf(p) >< slashByte(0) AND &
                      inBuf(p) >< spaceByte(0)
                p + 1 =: p
            ENDDO
            p - nameAt =: nameLen2

            % A SLASH MUST FOLLOW. Anything else is a malformed entry - the room
            % is left empty, the entry fails the test below and is dropped, and
            % the loop carries on with the next one. One bad entry from a peer
            % must not cost us the rest of its list.
            FALSE =: good
            0 =: roomLen2
            IF p < stop THEN
                IF inBuf(p) = slashByte(0) THEN
                    p + 1 =: p
                    p =: roomAt
                    DO
                        WHILE p < stop AND inBuf(p) >< spaceByte(0)
                        p + 1 =: p
                    ENDDO
                    p - roomAt =: roomLen2
                    TRUE =: good
                ENDIF
            ENDIF

            IF good THEN
                IF nameLen2 > 0 AND nameLen2 <= maxNameLen AND &
                   roomLen2 > 0 AND roomLen2 <= maxNameLen THEN
                    0 =: row
                    FOR i IN 1:maxRemote DO
                        IF rmtSystem(i) = 0 THEN
                            IF row = 0 THEN
                                i =: row
                            ENDIF
                        ENDIF
                    ENDFOR
                    IF row >< 0 THEN
                        system =: rmtSystem(row)
                        FOR i IN 1:nameLen2 DO
                            inBuf(nameAt + i - 1) =: rmtName(row, i)
                        ENDFOR
                        nameLen2 =: rmtNameLen(row)
                        FOR i IN 1:roomLen2 DO
                            inBuf(roomAt + i - 1) =: rmtRoom(row, i)
                        ENDFOR
                        roomLen2 =: rmtRoomLen(row)
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDDO
    ENDROUTINE

    % Is this member in the same room as that one?
    %
    % Compared byte by byte on the stored name. A room is only "the same room"
    % if it is spelled the same - there is no room id, because a room is not a
    % registered thing any more, just a name people agree on.
    %
    % DECLARED HERE, ABOVE buildWho, AND THAT POSITION IS LOAD-BEARING. It used
    % to sit next to broadcast, 250 lines further down; PLANC needs a routine
    % declared before it is called, so buildWho could not have used it even if
    % somebody had thought to. Moved up 2026-08-22 when /who was fixed.
    ROUTINE VOID, BOOLEAN (INTEGER, INTEGER) : sameRoom(slotA, slotB)
        INTEGER : i
        IF mbrRoomLen(slotA) >< mbrRoomLen(slotB) THEN
            FALSE RETURN
        ENDIF
        FOR i IN 1:mbrRoomLen(slotA) DO
            IF mbrRoom(slotA, i) >< mbrRoom(slotB, i) THEN
                FALSE RETURN
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        TRUE RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Which topic entry belongs to this member's room? 0 if the room has never
    % had a topic set.
    %
    % Compared the same way sameRoom compares: byte for byte on the spelling.
    % A room IS its name here, so LOBBY and lobby are two rooms, and the topic
    % table has to agree with the broadcast about that or a topic would show up
    % in a room that cannot hear the messages.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER) : findTopic(slot)
        INTEGER : t, i
        BOOLEAN : match

        FOR t IN 1:maxTopics DO
            IF topicRoomLen(t) = mbrRoomLen(slot) THEN
                TRUE =: match
                FOR i IN 1:topicRoomLen(t) DO
                    IF topicRoom(t, i) >< mbrRoom(slot, i) THEN
                        FALSE =: match
                    ENDIF
                ENDFOR
                IF match THEN
                    t RETURN
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        0 RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Claim a topic entry for this member's room, or 0 if the table is full.
    %
    % An unused entry is one with topicRoomLen 0 - a room name is never empty,
    % so zero cannot collide with a real one.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER) : claimTopic(slot)
        INTEGER : t, i

        findTopic(slot) =: t
        IF t >< 0 THEN
            t RETURN                          % already has one; reuse it
        ENDIF

        FOR t IN 1:maxTopics DO
            IF topicRoomLen(t) = 0 THEN
                FOR i IN 1:mbrRoomLen(slot) DO
                    mbrRoom(slot, i) =: topicRoom(t, i)
                ENDFOR
                mbrRoomLen(slot) =: topicRoomLen(t)
                0 =: topicTextLen(t)

                % TAKE A COPY OF THE DEFAULT AS IT IS NOW. This is what makes
                % "INITIALIZE 20" apply to rooms created LATER without reaching
                % back into rooms that already have messages - the copy is
                % taken once, here, at the moment the room comes into being.
                histDefault =: histSize(t)
                0 =: histNext(t)
                t RETURN
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        0 RETURN                              % full - the caller must say so
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Find a room entry BY NAME, out of the incoming buffer, or 0.
    %
    % A SEPARATE ROUTINE FROM findTopic, which looks a room up by the SEAT of
    % somebody in it. This one is for an operator naming a room they are not in
    % - and may not even exist yet. One routine taking "which kind of thing is
    % this" would be a flag deciding what the code means, which is the same
    % mistake sameRoom and sameRoomAs were deliberately kept apart to avoid.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : findRoomNamed(at, len)
        INTEGER : t, i
        BOOLEAN : match

        IF len < 1 THEN
            0 RETURN
        ENDIF
        FOR t IN 1:maxTopics DO
            IF topicRoomLen(t) = len THEN
                TRUE =: match
                FOR i IN 1:len DO
                    IF topicRoom(t, i) >< inBuf(at + i - 1) THEN
                        FALSE =: match
                    ENDIF
                ENDFOR
                IF match THEN
                    t RETURN
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        0 RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Claim a room entry for a NAMED room, or 0 if the table is full.
    %
    % Lets a room be configured BEFORE anybody is in it, which is the useful
    % case: an operator sets up the room's history and the first person to
    % arrive gets the size that was intended rather than the default.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : claimRoomNamed(at, len)
        INTEGER : t, i

        findRoomNamed(at, len) =: t
        IF t >< 0 THEN
            t RETURN
        ENDIF
        IF len < 1 THEN
            0 RETURN
        ENDIF
        IF len > 16 THEN
            0 RETURN                          % a room name cannot be longer
        ENDIF

        FOR t IN 1:maxTopics DO
            IF topicRoomLen(t) = 0 THEN
                FOR i IN 1:len DO
                    inBuf(at + i - 1) =: topicRoom(t, i)
                ENDFOR
                len =: topicRoomLen(t)
                0 =: topicTextLen(t)

                % Same copy as claimTopic, for the same reason - see the note
                % there. A room configured by name before anybody is in it is
                % still a room being CREATED.
                histDefault =: histSize(t)
                0 =: histNext(t)
                t RETURN
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        0 RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Open this room's history file, creating it the first time.
    %
    % Returns the device number, or 0 if it could not be had. A history that
    % cannot be opened is NOT a reason to refuse the room: the chat carries on
    % without it, and that is deliberate - losing the record of a conversation
    % is bad, losing the conversation is worse.
    %
    % OPENED ONCE AND LEFT OPEN. A room is written on every line anybody types,
    % and an open plus a close in that path would put two file operations
    % between a keystroke and the room hearing it.
    %
    % TRY THE PLAIN OPEN FIRST, CREATE ONLY IF IT FAILS. A create at a name
    % that already exists is refused (SINTRAN error 62), so create-first fails
    % on every run after the first - which is every run that matters.
    %
    % ACCESS CODE 2 IS RANDOM READ/WRITE, and it has to be: MN117 and MN120
    % require it, and the whole ring depends on addressing a block directly.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Ask SINTRAN to create this room's history file.
    %
    % The same shape as makeLog, and for the same reason: MON50 cannot create,
    % CreateFile (221B) is a separate call, and it has no MONn routine - which
    % is why it goes through MONITOR_CALL. That works here because
    % MON-CALL-1B-A00 supplies 5MON_P and this program already needs it for
    % TimeOut.
    %
    % The name carried here is the FULL specification WITH the type -
    % "(SYSTEM)CHATH1:DATA" - where MON50 takes the name and the type as two
    % separate strings. Two calls, two conventions.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER) : makeHistFile(t)
        INTEGER : i, at

        FOR i IN 1:nameBufSize DO
            blankByte(0) =: nameBuf(i - 1)
        ENDFOR

        0 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:lenHistUser DO
            histUser(i - 1) =: nameBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        FOR i IN 1:5 DO
            histStem(i - 1) =: nameBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        digitZero + t =: nameBuf(at)
        at + 1 =: at
        colonByte(0) =: nameBuf(at)
        at + 1 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:4 DO
            histType(i - 1) =: nameBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR

        % One page per block, and the ring addresses histSize of them.
        histSize(t) =: histPages
        MONITOR_CALL(221B, nameBuf, startAddr, histPages)
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER) : histOpen(t)
        INTEGER : dev, i
        BOOLEAN : failed

        IF t < 1 THEN
            0 RETURN
        ENDIF
        IF histDev(t) >< 0 THEN
            histDev(t) RETURN                 % already open
        ENDIF

        % "(SYSTEM)CHATH" + the table index as one digit. The index is 1..8 so
        % a single digit always fits, and digitZero + t is the character for it.
        %
        % BUILT WITH A LOOP, not eight assignments. The first version of this
        % copied the five stem bytes out one at a time, which was five chances
        % to type the wrong subscript and would have needed rewriting the moment
        % the name changed - which it then did, on the very next build.
        FOR i IN 1:lenHistUser DO
            histUser(i - 1) =: histName(i - 1)
        ENDFOR
        FOR i IN 1:5 DO
            histStem(i - 1) =: histName(lenHistUser + i - 1)
        ENDFOR
        digitZero + t =: histName(lenHistUser + 5)

        % A FLAG, NOT THE DEVICE NUMBER. This file's own header says it in as
        % many words - "leaves 46 in the device number when the file is not
        % there - NOT zero. Never test the number".
        %
        % AND THE FLAG IS CLEARED AFTER THE ON BLOCK, NOT BEFORE. logOpen right
        % above records why, measured in CHAT.PLNC on 2026-08-19: "cleared
        % before, the handler body had already run and every open reported
        % failure while returning a good device". The first version of this
        % routine cleared it before - so a PERFECTLY GOOD open was reported as
        % a failure, the create branch ran, and the final "IF failed THEN
        % 0 =: dev" then threw away the device it had just been given.
        0 =: dev
        ON ROUTINEERROR DO
            TRUE =: failed
        ENDON
        FALSE =: failed

        MON50(histName(0:lenHistUser + 5), histType, 2) =: dev

        IF failed THEN
            % ---- MON50 CANNOT CREATE A FILE --------------------------------
            %
            % Quoting the name does NOTHING - measured in CHAT.PLNC and written
            % down beside makeLog above: "CreateFile is 221B and it is a
            % separate call". The first version of this routine quoted the name
            % and believed it, so no history file was ever created and the only
            % one that ever worked was the one made BY HAND from a terminal.
            %
            % Same shape as makeLog: fill the buffer with SPACES first so
            % whatever was in the rest is never read as part of the name, then
            % copy the full specification INCLUDING the type over the front.
            %
            % PAGES = THE RING'S SIZE, so every block the ring will ever
            % address exists from the start. A file that grows as it is written
            % would work for an append, but this writes block (id MOD size)
            % straight away and the last block is touched long before the ones
            % before it.
            makeHistFile(t)

            ON ROUTINEERROR DO
                TRUE =: failed
            ENDON
            FALSE =: failed
            MON50(histName(0:lenHistUser + 5), histType, 2) =: dev
        ENDIF

        % STILL FAILED MEANS NO HISTORY, AND THAT MUST LEAVE ZERO BEHIND -
        % not 46, not whatever MON50 happened to write. Everything downstream
        % tests histDev against 0 to decide whether there is a file at all.
        IF failed THEN
            0 =: dev
        ENDIF

        dev =: histDev(t)
        dev RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Write one message into the ring.
    %
    % ONE BYTE PER WORD, AND NO PACKING. A block is 256 words and the biggest
    % message is a 16-byte name plus 200 bytes of text - 219 words with three
    % words of header, which fits with room to spare. Packing two bytes to a
    % word would save space in a block that is already mostly empty, and would
    % buy a shift-and-mask on every byte in both directions for it. The block
    % is the unit the machine gives us; there is nothing to gain by filling it.
    %
    %   word 0   the id
    %   word 1   how many bytes of name
    %   word 2   how many bytes of text
    %   then     the name, then the text
    %
    % The id goes in the block as well as in the table because the table does
    % not survive a restart and the file does. Reading a block back and finding
    % an id that is not the one the ring expects is how a half-written or
    % never-written block is told from a real one.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            histSave(t, slot, textAt, textLength)
        INTEGER : dev, i, at, id, blk, nLen, tLen

        IF t < 1 THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        % ---- SAY WHAT HAPPENED, INTO THE LOG --------------------------------
        %
        % Three builds have now gone to the machine on a THEORY about why no
        % history file appears - the filename, then the user, then the create
        % call - and each time the only evidence was "OPENED 0 TIMES", which
        % says nothing about WHICH step failed or whether this code ran at all.
        %
        % One log line ends that, the same way printing the LDN and the status
        % ended the no-wait hunt in the client. It costs one line per message
        % and comes out the moment the answer is known.
        histOpen(t) =: dev
        logLine('SV hist slot      ', 18, t)
        logLine('SV hist dev       ', 18, dev)

        IF dev = 0 THEN
            RETURN                            % no history; the room still works
        ENDIF

        memberLen(slot) =: nLen
        textLength =: tLen

        % BOUNDED, because PLANC checks no array bound and a long name plus a
        % long text written past the end of histBlock would corrupt whatever
        % the loader put after it.
        IF nLen > 16 THEN
            16 =: nLen
        ENDIF
        IF 3 + nLen + tLen > histWords THEN
            histWords - 3 - nLen =: tLen
        ENDIF

        histNext(t) =: id
        id =: histBlock(0)
        nLen =: histBlock(1)
        tLen =: histBlock(2)
        3 =: at

        FOR i IN 1:nLen DO
            memberName(slot, i) =: histBlock(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        FOR i IN 1:tLen DO
            inBuf(textAt + i - 1) =: histBlock(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR

        % THE RING, and it is this one line. No compaction, no rewrite, no
        % check for "is it full" - block id MOD size is where this message
        % lives and the one it displaces is the one N messages ago.
        id - ((id / histSize(t)) * histSize(t)) =: blk

        ON ROUTINEERROR DO
            0 =: dev                          % a failed write must not unwind
        ENDON
        MN120(dev, 0, histBlock, blk, histWords)

        id + 1 =: histNext(t)
    ENDROUTINE

    %
    % A separate routine from sameRoom because the two read from DIFFERENT
    % TABLES. One routine taking "which table" would be a flag deciding what the
    % code means - it reads fine and goes wrong in silence the day a caller
    % passes the wrong one.
    %
    % DECLARED HERE, ABOVE buildWho, because PLANC needs it declared before it
    % is called - the same reason sameRoom had to move up when /who was fixed.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, BOOLEAN (INTEGER, INTEGER) : sameRoomAs(slot, row)
        INTEGER : i
        IF mbrRoomLen(slot) >< rmtRoomLen(row) THEN
            FALSE RETURN
        ENDIF
        FOR i IN 1:mbrRoomLen(slot) DO
            IF mbrRoom(slot, i) >< rmtRoom(row, i) THEN
                FALSE RETURN
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        TRUE RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Build the answer to /who: every seated member's name, one space between.
    %
    % The name field is EMPTY - the room is answering, not a member - and the
    % names all go in the text. One message, not one per member: a message each
    % would multiply the scarcest thing XMSG has by the size of the room. The
    % measurement is in DOC/BRINGUP-ORDER-AND-TRAPS-2026-08-18.md: "Data
    % transmit blocks" has a limit of TEN and a 20-user burst hits it.
    %
    % BOUNDED, AND THE BOUND IS NOT DECORATION. outBuf holds 256 bytes and the
    % header eats four, so 252 are left; sixteen seats of sixteen characters
    % plus separators come to 271. A full room of long names would therefore
    % write past the end of outBuf, and PLANC checks no array bound - it would
    % simply corrupt whatever the loader put next. Names that do not fit are
    % dropped, which is a worse answer than a longer buffer would give and a
    % far better one than a corrupted program.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER) : buildWho(askerSlot)
        INTEGER : i, j, at, textAt, textLen, room

        kWho =: outBuf(0)
        0 =: outBuf(1)                        % no name: the ROOM is answering
        2 =: at
        0 =: outBuf(at)                       % text length, high byte
        0 =: outBuf(at + 1)                   % text length, low byte, filled in below
        at + 2 =: textAt
        textAt =: at
        0 =: textLen

        FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
            % THE ASKER'S ROOM ONLY - and this test was MISSING until
            % 2026-08-22, which made /who the one thing on this server that
            % crossed a room boundary. Measured on D100: TESTER alone in LOBBY,
            % ANNA in GENERAL, and TESTER's /who answered
            %     CHAT: in (LOBBY): TESTER ANNA
            % The client's "in (LOBBY)" label made the answer not merely too
            % long but FALSE - it named a room ANNA was not in. Messages were
            % isolated the whole time; only the roster leaked, which is why
            % this survived a two-room test that watched what people SAID.
            %
            % sameRoom is the same compare the broadcast uses. Using it here
            % rather than a fresh loop is the point: the roster and the
            % broadcast must never disagree about who is in the room.
            IF memberUsed(i) = 1 THEN
              IF sameRoom(i, askerSlot) THEN
                % What this name needs: a separator if it is not the first,
                % then the name itself.
                memberLen(i) =: room
                IF textLen > 0 THEN
                    room + 1 =: room
                ENDIF

                IF textLen + room <= 250 THEN
                    IF textLen > 0 THEN
                        % A BYTE FROM A BYTES ARRAY, not a string literal.
                        % "' ' =: outBuf(at)" is what stood here and the
                        % compiler answered
                        %   1082 (422)/BUILDWHO *** ERROR
                        %        - ILLEGAL DATA TYPE "OUTBUF"
                        % because 'x' is a STRING and outBuf(at) is a BYTE.
                        % Copying out of a one-byte BYTES gives the element the
                        % type it wants - the same move oneChar uses on the
                        % client side for printing.
                        spaceByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                        at + 1 =: at
                        textLen + 1 =: textLen
                    ENDIF
                    FOR j IN 1:memberLen(i) DO
                        memberName(i, j) =: outBuf(at)
                        at + 1 =: at
                    ENDFOR
                    textLen + memberLen(i) =: textLen
                ENDIF
              ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        % ---- AND THE PEOPLE ON THE OTHER MACHINES --------------------------
        %
        % Phase C. Same room, but on a peer, shown QUALIFIED - "ANNA@D102" -
        % because two machines can each have an ANNA and a bare name would make
        % them look like one person. The qualification is what makes a federated
        % room honest rather than merely bigger.
        %
        % ROOM MATCHED THE SAME WAY as the local test: byte for byte on the
        % spelling, because a room IS its name. The comparison is against the
        % ASKER's room, not against anything the peer said about rooms.
        FOR i IN 1:maxRemote DO
            IF rmtSystem(i) >< 0 THEN
                IF sameRoomAs(askerSlot, i) THEN
                    % name + '@' + 'D' + up to 5 digits, plus a separator
                    rmtNameLen(i) + 8 =: room
                    IF textLen + room <= 250 THEN
                        IF textLen > 0 THEN
                            spaceByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                            at + 1 =: at
                            textLen + 1 =: textLen
                        ENDIF
                        FOR j IN 1:rmtNameLen(i) DO
                            rmtName(i, j) =: outBuf(at)
                            at + 1 =: at
                        ENDFOR
                        atByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                        at + 1 =: at
                        dByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                        at + 1 =: at
                        putNumber(rmtSystem(i), at) =: at
                        at - textAt =: textLen
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        % Now the length is known. It cannot exceed 250, so the high byte the
        % format carries stays zero.
        at - textAt =: textLen
        textLen =: outBuf(3)
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE


    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Build the answer to /topic: the ROOM NAME in the name field, the topic in
    % the text. An empty text means the room has no topic yet - the client says
    % so in words rather than showing a blank line.
    %
    % The room name goes in the NAME field on purpose. It is the one message
    % that is about a room rather than about a person, and putting the room
    % where a nickname normally sits means the client can label it without
    % having to remember which room it asked about - which matters, because a
    % /join can land between the question and the answer.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER) : buildTopic(slot)
        INTEGER : i, at, t, len

        kTopic =: outBuf(0)
        mbrRoomLen(slot) =: outBuf(1)
        2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:mbrRoomLen(slot) DO
            mbrRoom(slot, i) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR

        findTopic(slot) =: t
        0 =: len
        IF t >< 0 THEN
            topicTextLen(t) =: len
        ENDIF

        0 =: outBuf(at)                       % text length, high byte
        len =: outBuf(at + 1)                  % low byte; len <= 64
        at + 2 =: at
        IF t >< 0 THEN
            FOR i IN 1:len DO
                topicText(t, i) =: outBuf(at)
                at + 1 =: at
            ENDFOR
        ENDIF
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Build the answer to /list: every room that somebody is sitting in, with
    % how many people are in it - "LOBBY(2) GENERAL(1)".
    %
    % THE ROOM LIST IS DERIVED FROM THE SEATS, NOT KEPT. There is no room
    % table to go stale: a room exists exactly as long as somebody is in it,
    % which is the same rule the broadcast already follows. A room with a topic
    % and nobody in it does NOT appear - it is not a place you can talk to.
    %
    % The walk is O(seats squared) and that is fine at sixteen: for each seat,
    % look back over the earlier seats and skip it if its room has already been
    % counted. No temporary room table, so nothing to size wrong.
    %
    % BOUNDED like buildWho, and for the same reason - see the note there.
    % Rooms that do not fit are dropped rather than written past the end.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER : buildRooms
        INTEGER : i, j, at, textLen, count, need, tens, ones
        BOOLEAN : already

        kRooms =: outBuf(0)
        0 =: outBuf(1)                        % no name: the SERVER is answering
        2 =: at
        0 =: outBuf(at)                       % text length, high byte
        0 =: outBuf(at + 1)                   % low byte, filled in below
        at + 2 =: at
        0 =: textLen

        FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
            IF memberUsed(i) = 1 THEN
                % Has an earlier seat already named this room?
                %
                % GUARDED, because seat 1 has no earlier seats and "FOR j IN
                % 1:0" is an EMPTY RANGE. This compiler checks nothing, so an
                % empty FOR is not a thing to rely on - the first room would be
                % compared against seat 0, which is outside the array.
                FALSE =: already
                IF i > 1 THEN
                    FOR j IN 1:(i - 1) DO
                        IF memberUsed(j) = 1 THEN
                            IF sameRoom(i, j) THEN
                                TRUE =: already
                            ENDIF
                        ENDIF
                    ENDFOR
                ENDIF

                IF NOT already THEN
                    % Count everybody in it, including seat i itself.
                    0 =: count
                    FOR j IN 1:maxSeats DO
                        IF memberUsed(j) = 1 THEN
                            IF sameRoom(i, j) THEN
                                count + 1 =: count
                            ENDIF
                        ENDIF
                    ENDFOR

                    % name + "(" + up to two digits + ")" + a leading space
                    % once there is something to separate from.
                    mbrRoomLen(i) + 4 =: need
                    IF textLen > 0 THEN
                        need + 1 =: need
                    ENDIF

                    IF textLen + need <= 250 THEN
                        IF textLen > 0 THEN
                            spaceByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                            at + 1 =: at
                            textLen + 1 =: textLen
                        ENDIF
                        FOR j IN 1:mbrRoomLen(i) DO
                            mbrRoom(i, j) =: outBuf(at)
                            at + 1 =: at
                        ENDFOR
                        textLen + mbrRoomLen(i) =: textLen

                        openParen(0) =: outBuf(at)
                        at + 1 =: at
                        textLen + 1 =: textLen

                        % At most sixteen seats, so at most two digits. Written
                        % straight out rather than through a general routine -
                        % a loop for a number that cannot exceed 16 would be
                        % more code than the thing it replaces.
                        count / 10 =: tens
                        count - (tens * 10) =: ones
                        IF tens > 0 THEN
                            digitZero + tens =: outBuf(at)
                            at + 1 =: at
                            textLen + 1 =: textLen
                        ENDIF
                        digitZero + ones =: outBuf(at)
                        at + 1 =: at
                        textLen + 1 =: textLen

                        closeParen(0) =: outBuf(at)
                        at + 1 =: at
                        textLen + 1 =: textLen
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        textLen =: outBuf(3)
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Build the answer to /who FROM SOMEBODY WITH NO SEAT: every room, and the
    % people in each.
    %
    %     LOBBY: TESTER OLAV  GENERAL: ANNA@D102
    %
    % Rooms separated by TWO spaces, people inside a room by one. No parsing on
    % the client side at all - it prints the text as it stands. That is on
    % purpose: a format nobody parses cannot be parsed wrongly, and this answer
    % exists to be READ by a person who has just been told they are nowhere.
    %
    % THE ROOM WALK IS buildRooms', DELIBERATELY. Same O(seats squared) skip of
    % rooms an earlier seat already named, same rule that a room exists exactly
    % as long as somebody is in it. If the two ever disagree, /list and /who
    % would name different sets of rooms, which is the kind of small lie that
    % takes an afternoon to chase.
    %
    % REMOTE PEOPLE ARE INCLUDED, qualified - "ANNA@D102" - and their room comes
    % from the peer's own report. A remote room with nobody local in it WILL
    % therefore be missed, because the walk starts from local seats. That is a
    % known gap, not an oversight: fixing it means walking the remote table for
    % rooms too and de-duplicating across both, and it is not worth the code
    % until somebody has actually sat alone in a room on another machine.
    %
    % BOUNDED at 250 like its neighbours - see the note on buildWho. What does
    % not fit is dropped, never written past the end of outBuf.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER : buildAllWho
        INTEGER : i, j, k, at, textAt, textLen, need
        BOOLEAN : already

        kAllWho =: outBuf(0)
        0 =: outBuf(1)                        % no name: the SERVER is answering
        2 =: at
        0 =: outBuf(at)                       % text length, high byte
        0 =: outBuf(at + 1)                   % low byte, filled in below
        at + 2 =: textAt
        textAt =: at
        0 =: textLen

        FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
            IF memberUsed(i) = 1 THEN

                % Has an earlier seat already named this room? Guarded for the
                % same reason buildRooms guards it: seat 1 has no earlier seats
                % and an empty FOR range is not a thing this compiler promises.
                FALSE =: already
                IF i > 1 THEN
                    FOR j IN 1:(i - 1) DO
                        IF memberUsed(j) = 1 THEN
                            IF sameRoom(i, j) THEN
                                TRUE =: already
                            ENDIF
                        ENDIF
                    ENDFOR
                ENDIF

                IF NOT already THEN
                    % ---- the room name, then a colon ----------------------
                    % Two spaces before it once there is something to separate
                    % from, so the groups read apart at a glance.
                    mbrRoomLen(i) + 1 =: need
                    IF textLen > 0 THEN
                        need + 2 =: need
                    ENDIF

                    IF textLen + need <= 250 THEN
                        IF textLen > 0 THEN
                            spaceByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                            at + 1 =: at
                            spaceByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                            at + 1 =: at
                        ENDIF
                        FOR j IN 1:mbrRoomLen(i) DO
                            mbrRoom(i, j) =: outBuf(at)
                            at + 1 =: at
                        ENDFOR
                        colonByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                        at + 1 =: at
                        at - textAt =: textLen

                        % ---- everybody local in it ------------------------
                        FOR j IN 1:maxSeats DO
                            IF memberUsed(j) = 1 THEN
                                IF sameRoom(i, j) THEN
                                    memberLen(j) + 1 =: need
                                    IF textLen + need <= 250 THEN
                                        spaceByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                                        at + 1 =: at
                                        FOR k IN 1:memberLen(j) DO
                                            memberName(j, k) =: outBuf(at)
                                            at + 1 =: at
                                        ENDFOR
                                        at - textAt =: textLen
                                    ENDIF
                                ENDIF
                            ENDIF
                        ENDFOR

                        % ---- and everybody remote in it -------------------
                        % sameRoomAs compares a SEAT's room against a remote
                        % row, and seat i is in this room by construction, so
                        % it is the right thing to compare against.
                        FOR j IN 1:maxRemote DO
                            IF rmtSystem(j) >< 0 THEN
                                IF sameRoomAs(i, j) THEN
                                    % name + '@' + 'D' + up to 5 digits + space
                                    rmtNameLen(j) + 8 =: need
                                    IF textLen + need <= 250 THEN
                                        spaceByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                                        at + 1 =: at
                                        FOR k IN 1:rmtNameLen(j) DO
                                            rmtName(j, k) =: outBuf(at)
                                            at + 1 =: at
                                        ENDFOR
                                        atByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                                        at + 1 =: at
                                        dByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                                        at + 1 =: at
                                        putNumber(rmtSystem(j), at) =: at
                                        at - textAt =: textLen
                                    ENDIF
                                ENDIF
                            ENDIF
                        ENDFOR
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        % An EMPTY text is a real answer here and the client says so in words:
        % it means nobody is anywhere, which is different from "I could not
        % tell you". Leaving the length at zero is the whole implementation.
        at - textAt =: textLen
        textLen =: outBuf(3)
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Build a message whose name comes from a member slot rather than from the
    % incoming buffer - used for Said, where the speaker is named by the room
    % and NOT by whatever the sender claimed.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            buildFromSlot(kind, slot, textAt, textLength)
        INTEGER : i, at
        kind =: outBuf(0)
        memberLen(slot) =: outBuf(1)
        2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:memberLen(slot) DO
            memberName(slot, i) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        0 =: outBuf(at)
        textLength =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
            inBuf(textAt + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Copy a name out of the incoming buffer into a member slot.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : copyNameIn(slot, at, length)
        INTEGER : i
        FOR i IN 1:length DO
            inBuf(at + i - 1) =: memberName(slot, i)
        ENDFOR
        length =: memberLen(slot)
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Remember which room a member joined.
    %
    % The room arrives in the JOIN'S TEXT FIELD - the client puts it there
    % because there is one port now and the room can no longer be inferred from
    % which port the message came in on.
    %
    % A JOIN WITH NO ROOM IS PUT IN THE HOME ROOM rather than refused. An older
    % client that predates this change sends an empty text, and refusing it
    % would take the room out from under somebody whose only crime is not having
    % been rebuilt yet. LOBBY is where they used to be, so LOBBY is where they
    % go.
    %
    % Bounded at sixteen, the width of the column. PLANC checks no array bound,
    % so this test is the only thing between a long room name and whatever the
    % loader put next.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : copyRoomIn(slot, at, length)
        INTEGER : i, take

        length =: take
        IF take > 16 THEN
            16 =: take
        ENDIF

        IF take <= 0 THEN
            FOR i IN 1:lenHomeRoom DO
                homeRoom(i - 1) =: mbrRoom(slot, i)
            ENDFOR
            lenHomeRoom =: mbrRoomLen(slot)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        FOR i IN 1:take DO
            inBuf(at + i - 1) =: mbrRoom(slot, i)
        ENDFOR
        take =: mbrRoomLen(slot)
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Build the Renamed announcement. Returns its total length.
    %
    % The NEW name goes in the name field and the OLD one in the text. That is
    % not a choice made here - ChatMessageKind.cs states it, and the reason is
    % that everybody has a transcript on screen under the old name and no way to
    % connect the two without it. CALL THIS BEFORE the slot is changed, while
    % the old name is still in it.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            buildRenamed(slot, newAt, newLength)
        INTEGER : i, at
        kRenamed =: outBuf(0)
        newLength =: outBuf(1)
        2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:newLength DO
            inBuf(newAt + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR

        % The old name as the text.
        0 =: outBuf(at)
        memberLen(slot) =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:memberLen(slot) DO
            memberName(slot, i) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Send outBuf to one magic number.
    %
    % XMPFGET reserves a buffer and makes it CURRENT; XMPFWRI writes into the
    % current message; XMPFSND sends it. Note the userAddress coercion - the
    % guide's own examples write ADDR(buf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER4, INTEGER) : sendTo(magic, length)
        XMMSGIDENTIFIER : ident
        INTEGER : deadSlot
        % Each of the three steps below printed its status on EVERY send while
        % the room was being brought up - three lines per message, per member.
        % They now speak only when a step fails, which is the case that was
        % actually worth seeing: a send that half-succeeds is how a member ends
        % up in the room and hearing nothing.
        xmpfget(0, length, ident) =: returnStatus
        IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL18', 'SV: get failed st=')
            OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            logLine('SV get failed st= ', 18, returnStatus)
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        xmpfwri(0, 0, &
            ADDR(outBuf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS, &
            0, length, writtenLength) =: returnStatus
        IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL18', 'SV: wri failed st=')
            OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            logLine('SV wri failed st= ', 18, returnStatus)
        ENDIF
        IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
            xmpfrel(0, ident) =: returnStatus
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        % XMPSEND, NOT XMPFSND - and this is the whole bug, not a tidy-up.
        %
        % XMPFSND takes no message parameter. The guide says which one it picks:
        % "the current (default) message buffer is assumed, namely the 'port
        % current' message if one exists, or, if none, the 'task current'
        % message" (ND-60.164.3, XMPFSND, EXPLANATION). XMPFGET above only makes
        % the new buffer TASK current - it has no port parameter and cannot make
        % it port current. This port has just received a message, so a 'port
        % current' message may well still exist, and XMPFSND would send THAT
        % instead of the reply we just built. The guide never says whether
        % XMPFREL clears the port-current pointer, so relying on it would be a
        % guess.
        %
        % XMPSEND("Send message, not necessarily current") removes the question:
        % "The message specified by msgIdentifier is set as 'task current'
        % message and as 'port current' message for localPort. Then the message
        % will be sent as described under routine XMPFSND."
        %
        % Appendix G's SERVER gets away with XMPFSND only because it never calls
        % XMPFGET - it replies in the very buffer that arrived, which is already
        % both port and task current. We cannot copy that: a broadcast needs one
        % fresh buffer per member.
        xmpsend(sendFlags, ident, myPort, magic) =: returnStatus
        IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL18', 'SV: snd failed st=')
            OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            logLine('SV snd failed st= ', 18, returnStatus)

            % ---- A MEMBER WHO IS NO LONGER THERE, IF WE ARE EVER TOLD HERE --
            % Two statuses would mean the seat is holding somebody who has gone.
            % Both are read off XMP-B02:DEFS rather than guessed:
            %
            %   XMXEIMA 16915  Invalid magic number
            %   XMXEPCL 16932  Remote port closed while message queued
            %
            % NEITHER OF THEM ACTUALLY TURNS UP for a client killed on this
            % machine - measured, see the note on sendFlags. XMPSEND took the
            % message for a closed port and answered XMOK. This test is kept
            % rather than deleted because it costs one compare and it is right
            % if a status ever does arrive - a remote member across a link is
            % the obvious untested case - but it is NOT what fixes the leak.
            % The XMTRE branch in the receive loop is.
            %
            % ONLY THESE TWO, and deliberately not "any failure". XMXEMFL
            % (message space full) is a bad moment, not a dead member, and
            % throwing somebody out of the room because the machine was briefly
            % short of buffers would be worse than the bug being fixed. Every
            % other status still prints above.
            IF returnStatus = XMXEIMA OR returnStatus = XMXEPCL THEN
                findByMagic(magic) =: deadSlot
                IF deadSlot >< 0 THEN
                    1 =: memberDead(deadSlot)
                    1 =: deadFound
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDIF
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Replay what this room remembers, to ONE person, oldest first.
    %
    % Sent to the JOINER ALONE - the room has not changed, so nobody else has
    % anything to learn. Oldest first because that is the order it was said in;
    % a screen that fills backwards is a puzzle, not a transcript.
    %
    % WHERE TO START: the ring holds at most histSize messages, so the oldest
    % still present is next - size, or 0 if fewer than size have ever been
    % said. Nothing here has to know whether the ring has wrapped; the
    % arithmetic gives the same answer either way.
    %
    % A BLOCK WHOSE ID IS NOT THE ONE EXPECTED IS SKIPPED, and that is what
    % makes a half-written or never-written block harmless. It happens for real
    % on a file created and not yet filled: SINTRAN gives back whatever the
    % blocks held, and without this test the room would replay rubbish as
    % though somebody had said it.
    %
    % NOT SENT ACROSS A TRUNK, and that is deliberate - see the registry.
    % Somebody joining on D102 sees what was said ON D102. Relaying history
    % would mean deciding whose copy is authoritative and merging rings across
    % machines whose clocks differ.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % INTEGER4 for the magic, because that is what sendTo takes. An
    % XMMSGIDENTIFIER is what xmpfget fills in and is a different thing; the
    % magic is the ADDRESS a reply goes to, and it is 32 bits.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER4) : histReplay(t, magic)
        INTEGER : dev, id, first, blk, i, at, nLen, tLen, length

        IF t < 1 THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        histOpen(t) =: dev
        IF dev = 0 THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        histNext(t) - histSize(t) =: first
        IF first < 0 THEN
            0 =: first
        ENDIF

        FOR id IN first:(histNext(t) - 1) DO
            id - ((id / histSize(t)) * histSize(t)) =: blk

            ON ROUTINEERROR DO
                -1 =: histBlock(0)            % unreadable: cannot match an id
            ENDON
            MN117(dev, 0, histBlock, blk, histWords)

            IF histBlock(0) = id THEN
                histBlock(1) =: nLen
                histBlock(2) =: tLen

                % A BLOCK CAN HOLD RUBBISH AND STILL MATCH ITS ID. The lengths
                % come off the disk, so they are bounded here before they are
                % used as loop limits - a stale block claiming a 30000-byte
                % name would otherwise write clean off the end of outBuf, and
                % PLANC checks no array bound.
                IF nLen < 0 THEN
                    0 =: nLen
                ENDIF
                IF nLen > 16 THEN
                    16 =: nLen
                ENDIF
                IF tLen < 0 THEN
                    0 =: tLen
                ENDIF
                IF tLen > 200 THEN
                    200 =: tLen
                ENDIF

                % FOR, NOT WHILE. PLANC has no WHILE...ENDWHILE: WHILE is a
                % CONTINUE-TEST inside DO ... ENDDO, so "WHILE c DO ... ENDWHILE"
                % is two errors, and the second one - ENDWHILE - is reported as
                % ILLEGAL SYNTAX, which reads like punctuation and is not. The
                % linter caught this before it reached the machine; it would
                % otherwise have cost a four-minute compile to be told.
                kHist =: outBuf(0)
                nLen =: outBuf(1)
                2 =: at
                FOR i IN 1:nLen DO
                    histBlock(2 + i) =: outBuf(at)
                    at + 1 =: at
                ENDFOR
                0 =: outBuf(at)
                tLen =: outBuf(at + 1)
                at + 2 =: at
                FOR i IN 1:tLen DO
                    histBlock(2 + nLen + i) =: outBuf(at)
                    at + 1 =: at
                ENDFOR

                at =: length
                sendTo(magic, length)
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Send to everybody in one room, optionally leaving one member out.
    %
    % ROOM-AWARE NOW, AND IT HAS TO BE. With one port every member of every
    % room sits in this one table, so an unfiltered broadcast would put
    % GENERAL's traffic on LOBBY's screens. Under the old port-per-room model
    % that could not happen - a separate port meant a separate server table -
    % which is why this routine had no room test until today.
    %
    % TWO PARAMETERS, NOT ONE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE FIX.
    %
    % There was one slot number doing two unrelated jobs: naming the room to
    % send to, AND naming the member to leave out. Slot 0 cannot mean both
    % "no room" and "leave nobody out", and it was read as the first while
    % every Say, Rename and Leave passed it meaning the second - so the guard
    % at the top returned before sending a single letter.
    %
    % MEASURED ON D100, 2026-08-20, with two clients in LOBBY: neither saw a
    % word the other said, while /who listed them both and the Joined notice
    % arrived. Those two are exactly the paths that do NOT go through here
    % with a zero - /who answers the asker directly, and Joined passes a real
    % slot. The room table was never wrong; nothing was ever sent.
    %
    % So they are separate now and neither is overloaded:
    %   roomOfSlot  whose room defines the audience. 0 sends to nobody,
    %               because "everybody in the same room as nobody" is nobody.
    %   skipSlot    one member to leave out, or 0 to leave nobody out.
    %
    % roomOfSlot's own seat may already be empty - a member is removed from
    % the room BEFORE the room is told they left - and that still works,
    % because sameRoom reads only the stored room name, which nothing clears.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : broadcast(length, roomOfSlot, skipSlot)
        INTEGER : i, sent
        IF roomOfSlot = 0 THEN
            % Worth a line of its own. "Nobody was in the room" and "this was
            % never called" look identical from outside, and telling them apart
            % is most of the work when a message does not arrive.
            logLine('SV bcast NO ROOM  ', 18, skipSlot)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        0 =: sent
        FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
            IF memberUsed(i) >< 0 THEN
                IF i >< skipSlot THEN
                    IF sameRoom(i, roomOfSlot) THEN
                        sendTo(memberMagic(i), length)
                        sent + 1 =: sent
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        % HOW MANY IT CHOSE, every time, and this is the instrument the whole
        % afternoon of 2026-08-20 was missing. A zero here says the room filter
        % rejected everybody; a number with nothing arriving says the sends are
        % the problem; no line at all says broadcast was never reached. Three
        % different faults that all look the same from a terminal.
        logLine('SV bcast sent to  ', 18, sent)
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Empty the seats of members who have vanished, and give each seat back.
    %
    % CALLED FROM THE TOP OF THE RECEIVE LOOP, never from inside a broadcast -
    % see the note on memberDead for why that is not a style choice.
    %
    % A vanished member costs TWO things and they are not the same thing:
    %
    %   the seat in this program  memberUsed, which decides who is in the room;
    %   the seat in XROUT         the free-connection counter, which decides
    %                             whether a new joiner's letter is forwarded at
    %                             all. XROUT decremented it to deliver the join
    %                             and only the port's owner - this program -
    %                             ever puts one back, with XMPINFC.
    %
    % Clearing only the first leaves the room looking empty in LIST-NAMES while
    % it refuses everybody. MEASURED 2026-08-18, before this routine existed:
    % CHAT-LOBBY went 16 free to 15 when a client was aborted with ESC
    % ("USER BREAK AT 16665B") and stayed at 15 for good.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : reapDead
        INTEGER : round, i, length

        % Cleared FIRST, not last. A broadcast below can discover another dead
        % member - telling the room somebody left is exactly when the next dead
        % port gets found - and clearing at the end would throw that mark away.
        % Cleared here, a mark raised during our own broadcasts simply re-arms
        % this routine for the next message.
        0 =: deadFound

        % Bounded rather than a WHILE. Each pass empties at least one seat and a
        % pass can only mark members still in the room, so maxSeats passes empty
        % the worst room there is. An unbounded loop here would be taking on
        % trust that a broadcast to a dying room always terminates, and this
        % program has no way to recover if it does not.
        FOR round IN 1:maxSeats DO
            FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
                IF memberDead(i) >< 0 THEN
                    0 =: memberDead(i)
                    IF memberUsed(i) >< 0 THEN
                        % Same order as the kLeave path: build the announcement
                        % while the name is still in the slot, empty the seat,
                        % THEN tell the room - so the departing member is not
                        % sent their own Left notice at a port already gone,
                        % which would only mark them dead all over again.
                        buildFromSlot(kLeft, i, 0, 0) =: length
                        0 =: memberUsed(i)
                        broadcast(length, i, i)   % their room, not them

                        xmpinfc(0, myPort, 1, 0) =: returnStatus
                        IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
                            OUTPUT(1, 'AL18', 'SV: infc failed st')
                            OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
                            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
                            logLine('SV infc faild st= ', 18, returnStatus)
                        ENDIF

                        % Worth one line: an operator watching the room needs to
                        % be able to tell "they left" from "they died", because
                        % only the second is evidence of anything.
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL21', 'SV: reaped dead slot ')
                        OUTPUT(1, 'I6', i)
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
                        logLine('SV reaped slot    ', 18, i)
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDFOR
        ENDFOR
    ENDROUTINE

    % ----- the refusal reasons, worded exactly as ChatRoom.cs words them ----
    % Carried on the wire so a client shows the same sentence whichever door it
    % came in by. If these and ChatRoom ever diverge, one of the two is wrong.
    %
    % EACH LENGTH IS WRITTEN OUT beside its string, and that is deliberate. An
    % earlier version called LENGTH(reason) - PLANC has no such routine. The one
    % it does have is SIZE, whose syntax is "SIZE ident =: var" with no
    % parentheses, and which returns the STORAGE size; what that means for a
    % BYTES value passed as a routine parameter is not established. Passing the
    % count removes the question rather than trading one guess for another.
    %
    % The cost is that a string and its number must be changed together. That is
    % a real risk and it is why they are on adjacent lines.
    BYTES : reasonNeedNick := 'a nickname is required'
    INTEGER : lenNeedNick := 22
    BYTES : reasonNickTaken    := 'that nickname is taken'
    INTEGER : lenNickTaken := 22
    BYTES : reasonAlreadyIn    := 'already joined'
    INTEGER : lenAlreadyIn := 14
    BYTES : reasonRoomFull     := 'the room is full'
    INTEGER : lenRoomFull := 16
    BYTES : reasonTopicFull    := 'too many rooms have topics'
    INTEGER : lenTopicFull := 26

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Send outBuf to a NAMED PORT ON ANOTHER SYSTEM, through XROUT.
    %
    % THIS SERVER HAS NEVER ORIGINATED A LETTER BEFORE. Everything it sends is a
    % reply to a magic number that arrived with a message - sendTo above. A trunk
    % is different: we address a machine that has not spoken to us, and may never
    % have, so there is no magic to reply to.
    %
    % The sequence is the COSMOS Programmer Guide's worked example (page 99) and
    % is exactly what CHAT.PLNC's sendJoin does:
    %
    %   XMPBLET   build an XSLET letter header naming SYSTEM and PORT NAME
    %   XMPFGET   reserve an XMSG buffer
    %   XMPFWRI   write the header, then OUR bytes after it
    %   XMPROUT   hand the whole thing to XROUT, which does the lookup
    %
    % ADDRESSED BY NAME, NEVER BY A REMEMBERED MAGIC. A magic carries a
    % generation, so one kept from before the peer restarted is stale in a way
    % nothing reports until a send quietly fails. Looking the name up every time
    % is what lets a machine that has been switched off for a day come back with
    % nobody touching either end.
    %
    % A NON-ZERO STATUS IS NOT A FAULT HERE. The peer being absent is the normal
    % case a trunk exists to survive; the caller records "still not up".
    %
    % THE SYSTEM IS A NAME, AS BYTES - NOT A NUMBER. That is the whole of this
    % trap and it cost THREE compile cycles, all reporting the same thing:
    %
    %     2521 (1861)/SENDTONAME *** ERROR - ILLEGAL DATA TYPE "XMPBLET"
    %
    % The error names the CALL, so the first two attempts went looking at the
    % PORT NAME argument - was it sliced, was it a parameter - and neither was
    % the problem. It is parameter 5, the SYSTEM: XMPBLET wants the system's
    % NAME as BYTES ('D102'), and an INTEGER 102 is refused.
    %
    % CHATMON.PLNC says so in as many words, from 2026-08-20:
    %
    %     "THE SYSTEM NAME IS BYTES, NOT AN INTEGER ... Declaring it INTEGER
    %      drew *** ERROR - ILLEGAL DATA TYPE "XMPBLET" on the CALL rather than
    %      on the declaration, which sends you looking at the arguments you
    %      changed instead of the one you copied wrong."
    %
    % It even predicted how the search would go wrong, and it went exactly that
    % way. READ THE NOTE THAT NAMES YOUR ERROR before changing anything.
    %
    % So the number is turned into a name here - 'D' and the decimal digits,
    % which is the convention every DEFINE-REMOTE-NAME on these machines uses
    % (DEFINE-REMOTE-NAME,,D102,102). peerName is declared with explicit BOUNDS
    % and filled at RUNTIME, so it MUST be passed as a subarray saying how much
    % of itself is the name - the same shape as the client's roomName.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : sendToName(system, length)
        XMMSGIDENTIFIER : ident
        INTEGER : offSet, uLength, wLength, st, nmAt

        % 'D' then the digits - D100, D102, D103.
        dByte(0) =: peerName(0)
        putPeerDigits(system, 1) =: nmAt

        xmpblet(letterBuf, 64, offSet, 123, peerName(0:nmAt - 1), trkName) =: st
        IF st >< XMOK THEN
            st RETURN
        ENDIF

        xmpfget(0, 200, ident) =: st
        IF st >< XMOK THEN
            st RETURN
        ENDIF

        % The header first...
        offSet =: uLength
        xmpfwri(0, 0, &
            ADDR(letterBuf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS, &
            0, uLength, wLength) =: st

        % ...then our own bytes after it. XROUT reads only the header and
        % forwards the rest untouched.
        xmpfwri(0, wLength, &
            ADDR(outBuf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS, &
            0, length, offSet) =: st

        xmprout(0, ident, trunkPort) =: st
        st RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Which peer row holds this system, or 0.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER) : findPeer(system)
        INTEGER : i
        FOR i IN 1:maxPeers DO
            IF peerSystem(i) = system THEN
                i RETURN
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        0 RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Say HELLO to one peer.
    %
    % ADDRESSED BY NAME THROUGH XROUT, not by a magic number we remember. A
    % magic carries a generation, so one kept from before the peer restarted is
    % stale in a way that is invisible until a send fails. The name is looked up
    % fresh every time, which is exactly what makes a peer that has been away
    % come back without anybody touching either machine.
    %
    % A FAILED SEND IS NOT AN ERROR HERE. A peer that is switched off is the
    % normal case this routine exists to handle - the status is recorded by the
    % caller as "still not up", not reported as a fault.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : sendHello(system, direction)
        INTEGER : at, st

        kTrkHello =: outBuf(0)
        0 =: outBuf(1)                        % no name: a MACHINE is speaking
        2 =: at
        0 =: outBuf(at)                       % text length, high byte
        1 =: outBuf(at + 1)                   % one byte of text
        at + 2 =: at
        direction =: outBuf(at)               % trkAsk or trkAnswer
        at + 1 =: at

        sendToName(system, at) =: st
        st RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % A peer's member spoke. Show it to our own people in that room.
    %
    % SENT ONE BY ONE rather than through broadcast(), because broadcast picks
    % its audience from a SLOT - "everybody in the same room as this member" -
    % and the speaker has no slot here. They are not on this machine.
    %
    % The room arrives as text and is compared against each member's stored
    % room, byte for byte, the same rule as everywhere else: a room IS its name.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            deliverRemoteSaid(system, nameAt, nameLength, textAt, textLength)
        INTEGER : i, j, at, roomAt, roomLen2, msgAt, msgLen, p, textStart
        BOOLEAN : match

        % Split "ROOM/message" at the FIRST slash. A message may well contain
        % slashes of its own; the room cannot, so the first one is the divider.
        textAt =: roomAt
        textAt =: p
        DO
            WHILE p < textAt + textLength AND inBuf(p) >< slashByte(0)
            p + 1 =: p
        ENDDO
        p - roomAt =: roomLen2

        % No slash at all is a malformed forward - drop it rather than deliver
        % something to a room nobody named.
        IF p >= textAt + textLength THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        p + 1 =: msgAt
        textAt + textLength - msgAt =: msgLen

        FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
            IF memberUsed(i) = 1 THEN
                TRUE =: match
                IF mbrRoomLen(i) >< roomLen2 THEN
                    FALSE =: match
                ELSE
                    FOR j IN 1:roomLen2 DO
                        IF mbrRoom(i, j) >< inBuf(roomAt + j - 1) THEN
                            FALSE =: match
                        ENDIF
                    ENDFOR
                ENDIF

                IF match THEN
                    % "NAME@Dnnn" in the name field, so a client that already
                    % prints "<name>: <text>" needs no change at all.
                    kSaid =: outBuf(0)
                    2 =: at
                    FOR j IN 1:nameLength DO
                        inBuf(nameAt + j - 1) =: outBuf(at)
                        at + 1 =: at
                    ENDFOR
                    atByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                    at + 1 =: at
                    dByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                    at + 1 =: at
                    putNumber(system, at) =: at
                    at - 2 =: outBuf(1)       % the name length, now known

                    0 =: outBuf(at)
                    0 =: outBuf(at + 1)
                    at + 2 =: at
                    at =: textStart
                    FOR j IN 1:msgLen DO
                        IF at < 250 THEN
                            inBuf(msgAt + j - 1) =: outBuf(at)
                            at + 1 =: at
                        ENDIF
                    ENDFOR
                    at - textStart =: outBuf(textStart - 1)

                    sendTo(memberMagic(i), at)
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Send what a local member just said to every peer we believe is up.
    %
    % ONLY TO PEERS THAT ARE UP. A peer that is Unknown or Down gets nothing:
    % the letter would bounce, and a bounce per message per absent machine is a
    % lot of noise for something we already know.
    %
    % The text is rebuilt as "ROOM/message" rather than sent as two fields,
    % which keeps it the same shape as the member list and readable on a trace.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            forwardSaid(slot, textAt, textLength)
        INTEGER : i, j, at, st, textStart

        % STAMPED ONCE, HERE, and the same number goes to every peer. That is
        % what lets a machine two hops away tell one line arriving twice from
        % two different lines. Stamping inside the loop would give each peer a
        % different number and defeat the whole thing.
        myLineId + 1 =: myLineId
        % WRAPS AT 32000, NOT 65535. A PLANC INTEGER on the ND-100 is SIXTEEN
        % BITS SIGNED, so it stops at 32767 - counting past that gives a
        % NEGATIVE number, and a negative id would be written out as two
        % nonsense bytes. 32000 leaves plenty of room and is obviously safe.
        % The window is 64 entries, so a number is long forgotten before it
        % can come round again.
        IF myLineId > 32000 THEN
            1 =: myLineId
        ENDIF

        FOR i IN 1:maxPeers DO
            IF peerSystem(i) >< 0 THEN
                IF peerState(i) = peerUp THEN

                    % REBUILT FOR EVERY PEER, not built once outside the loop.
                    % sendToName hands outBuf to XMPFWRI, and building it once
                    % would be fine today - but the moment anything in the send
                    % path touches outBuf this becomes a bug that only shows up
                    % with two peers, which is the worst kind to find.
                    % KIND 53, not 51: it carries who we are and the number we
                    % just gave this line, so a machine further out can spot a
                    % repeat. hops is one less than the ceiling because this
                    % send IS the first hop.
                    kTrkRelId =: outBuf(0)
                    mySystem / 256 =: outBuf(1)
                    mySystem MOD 256 =: outBuf(2)
                    maxHops - 1 =: outBuf(3)
                    myLineId / 256 =: outBuf(4)
                    myLineId MOD 256 =: outBuf(5)
                    memberLen(slot) =: outBuf(6)
                    7 =: at
                    FOR j IN 1:memberLen(slot) DO
                        memberName(slot, j) =: outBuf(at)
                        at + 1 =: at
                    ENDFOR

                    0 =: outBuf(at)
                    0 =: outBuf(at + 1)       % low byte, filled in below
                    at + 2 =: at
                    at =: textStart

                    FOR j IN 1:mbrRoomLen(slot) DO
                        mbrRoom(slot, j) =: outBuf(at)
                        at + 1 =: at
                    ENDFOR
                    slashByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
                    at + 1 =: at

                    % CLAMPED. The text arrived from a client and outBuf is 256
                    % bytes; the room and the name have already taken some of
                    % it. PLANC checks no bound, so anything long enough would
                    % write straight past the end.
                    FOR j IN 1:textLength DO
                        IF at < 250 THEN
                            inBuf(textAt + j - 1) =: outBuf(at)
                            at + 1 =: at
                        ENDIF
                    ENDFOR

                    at - textStart =: outBuf(textStart - 1)
                    sendToName(peerSystem(i), at) =: st
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Pass a line on to every peer EXCEPT the one it came from.
    %
    % THIS IS THE WHOLE OF PHASE D. D102 and D103 have no trunk between them -
    % D100 is the only machine either can reach - so a line typed on D103 gets
    % to D102 only if D100 forwards it. Measured on 2026-08-24, with a control:
    % a line from D103 reached D100 and did NOT reach D102, while a line from
    % D100 reached D102 seconds later on the same client.
    %
    % TWO THINGS STOP IT LOOPING FOR EVER, and both are needed:
    %
    %   never back to the sender  a two-machine trunk would otherwise bounce
    %                             the same line between the pair immediately;
    %   the hop count             a RING would come back round the other way,
    %                             where the sender test does not help at all.
    %
    % WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, and the difference matters: the hop count makes a
    % ring TERMINATE, it does not make it DELIVER ONCE. Trace three machines
    % all trunked to each other and a line arrives at some of them twice - once
    % direct, once relayed - and the count only stops that repeating for ever.
    % De-duplication is Phase E of CHAT-FEDERATION-DESIGN.md and is not written.
    % It costs nothing today because the real topology is a CHAIN: D102 and
    % D103 each have exactly one trunk, both to D100, so no line reaches either
    % of them by two routes.
    %
    % The ORIGIN travels rather than being taken from the letter. A receiver
    % normally qualifies the speaker with the system the letter arrived from,
    % and on a forwarded line that is the RELAY - so without this D102 would
    % show a D103 user as being on D100.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Have we seen this line before? Records it if not.
    %
    % ONE routine that both asks and records, deliberately. Two - a check and
    % a separate remember - invite the bug where a caller tests, forgets to
    % record, and every copy looks new, which is a dedup that does nothing.
    %
    % Answers 1 for a line we have not seen (and remembers it), 0 for a
    % repeat (and changes nothing).
    %
    % A line older than the window is treated as NEW again. That is the
    % deliberate limit of a fixed window, written down here so it is not read
    % as a fault later: a repeat arrives within a hop or two of the original,
    % so it is always well inside 64.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : lineIsNew(fromSys, id)
        INTEGER : i

        FOR i IN 1:seenUsed DO
            IF seenSys(i) = fromSys THEN
                IF seenId(i) = id THEN
                    0 RETURN
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        fromSys =: seenSys(seenAt)
        id =: seenId(seenAt)

        seenAt + 1 =: seenAt
        IF seenAt > maxSeen THEN
            1 =: seenAt
        ENDIF
        IF seenUsed < maxSeen THEN
            seenUsed + 1 =: seenUsed
        ENDIF

        1 RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % SEVEN parameters, so SEVEN types. Getting this wrong is not caught by the
    % eye: the compiler answers EXPECTS ")" at the first name it has no type
    % for, which reads like a bracket fault rather than a counting fault.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, &
            INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            relayOn(fromSys, originSys, lineId, hopsLeft, nmAt, nmLen, &
            txAt, txLen)
        INTEGER : i, j, at, st, textStart

        % Nothing to do once the count runs out. Said plainly rather than
        % guarded at the call site, so every caller gets the rule.
        IF hopsLeft <= 0 THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        FOR i IN 1:maxPeers DO
            IF peerSystem(i) >< 0 THEN
                IF peerState(i) = peerUp THEN
                    IF peerSystem(i) >< fromSys THEN

                        % THE ID IS PASSED ON UNTOUCHED. Renumbering it here
                        % would make one line look like two to anybody who
                        % can reach us by a second path - which is the whole
                        % fault dedup exists to stop.
                        kTrkRelId =: outBuf(0)
                        originSys / 256 =: outBuf(1)
                        originSys MOD 256 =: outBuf(2)
                        hopsLeft =: outBuf(3)
                        lineId / 256 =: outBuf(4)
                        lineId MOD 256 =: outBuf(5)
                        nmLen =: outBuf(6)
                        7 =: at
                        FOR j IN 1:nmLen DO
                            inBuf(nmAt + j - 1) =: outBuf(at)
                            at + 1 =: at
                        ENDFOR

                        0 =: outBuf(at)
                        0 =: outBuf(at + 1)     % low byte, filled in below
                        at + 2 =: at
                        at =: textStart

                        % CLAMPED, for the same reason forwardSaid is: the text
                        % came from a stranger and outBuf is 256 bytes. PLANC
                        % checks no bound, so a long enough line would write
                        % straight past the end of it.
                        FOR j IN 1:txLen DO
                            IF at < 250 THEN
                                inBuf(txAt + j - 1) =: outBuf(at)
                                at + 1 =: at
                            ENDIF
                        ENDFOR

                        at - textStart =: outBuf(textStart - 1)
                        sendToName(peerSystem(i), at) =: st
                        nRelayed + 1 =: nRelayed
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % One second has passed. Age every trunk.
    %
    % Called ONLY from the idle sleep, so a tick really is a second. Hanging it
    % off the message loop would make a busy room time peers out faster, which
    % is exactly backwards.
    %
    % TWO THINGS HAPPEN HERE and they are deliberately separate:
    %
    %   a peer we HAD goes quiet    -> declare it Down and DROP ITS PEOPLE, so
    %                                  nobody is shown chatting from a machine
    %                                  that is not answering;
    %   a peer that is not Up       -> say hello again, on a doubling wait.
    %
    % A DOWN PEER KEEPS BEING GREETED. That is the automatic re-join: nobody has
    % to notice the machine came back, and no operator command is needed. The
    % peer's own hello usually arrives first anyway.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % =======================================================================
    % DIRECT MESSAGES
    %
    % THE ADDRESS FORMS, and what each one costs to resolve:
    %
    %   RONNY            a bare alias. Looked for on THIS machine and on every
    %                    machine we hold a member list for. Exactly one match
    %                    is delivered; two or more are REFUSED with the list.
    %   D102!RONNY       qualified. Looked for on that machine alone.
    %
    % A BARE ALIAS MATCHING TWO PEOPLE IS NEVER GUESSED AT. Two machines can
    % each hold a RONNY long before they are ever trunked together, so no
    % global uniqueness rule could have been applied even in principle - and a
    % private message delivered to the wrong person is a privacy failure, not
    % an inconvenience. The refusal carries both candidates so the sender can
    % retype one of them off the screen.
    % =======================================================================

    % Where the '/' is that separates the target from the message, counted
    % from "at". -1 if there is none.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : findSlash(at, length)
        INTEGER : i
        IF length < 1 THEN
            0 - 1 RETURN
        ENDIF
        FOR i IN 0:length - 1 DO
            IF inBuf(at + i) = slashByte(0) THEN
                i RETURN
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        0 - 1 RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % Where the '!' is inside a target, counted from "at". -1 if there is none,
    % which is what says "this is a bare alias".
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : findBang(at, length)
        INTEGER : i
        IF length < 1 THEN
            0 - 1 RETURN
        ENDIF
        FOR i IN 0:length - 1 DO
            IF inBuf(at + i) = bangByte(0) THEN
                i RETURN
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        0 - 1 RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % "D102" or "102" as the number 102.
    %
    % trunkNumber already parses digits and is used for the admin commands, but
    % it REFUSES anything that is not a digit - so it answers 0 for "D102",
    % which is exactly how people write a machine. Leading letters are skipped
    % here rather than changing trunkNumber, because the admin commands take a
    % bare number and loosening that would let a typo through there.
    %
    % Answers 0 for anything with no digits at all, and 0 is not a machine.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : machineNum(at, length)
        INTEGER : i, d, value, seen
        0 =: value
        0 =: seen
        IF length < 1 THEN
            0 RETURN
        ENDIF
        FOR i IN 1:length DO
            inBuf(at + i - 1) =: d
            IF d >= 48 THEN
                IF d <= 57 THEN
                    (value * 10) + (d - 48) =: value
                    1 =: seen
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        IF seen = 0 THEN
            0 RETURN
        ENDIF
        value RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % Does the person we hold in remote row "row" have this name?
    %
    % Case folded exactly as sameName does for a local seat. A direct message
    % that works for a local RONNY and fails for a remote ronny would be a
    % maddening thing to report and an obvious thing to prevent.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : sameRmt(row, at, length)
        INTEGER : j, a, b
        IF rmtNameLen(row) >< length THEN
            0 RETURN
        ENDIF
        FOR j IN 1:length DO
            rmtName(row, j) =: a
            inBuf(at + j - 1) =: b
            IF a > 96 THEN a - 32 =: a ENDIF
            IF b > 96 THEN b - 32 =: b ENDIF
            IF a >< b THEN
                0 RETURN
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        1 RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % Who does this alias mean? Answers into the four dir* variables rather
    % than returning, because it has four things to say and PLANC returns one.
    %
    % wantSys = 0 means "any machine"; otherwise only that one is searched.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            resolveTo(at, length, wantSys)
        INTEGER : i
        0 =: dirCount
        0 =: dirLocal
        0 =: dirRemote
        0 =: dirSystem

        IF wantSys = 0 OR wantSys = mySystem THEN
            FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
                IF memberUsed(i) = 1 THEN
                    IF sameName(i, at, length) = 1 THEN
                        dirCount + 1 =: dirCount
                        IF dirLocal = 0 THEN
                            i =: dirLocal
                            mySystem =: dirSystem
                        ENDIF
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDFOR
        ENDIF

        FOR i IN 1:maxRemote DO
            IF rmtSystem(i) >< 0 THEN
                IF wantSys = 0 OR wantSys = rmtSystem(i) THEN
                    IF sameRmt(i, at, length) = 1 THEN
                        dirCount + 1 =: dirCount
                        IF dirRemote = 0 THEN
                            IF dirLocal = 0 THEN
                                i =: dirRemote
                                rmtSystem(i) =: dirSystem
                            ENDIF
                        ENDIF
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDFOR
        ENDFOR
    ENDROUTINE

    % Put "Dnnn!" into outBuf at "at". Answers the new cursor.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : putMachine(sysNum, at)
        INTEGER : p
        at =: p
        dByte(0) =: outBuf(p)
        p + 1 =: p
        putNumber(sysNum, p) =: p
        bangByte(0) =: outBuf(p)
        p + 1 =: p
        p RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % "Dnnn!NAME" from a LOCAL seat. Answers the new cursor.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            putQualSlot(sysNum, slot, at)
        INTEGER : i, p
        putMachine(sysNum, at) =: p
        FOR i IN 1:memberLen(slot) DO
            memberName(slot, i) =: outBuf(p)
            p + 1 =: p
        ENDFOR
        p RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % "Dnnn!NAME" where the name is sitting in inBuf. Answers the new cursor.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            putQualIn(sysNum, nameAt, nmLen, at)
        INTEGER : i, p
        putMachine(sysNum, at) =: p
        FOR i IN 1:nmLen DO
            inBuf(nameAt + i - 1) =: outBuf(p)
            p + 1 =: p
        ENDFOR
        p RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % REFUSED, and here is why.
    %
    % The reason's length comes from MAXINDEX rather than being typed beside
    % the literal - a hand-counted length builds clean and shows only as a
    % truncated sentence on somebody else's screen.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER4, INTEGER, INTEGER, BYTES) : &
            sendDirBad(magic, tgtAt, tgtLen, reason)
        INTEGER : i, at, textStart, reasonLen

        kDirBad =: outBuf(0)
        tgtLen =: outBuf(1)
        2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:tgtLen DO
            inBuf(tgtAt + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR

        0 =: outBuf(at)
        0 =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at
        at =: textStart

        MAXINDEX(reason, 1) + 1 =: reasonLen
        FOR i IN 1:reasonLen DO
            IF at < 250 THEN
                reason(i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
                at + 1 =: at
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        at - textStart =: outBuf(textStart - 1)
        dmBad + 1 =: dmBad
        sendTo(magic, at)
    ENDROUTINE

    % REFUSED BECAUSE THE NAME MEANS MORE THAN ONE PERSON, and here they are.
    %
    % Carrying the candidates is what makes this usable rather than merely
    % annoying: the sender retypes one of them straight off their screen. The
    % table is walked a second time - resolveTo only kept the FIRST match,
    % because keeping all of them would need a list for a case that is rare.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER4, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            sendDirAmbig(magic, aliasAt, aliasLen)
        INTEGER : i, j, at, textStart, first

        kDirBad =: outBuf(0)
        aliasLen =: outBuf(1)
        2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:aliasLen DO
            inBuf(aliasAt + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR

        0 =: outBuf(at)
        0 =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at
        at =: textStart

        putWord('which one? ', 11, at) =: at
        1 =: first

        FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
            IF memberUsed(i) = 1 THEN
                IF sameName(i, aliasAt, aliasLen) = 1 THEN
                    IF first = 0 THEN
                        putWord(', ', 2, at) =: at
                    ENDIF
                    0 =: first
                    IF at < 200 THEN
                        putQualSlot(mySystem, i, at) =: at
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        FOR i IN 1:maxRemote DO
            IF rmtSystem(i) >< 0 THEN
                IF sameRmt(i, aliasAt, aliasLen) = 1 THEN
                    IF first = 0 THEN
                        putWord(', ', 2, at) =: at
                    ENDIF
                    0 =: first
                    IF at < 200 THEN
                        putMachine(rmtSystem(i), at) =: at
                        FOR j IN 1:rmtNameLen(i) DO
                            rmtName(i, j) =: outBuf(at)
                            at + 1 =: at
                        ENDFOR
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        at - textStart =: outBuf(textStart - 1)
        dmBad + 1 =: dmBad
        sendTo(magic, at)
    ENDROUTINE

    % DELIVERED - and this says to whom, in the form it can be typed back in.
    %
    % Sent on EVERY delivery and not only the ambiguous ones. Refusing an
    % ambiguous alias protects the sender when the collision is visible; this
    % protects them when it is NOT - a machine that just went down, a second
    % RONNY who logged in a moment ago, a trunk that came back with a different
    % user list. A wrong delivery becomes visible at once instead of days
    % later, and it costs one line.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER4, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            sendDirSent(magic, sysNum, nameAt, nmLen)
        INTEGER : at

        kDirSent =: outBuf(0)
        0 =: outBuf(1)                     % filled in below
        2 =: at
        putQualIn(sysNum, nameAt, nmLen, at) =: at
        at - 2 =: outBuf(1)

        % AN EMPTY TEXT STILL WRITES BOTH LENGTH BYTES. Leaving them out makes
        % the message one byte short and the reader takes the next field from
        % the wrong offset - which looks like a corrupt name rather than a
        % missing length. Pinned by the C# golden test.
        0 =: outBuf(at)
        0 =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at

        dmSent + 1 =: dmSent
        sendTo(magic, at)
    ENDROUTINE

    % HAND IT TO SOMEBODY ON THIS MACHINE.
    %
    % The sender is named by their SEAT and never by anything in the message -
    % otherwise anyone could send a direct message as anyone. That is the same
    % rule kSay already follows, and it matters more here, not less.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            deliverLocal(fromSlot, toSlot, msgAt, msgLen)
        INTEGER : i, at, textStart

        kDirected =: outBuf(0)
        0 =: outBuf(1)
        2 =: at
        putQualSlot(mySystem, fromSlot, at) =: at
        at - 2 =: outBuf(1)

        0 =: outBuf(at)
        0 =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at
        at =: textStart

        % CLAMPED. The text came from a client and outBuf is 256 bytes; the
        % qualified sender has already taken some of it. PLANC checks no bound,
        % so a long enough message would write straight past the end.
        FOR i IN 1:msgLen DO
            IF at < 250 THEN
                inBuf(msgAt + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
                at + 1 =: at
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        at - textStart =: outBuf(textStart - 1)
        sendTo(memberMagic(toSlot), at)
    ENDROUTINE

    % SEND IT ACROSS A TRUNK.
    %
    % Same six-byte header as a relayed room line, so the far end can
    % de-duplicate it with the machinery that already exists. The text is
    % TARGET/message where a room line carries ROOM/message, which leaves the
    % name field for the SENDER - the one thing the far end has to display.
    %
    % THE TARGET GOES OUT UNQUALIFIED. The letter is addressed to that machine
    % already, so repeating the machine inside the text would only give the two
    % copies a chance to disagree.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, &
                           INTEGER) : &
            sendDirTrunk(fromSlot, toSys, aliasAt, aliasLen, msgAt, msgLen)
        INTEGER : j, at, st, textStart

        % STAMPED HERE, exactly as forwardSaid stamps a room line and for the
        % same reason: the number has to come from the ORIGIN, or a machine two
        % hops out cannot tell one message arriving twice from two messages.
        myLineId + 1 =: myLineId
        IF myLineId > 32000 THEN
            1 =: myLineId
        ENDIF

        kTrkDirect =: outBuf(0)
        mySystem / 256 =: outBuf(1)
        mySystem MOD 256 =: outBuf(2)
        maxHops - 1 =: outBuf(3)
        myLineId / 256 =: outBuf(4)
        myLineId MOD 256 =: outBuf(5)
        memberLen(fromSlot) =: outBuf(6)
        7 =: at
        FOR j IN 1:memberLen(fromSlot) DO
            memberName(fromSlot, j) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR

        0 =: outBuf(at)
        0 =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at
        at =: textStart

        FOR j IN 1:aliasLen DO
            inBuf(aliasAt + j - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR
        slashByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
        at + 1 =: at

        FOR j IN 1:msgLen DO
            IF at < 250 THEN
                inBuf(msgAt + j - 1) =: outBuf(at)
                at + 1 =: at
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        at - textStart =: outBuf(textStart - 1)
        sendToName(toSys, at) =: st
        st RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % =======================================================================
    % A DIRECT MESSAGE ARRIVES FROM A CLIENT
    %
    % The text is TARGET/message. Everything that can go wrong is answered with
    % kDirBad and a sentence, because the person who typed it is the only one
    % who can put it right and they cannot see this machine.
    % =======================================================================
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            handleDirect(slot, textAt, textLength)
        INTEGER : cut, tgtAt, tgtLen, msgAt, msgLen
        INTEGER : bang, sysNum, aliasAt, aliasLen, row, st

        findSlash(textAt, textLength) =: cut
        IF cut < 1 THEN
            sendDirBad(memberMagic(slot), textAt, 0, &
                'a direct message is NAME/text - a name, a slash, then what to say')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        textAt =: tgtAt
        cut =: tgtLen
        textAt + cut + 1 =: msgAt
        textLength - cut - 1 =: msgLen
        IF msgLen < 1 THEN
            sendDirBad(memberMagic(slot), tgtAt, tgtLen, &
                'there was nothing after the slash to send')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % Qualified by machine, or a bare alias?
        findBang(tgtAt, tgtLen) =: bang
        IF bang < 0 THEN
            tgtAt =: aliasAt
            tgtLen =: aliasLen
            0 =: sysNum
        ELSE
            machineNum(tgtAt, bang) =: sysNum
            tgtAt + bang + 1 =: aliasAt
            tgtLen - bang - 1 =: aliasLen
            IF sysNum = 0 THEN
                sendDirBad(memberMagic(slot), tgtAt, tgtLen, &
                    'that machine has no number in it - try D102!RONNY')
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            IF aliasLen < 1 THEN
                sendDirBad(memberMagic(slot), tgtAt, tgtLen, &
                    'there is a machine but no name after it - try D102!RONNY')
                RETURN
            ENDIF
        ENDIF

        resolveTo(aliasAt, aliasLen, sysNum)

        IF dirCount = 0 THEN
            % NOT A QUEUE. There is deliberately no store and forward: a
            % message that waits for somebody is a mailbox, a different product
            % with different rules, and SINTRAN already has mail for that.
            sendDirBad(memberMagic(slot), tgtAt, tgtLen, &
                'nobody of that name is here now, and nothing is kept for later')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF dirCount > 1 THEN
            % NEVER GUESSED AT. Two machines can each hold a RONNY long before
            % they are ever trunked together, so no uniqueness rule could have
            % been applied even in principle - and a private message delivered
            % to the wrong person is a privacy failure, not an inconvenience.
            sendDirAmbig(memberMagic(slot), aliasAt, aliasLen)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF dirLocal > 0 THEN
            deliverLocal(slot, dirLocal, msgAt, msgLen)
            sendDirSent(memberMagic(slot), mySystem, aliasAt, aliasLen)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % ---- ON ANOTHER MACHINE -------------------------------------------
        %
        % ONE HOP ONLY, AND THAT IS A DELIBERATE FIRST CUT.
        %
        % A room line is FLOODED to every peer and de-duplicated at the far
        % end. A private message must NOT be: flooding one hands it to machines
        % that have no business seeing it, which is a privacy failure dressed
        % up as routing. So this sends only to the machine that holds the
        % person, and refuses when that machine is not a direct peer.
        %
        % On the D102 - D100 - D103 chain that means D100 reaches both ends and
        % each end reaches only D100. THAT LIMIT IS REAL AND IT IS VISIBLE: the
        % sender is told in words rather than the message quietly vanishing.
        % Routing onward by the target machine - each hop forwarding to the
        % peer that matches - is the next step, and it needs a way to carry a
        % refusal back, which no kind does yet.
        findPeer(dirSystem) =: row
        IF row = 0 THEN
            sendDirBad(memberMagic(slot), tgtAt, tgtLen, &
                'they are on a machine this one has no trunk to')
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        IF peerState(row) >< peerUp THEN
            sendDirBad(memberMagic(slot), tgtAt, tgtLen, &
                'the trunk to their machine is down just now')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        sendDirTrunk(slot, dirSystem, aliasAt, aliasLen, msgAt, msgLen) =: st
        sendDirSent(memberMagic(slot), dirSystem, aliasAt, aliasLen)
    ENDROUTINE

    % A DIRECT MESSAGE THAT ARRIVED OVER A TRUNK, addressed to somebody here.
    %
    % The text is TARGET/message with the target UNQUALIFIED - the letter was
    % addressed to this machine, so the machine is not repeated inside it.
    %
    % THE SENDER IS QUALIFIED WITH THE ORIGIN, not with the machine the letter
    % came from. On a relayed message those are different, and naming the relay
    % would tell the reader to type a reply to the wrong place. The origin
    % travels in the header for exactly this reason.
    %
    % NOT FOUND HERE MEANS DROPPED, and that is worth being honest about: the
    % sender was already told DirectSent by their own server, so a person who
    % logged out in the second between the two ends disagreeing loses the
    % message with nobody told. Closing that needs a refusal that can travel
    % back over a trunk, and no kind carries one yet.
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            takeRemDirect(origSys, nameAt, nameLength, textAt, textLength)
        INTEGER : cut, tgtAt, tgtLen, msgAt, msgLen, i, at, textStart

        findSlash(textAt, textLength) =: cut
        IF cut < 1 THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        textAt =: tgtAt
        cut =: tgtLen
        textAt + cut + 1 =: msgAt
        textLength - cut - 1 =: msgLen
        IF msgLen < 1 THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % THIS MACHINE ONLY. Asking for any machine would find the sender's own
        % entry in our remote table and send the message straight back where it
        % came from.
        resolveTo(tgtAt, tgtLen, mySystem)
        IF dirLocal = 0 THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        kDirected =: outBuf(0)
        0 =: outBuf(1)
        2 =: at
        putQualIn(origSys, nameAt, nameLength, at) =: at
        at - 2 =: outBuf(1)

        0 =: outBuf(at)
        0 =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at
        at =: textStart

        FOR i IN 1:msgLen DO
            IF at < 250 THEN
                inBuf(msgAt + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
                at + 1 =: at
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        at - textStart =: outBuf(textStart - 1)
        sendTo(memberMagic(dirLocal), at)
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : peerTick
        INTEGER : i, st

        FOR i IN 1:maxPeers DO
            IF peerSystem(i) >< 0 THEN

                peerHeard(i) + 1 =: peerHeard(i)

                % ---- has a peer we had gone quiet? -------------------------
                IF peerState(i) = peerUp THEN
                    IF peerHeard(i) > peerDeadAfter THEN
                        peerDown =: peerState(i)
                        forgetRemote(peerSystem(i))
                        peerFirstWait =: peerWait(i)
                        peerFirstWait =: peerCount(i)
                        logLine('SV trunk down sys ', 18, peerSystem(i))
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF

                % ---- and greet anything that is not up ---------------------
                IF peerState(i) >< peerUp THEN
                    peerCount(i) - 1 =: peerCount(i)
                    IF peerCount(i) <= 0 THEN
                        sendHello(peerSystem(i), trkAsk) =: st

                        % DOUBLE, THEN CAP. The multiply is done before the
                        % test so the cap is a ceiling on the WAIT, not on the
                        % number of tries - a peer away for a week keeps being
                        % greeted every five minutes for ever, which is what
                        % makes it come back by itself.
                        peerWait(i) * 2 =: peerWait(i)
                        IF peerWait(i) > peerMaxWait THEN
                            peerMaxWait =: peerWait(i)
                        ENDIF
                        peerWait(i) =: peerCount(i)
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Ask one peer who is on it. Empty text - the kind IS the question.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER) : askWho(system)
        INTEGER : at, st

        kTrkWho =: outBuf(0)
        0 =: outBuf(1)                        % no name: a MACHINE is asking
        2 =: at
        0 =: outBuf(at)
        0 =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at

        sendToName(system, at) =: st
        st RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Mark a peer heard-from. Called for ANY arrival on the trunk port.
    %
    % AN UNKNOWN SYSTEM IS ADDED, not ignored. If a machine trunks to us we want
    % the trunk to work in both directions without an operator having to type the
    % matching TRUNK here - the design's "whichever machine boots second brings
    % the trunk up". If the table is full the greeting is simply dropped; a full
    % peer table is an operator problem, not something to fail a message over.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER) : markPeerUp(system)
        INTEGER : row, i

        % OURSELVES IS NOT A PEER. A hello to a machine with no CHAT-TRUNK name
        % bounces back to this very port wearing our own magic, and without this
        % the server adds a row for itself and reports it up. Measured the first
        % time a trunk was configured - see the note on mySystem.
        IF system = mySystem THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        IF system = 0 THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        findPeer(system) =: row
        IF row = 0 THEN
            FOR i IN 1:maxPeers DO
                IF peerSystem(i) = 0 THEN
                    IF row = 0 THEN
                        system =: peerSystem(i)
                        i =: row
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDFOR
        ENDIF
        IF row >< 0 THEN
            peerUp =: peerState(row)
            0 =: peerHeard(row)

            % RESET THE BACKOFF ON EVERY TRANSITION TO UP, not just the first.
            % A peer that flaps would otherwise inherit a five-minute wait from
            % its last absence and take five minutes to be missed again.
            peerFirstWait =: peerWait(row)
            peerFirstWait =: peerCount(row)

            logLine('SV trunk up sys   ', 18, system)
        ENDIF
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % TRUNK <system>: remember the peer and greet it.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER) : addPeer(system)
        INTEGER : row, i, length, st

        findPeer(system) =: row
        IF row = 0 THEN
            FOR i IN 1:maxPeers DO
                IF peerSystem(i) = 0 THEN
                    IF row = 0 THEN
                        system =: peerSystem(i)
                        peerUnknown =: peerState(i)
                        0 =: peerHeard(i)
                        % Start the backoff clock, or peerTick would count down
                        % from an uninitialised value and greet at random.
                        peerFirstWait =: peerWait(i)
                        peerFirstWait =: peerCount(i)
                        i =: row
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDFOR
        ENDIF

        IF row = 0 THEN
            buildAdmText('no room for another trunk', 25) =: length
            sendTo(senderMagic, length)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % Greet it now rather than waiting for the retry tick, so an operator
        % typing TRUNK sees the answer in seconds and not in half a minute.
        % A FAILURE HERE IS NOT REPORTED AS ONE - the peer being off is exactly
        % what the retry exists for.
        sendHello(system, trkAsk) =: st

        buildAdmText('trunk added', 11) =: length
        sendTo(senderMagic, length)
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % UNTRUNK <system>: forget the peer.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER) : dropPeer(system)
        INTEGER : row, length

        findPeer(system) =: row
        IF row = 0 THEN
            buildAdmText('no such trunk', 13) =: length
            sendTo(senderMagic, length)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        0 =: peerSystem(row)
        peerUnknown =: peerState(row)
        0 =: peerHeard(row)

        % AND ITS PEOPLE GO WITH IT. Leaving them behind would show somebody
        % chatting from a machine this server no longer talks to, and nothing
        % would ever come along to remove them.
        forgetRemote(system)

        buildAdmText('trunk removed', 13) =: length
        sendTo(senderMagic, length)
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Refuse the sender, echoing the name they asked for and naming the reason.
    %
    % The name is echoed so the asker can tell WHICH request failed when it has
    % more than one outstanding; the reason is carried so the wording is the
    % same on both doors.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, BYTES, INTEGER) : &
            refuse(nameAt, nameLength, reason, reasonLength)
        INTEGER : i, at

        % EVERY refusal passes through here - seven call sites do, and that is
        % why the count belongs here rather than at any of them. A join that
        % fails used to leave no trace at all: the client saw a reject and the
        % machine said nothing, so "it will not let me in" could not be told
        % from "it never heard me". STATUS answers that now.
        nRefused + 1 =: nRefused

        kReject =: outBuf(0)
        nameLength =: outBuf(1)
        2 =: at
        FOR i IN 1:nameLength DO
            inBuf(nameAt + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR

        0 =: outBuf(at)
        reasonLength =: outBuf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at
        % SUBSCRIPT FROM ZERO. A BYTES declared from a literal - which is what
        % every reason string above is - has its first character at index 0, so
        % "reason(i)" for i in 1:len silently drops the first letter and reads
        % one byte past the end. Same defect, same day, in CHAT.PLNC's buildOwn,
        % where it turned RONNY into ONNY on the wire and was only visible
        % because the raw bytes were dumped. Nothing warns; PLANC checks no
        % bound. Compare cmdIs in CHAT.PLNC, which has always used word(i - 1).
        FOR i IN 1:reasonLength DO
            reason(i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
            at + 1 =: at
        ENDFOR

        sendTo(senderMagic, at)
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % Decode one arrived message and apply the room rules.
    %
    % A transcription of Xmsg.Chat/ChatRoom.cs. Where that file refuses, this
    % refuses for the same reason and in the same order, because the two doors
    % lead into one room.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, VOID : handleMessage
        INTEGER : kind, nameLength, nameAt, textLength, textAt
        INTEGER : slot, taken, length
        % i is used by the topic copy, which is the only loop in this routine.
        INTEGER : i
        % The room's entry in the topic/history table. Named for what it is
        % used for here rather than for the table it comes from - the table is
        % called "topic" only because that was its first job.
        INTEGER : histSlot
        % Where the space between "room" and "count" was found in INITIALIZE.
        INTEGER : initSpace
        % A relayed line carries these three bytes in front of the usual shape.
        % Zero on every other kind, and zero is not a real system number, so a
        % handler that reads them by mistake gets an obviously wrong answer
        % rather than a plausible one.
        INTEGER : relayOrig
        INTEGER : relayHops
        % The number the ORIGIN gave this line, on a kind 53. Zero on
        % anything else, and zero is never a real line number - the
        % stamper starts at 1 - so a handler that reads it by mistake
        % gets an obviously wrong answer rather than a plausible one.
        INTEGER : relayId

        inBuf(0) =: kind

        % ---- A RELAYED LINE HAS A LONGER HEADER, so take it off HERE --------
        %
        % Every field below this point is read at a fixed offset - nameLength
        % at byte 1, the name at byte 2 - and a dozen handlers depend on that.
        % Rather than teach all of them about a second layout, the three extra
        % bytes are read off and the rest of the message is SLID DOWN to where
        % it would have been. From the next line on, a relayed line looks
        % exactly like any other and only its own handler knows the difference.
        %
        % The length test is not decoration: readLength is what actually
        % arrived, and a truncated relay header would otherwise be read as a
        % name length and produce a plausible-looking message out of rubbish.
        0 =: relayOrig
        0 =: relayHops
        0 =: relayId
        IF kind = kTrkRelay THEN
            IF readLength > 4 THEN
                inBuf(1) * 256 + inBuf(2) =: relayOrig
                inBuf(3) =: relayHops
                FOR i IN 1:readLength - 4 DO
                    inBuf(i + 3) =: inBuf(i)
                ENDFOR
                readLength - 3 =: readLength
            ELSE
                % Too short to be one. Drop it rather than read past the end.
                RETURN
            ENDIF
        ENDIF

        % SAME TRICK, TWO BYTES LONGER. The length test is not decoration:
        % a kind 53 cut short would otherwise have the high byte of its id
        % read as a name length, and a plausible-looking line built out of
        % rubbish. Note it needs SIX, not four - a message long enough to
        % satisfy the kind 52 test above can still be too short for this.
        % kTrkDirect CARRIES THE SAME SIX-BYTE HEADER, so it is stripped by
        % the same code rather than by a copy that could drift from it. What
        % differs is only what the text holds - TARGET/message here, and
        % ROOM/message for a room line - which its own handler deals with.
        IF kind = kTrkRelId OR kind = kTrkDirect THEN
            IF readLength > 6 THEN
                inBuf(1) * 256 + inBuf(2) =: relayOrig
                inBuf(3) =: relayHops
                % A peer that sent a high byte over 127 would overflow a
                % signed 16-bit INTEGER and land here NEGATIVE. That is ugly
                % but harmless: this number is only ever compared for
                % equality, never used as a size or an index, so a negative
                % one still names the line perfectly well. Our own stamper
                % never goes above 32000, so it does not arise between our
                % own machines.
                inBuf(4) * 256 + inBuf(5) =: relayId
                FOR i IN 1:readLength - 6 DO
                    inBuf(i + 5) =: inBuf(i)
                ENDFOR
                readLength - 5 =: readLength
            ELSE
                RETURN
            ENDIF
        ENDIF

        inBuf(1) =: nameLength
        2 =: nameAt

        % A "parse k= nlen= rlen=" line printed here on EVERY message while the
        % join was being brought up. It said "remove once the join works", and
        % the join works - it was what showed the kind arriving as the XSLET
        % serial number instead of kJoin, which is how the missing header skip
        % was found.
        %
        % THE THREE "DROP" MESSAGES BELOW STAY. They are not the same thing: a
        % silent drop is right for a stranger on the wire but leaves the operator
        % unable to tell a refused message from a lost one, and each prints only
        % when something is actually wrong rather than once per message.

        % ---- TRUST NOTHING THAT ARRIVED ------------------------------------
        % Every field below is a byte a stranger chose. Two things have to hold
        % before any of them is used, and neither is checked by the language:
        %
        %   the name must FIT      memberName has maxNameLen bytes per seat, and
        %                          a length byte can say 255.
        %   the fields must be IN  the message actually read is readLength
        %   THE MESSAGE            bytes; a length that points past it would read
        %                          whatever the previous, longer message left in
        %                          inBuf and answer with it.
        %
        % Dropped in silence rather than refused: a refusal would need a name to
        % echo, and the name is the part that is not trustworthy. The seat is
        % settled by the receive loop either way, which is exactly why that
        % accounting does not live in here.
        %
        % The sixteen is a rule of the ROOM, not a detail of this program:
        % ChatRoom.MaxNicknameLength says the same number, so a name this side
        % must drop is one the other side refuses rather than admits. Otherwise
        % the two rooms would disagree about who is in them.
        IF nameLength > maxNameLen THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL21', 'SV: DROP name too big')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF
        IF nameAt + nameLength + 1 > readLength THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL22', 'SV: DROP name past end')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        nameAt + nameLength =: textAt
        inBuf(textAt) * 256 + inBuf(textAt + 1) =: textLength
        textAt + 2 =: textAt

        IF textAt + textLength > readLength THEN
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL22', 'SV: DROP text past end')
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % ---- ONE VOCABULARY IS NOT ONE AUTHORITY ---------------------------
        %
        % Admin kinds share the room's numbering on purpose - a second enum
        % would mean a second decoder bound, and that bound has been left
        % behind twice, silently both times. But sharing the words is not
        % sharing the right to use them anywhere: an admin kind arriving on
        % CHAT-LOBBY would let any client that can reach the room stop the
        % server, and a room kind on the command port would put a member in a
        % seat nobody counted.
        %
        % Refused in SILENCE rather than with a reason. A caller on the wrong
        % port is either confused or probing, and neither is owed a map.
        % THREE PORTS, THREE VOCABULARIES. Each kind belongs on exactly one of
        % them, and arriving on the wrong one is refused in silence.
        %
        %   48 and up   TRUNK    machine to machine, on CHAT-TRUNK
        %   32 .. 47    ADMIN    an operator, on CHAT-ADMIN
        %   below 32    ROOM     a member, on the room port
        %
        % The trunk test comes FIRST because trunk numbers are also >= kAdmStat,
        % and testing admin first would demand fromAdmin of a HELLO - which
        % arrives on the trunk port, where fromAdmin is false. That is exactly
        % how the first build of this swallowed every hello in silence.
        IF kind >= kTrkHello THEN
            IF NOT fromTrunk THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF
        ELSE
            IF kind >= kAdmStat THEN
                IF NOT fromAdmin THEN
                    RETURN
                ENDIF
            ELSE
                IF fromAdmin THEN
                    RETURN
                ENDIF
                IF fromTrunk THEN
                    RETURN
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDIF

        % ---- THE ADMIN COMMANDS --------------------------------------------
        % ---- TRUNK CONFIGURATION, from CHAT-MON on the command port ----------
        %
        % Guarded by fromAdmin like every other admin verb: rewiring the network
        % is not something a room member may do.
        IF kind = kAdmTrunk THEN
            IF NOT fromAdmin THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            trunkNumber(textAt, textLength) =: taken
            IF taken = 0 THEN
                buildAdmText('TRUNK needs a system number', 27) =: length
                sendTo(senderMagic, length)
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            % NO "=: length" - addPeer is a VOID routine and sends its own
            % reply. Assigning from it drew ILLEGAL DATA TYPE "ADDPEER" at the
            % CALL, which reads like a problem with the argument.
            addPeer(taken)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % INITIALIZE <room> <n> - how many messages a room remembers.
        %
        % TWO FIELDS, unlike every other admin verb, so the text is split at the
        % first space: the room name, then the count. Split at the FIRST one
        % because a room name cannot contain a space and a trailing one would
        % otherwise become part of the number.
        %
        % REFUSED ONCE THE ROOM HAS HISTORY, and this is the part worth being
        % careful about. The ring is "block = id MOD size", so CHANGING size
        % moves where every existing id lands: the messages do not move, but
        % the arithmetic that finds them does, and the replay would hand back
        % other messages in the wrong order while looking perfectly healthy.
        % There is no cheap correct rewrite - it means reading the whole ring
        % out and writing it back in the new shape - so an empty room is the
        % only case allowed, and the refusal says exactly that. Setting a room
        % up BEFORE anybody speaks in it is the case this was asked for.
        IF kind = kAdmInit THEN
            IF NOT fromAdmin THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            % Find the space that separates the two fields.
            0 =: initSpace
            FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
                IF initSpace = 0 THEN
                    IF inBuf(textAt + i - 1) = spaceByte(0) THEN
                        i =: initSpace
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDFOR

            % ---- NO ROOM NAMED: SET THIS MACHINE'S DEFAULT ------------------
            %
            % Ronny, 2026-08-23: "INITIALIZE without room sets the default
            % amount for this machine, spread out by rooms as the rooms may be
            % created later."
            %
            % So one field is a DEFAULT and two are one ROOM. It reads the way
            % an operator would say it - "INITIALIZE 20" is how much a room
            % keeps here, "INITIALIZE LOBBY 20" is how much LOBBY keeps.
            %
            % IT DOES NOT TOUCH ROOMS THAT ALREADY EXIST, and that is the whole
            % meaning of "as the rooms may be created later": claimTopic and
            % claimRoomNamed take a copy of histDefault when they allocate, so
            % a room created after this picks the new number up and one created
            % before it keeps what it was given. Reaching back into live rooms
            % would change a ring that already has messages in it, which is the
            % thing the per-room form refuses to do for good reason.
            IF initSpace = 0 THEN
                trunkNumber(textAt, textLength) =: taken
                IF taken < 1 THEN
                    buildAdmText('INITIALIZE needs a count above zero', 35) &
                        =: length
                    sendTo(senderMagic, length)
                    RETURN
                ENDIF
                IF taken > maxHistSize THEN
                    buildAdmText('that is more than a room can keep', 33) &
                        =: length
                    sendTo(senderMagic, length)
                    RETURN
                ENDIF
                taken =: histDefault
                buildAdmText('default history size set', 24) =: length
                sendTo(senderMagic, length)
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            trunkNumber(textAt + initSpace, textLength - initSpace) =: taken
            IF taken < 1 THEN
                buildAdmText('INITIALIZE needs a count above zero', 35) &
                    =: length
                sendTo(senderMagic, length)
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            IF taken > maxHistSize THEN
                buildAdmText('that is more than a room can keep', 33) =: length
                sendTo(senderMagic, length)
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            claimRoomNamed(textAt, initSpace - 1) =: histSlot
            IF histSlot = 0 THEN
                buildAdmText('no room left in the table', 25) =: length
                sendTo(senderMagic, length)
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            IF histNext(histSlot) > 0 THEN
                buildAdmText('that room already has history', 29) =: length
                sendTo(senderMagic, length)
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            taken =: histSize(histSlot)
            buildAdmText('room history size set', 21) =: length
            sendTo(senderMagic, length)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kAdmUntrnk THEN
            IF NOT fromAdmin THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            trunkNumber(textAt, textLength) =: taken
            IF taken = 0 THEN
                buildAdmText('UNTRUNK needs a system number', 29) =: length
                sendTo(senderMagic, length)
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            dropPeer(taken)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kAdmTrunks THEN
            IF NOT fromAdmin THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            buildTrunks =: length
            sendTo(senderMagic, length)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % ---- A HELLO FROM ANOTHER SERVER, on the trunk port -----------------
        %
        % ANYTHING ARRIVING FROM A PEER MARKS IT UP, including an unsolicited
        % HELLO from a machine we have not trunked. That is what lets whichever
        % machine boots second bring the trunk up without the first noticing.
        %
        % An ASK is answered, an ANSWER is not - see the note on trkAsk. Without
        % that the two servers would greet each other for ever.
        IF kind = kTrkHello THEN
            IF NOT fromTrunk THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            % A RETURNED LETTER IS NOT A PEER SAYING HELLO - IT IS THE OPPOSITE.
            %
            % XMTRE means the message came BACK: "If msgType is XMTRE,
            % msgLengthOrStat contains the error status". The design counts on
            % exactly this as the CHEAP way to learn a peer is absent - "the
            % letter comes straight back undeliverable ... Immediate, no
            % waiting" - so reading it as proof of life inverts the one signal
            % that costs nothing.
            %
            % The bounce does NOT say who it was addressed to, so there is
            % nobody specific to mark down here; it is simply not treated as
            % good news, and the peer stays whatever it was until the idle
            % count declares it down. Carrying the addressee in the hello text
            % would let this mark the right row immediately - worth doing when
            % the state machine grows.
            IF msgType = XMTRE THEN
                logLine('SV trunk bounce   ', 18, receiveStat)
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            markPeerUp(senderSystem)
            IF textLength > 0 THEN
                IF inBuf(textAt) = trkAsk THEN
                    sendHello(senderSystem, trkAnswer) =: taken
                ENDIF
            ENDIF

            % PHASE C: A HELLO IS ALSO THE MOMENT TO ASK WHO IS THERE.
            %
            % Asked on EVERY hello, not only the first. A peer that says hello
            % again has very likely restarted, and everything we remember about
            % its members is then stale - the design's "treat Up as start
            % again". The answer REPLACES our rows for it, so asking twice
            % costs one message and can never leave a ghost.
            askWho(senderSystem) =: taken
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % ---- A PEER ASKS WHO IS ON US ---------------------------------------
        IF kind = kTrkWho THEN
            IF NOT fromTrunk THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            IF msgType = XMTRE THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            buildMembers =: length
            sendToName(senderSystem, length) =: taken
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % ---- SOMEBODY SPOKE ON ANOTHER MACHINE ------------------------------
        %
        % The text is "ROOM/what they said". The room decides who hears it here;
        % the speaker is shown qualified with the system the LETTER came from,
        % never with anything the sender put in the message.
        IF kind = kTrkSaid THEN
            IF NOT fromTrunk THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            IF msgType = XMTRE THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            markPeerUp(senderSystem)
            deliverRemoteSaid(senderSystem, nameAt, nameLength, &
                textAt, textLength)

            % AND PASS IT ON. This is what makes a chain work without changing
            % what the SENDER does at all: the machine in the middle turns a
            % direct line into a relayed one. An older server simply does not
            % do this, which is why nothing had to be upgraded in step.
            %
            % The origin is senderSystem because this line came STRAIGHT from
            % the machine it was typed on - one hop so far.
            % Id ZERO: kind 51 carries none, because it comes from a server
            % that predates dedup. Passing zero on is honest - it says "this
            % line has no name" - and the receiver treats an unnamed line as
            % always new, which is exactly today's behaviour. Inventing a
            % number here would be worse than useless: a second relay would
            % invent a different one for the same line.
            relayOn(senderSystem, senderSystem, 0, maxHops - 1, &
                nameAt, nameLength, textAt, textLength)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % ---- SOMEBODY SPOKE FURTHER AWAY ------------------------------------
        %
        % A line that has already been through at least one other machine. The
        % speaker is qualified with the ORIGIN THE LETTER CARRIES, not with the
        % system it arrived from - that one is the relay, and using it would
        % show a D103 user as being on D100.
        IF kind = kTrkRelay THEN
            IF NOT fromTrunk THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            IF msgType = XMTRE THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            % The machine that PASSED it to us is alive; that is what we heard
            % from. Says nothing about the origin, which we may have no trunk
            % to at all - and that is the entire point of relaying.
            markPeerUp(senderSystem)

            deliverRemoteSaid(relayOrig, nameAt, nameLength, &
                textAt, textLength)

            % On again, one hop shorter, never back the way it came.
            relayOn(senderSystem, relayOrig, 0, relayHops - 1, &
                nameAt, nameLength, textAt, textLength)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % ---- SOMEBODY SPOKE FURTHER AWAY, AND THE LINE HAS A NAME ------------
        %
        % Same as kTrkRelay above, with the one difference that matters: this
        % line carries the number its ORIGIN gave it, so we can tell a second
        % copy from a new line and show it once.
        IF kind = kTrkRelId THEN
            IF NOT fromTrunk THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            IF msgType = XMTRE THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            % The machine that PASSED it on is alive. Says nothing about the
            % origin, which we may have no trunk to at all.
            markPeerUp(senderSystem)

            % SEEN IT ALREADY? Then stop here - do not show it and do not pass
            % it on. Passing on a repeat would multiply it round a mesh even
            % though nobody would be shown it twice.
            IF lineIsNew(relayOrig, relayId) = 0 THEN
                nDupes + 1 =: nDupes
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            deliverRemoteSaid(relayOrig, nameAt, nameLength, &
                textAt, textLength)

            % On again, one hop shorter, never back the way it came, and with
            % the id UNCHANGED.
            relayOn(senderSystem, relayOrig, relayId, relayHops - 1, &
                nameAt, nameLength, textAt, textLength)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % ---- A PEER TELLS US WHO IS ON IT -----------------------------------
        % A DIRECT MESSAGE FROM ANOTHER MACHINE.
        %
        % Deduplicated by the same window as a relayed room line, because it
        % carries the same origin and id. NOT RELAYED ONWARD: a room line is
        % passed to every peer and sorted out at the far end, but doing that
        % with a private message would hand it to machines that have no
        % business seeing it. It arrived here because it was addressed here.
        IF kind = kTrkDirect THEN
            IF NOT fromTrunk THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            IF msgType = XMTRE THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            markPeerUp(senderSystem)

            IF lineIsNew(relayOrig, relayId) = 0 THEN
                nDupes + 1 =: nDupes
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            takeRemDirect(relayOrig, nameAt, nameLength, &
                textAt, textLength)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kTrkMbrs THEN
            IF NOT fromTrunk THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            IF msgType = XMTRE THEN
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            % Hearing from it is itself proof of life.
            markPeerUp(senderSystem)
            takeMembers(senderSystem, textAt, textLength)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kAdmStat THEN
            buildStatus =: length
            sendTo(senderMagic, length)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kAdmStop THEN
            % Say who did it. An unattributed stop in a log is worth little,
            % and the asker's name rode in the nickname field for this.
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL19', 'CHATSV: stopped by ')
            showName(nameAt, nameLength)
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            TRUE =: stopWanted
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % The seat this sender already holds, or -1 if none. A "slot now=" line
        % used to print here on every message; it was part of the same bring-up
        % as the parse line above and went with it.
        findByMagic(senderMagic) =: slot

        IF kind = kJoin THEN
            % Order matches ChatRoom.TryJoin: empty name, then taken name, then
            % already joined.
            IF nameLength = 0 THEN
                refuse(nameAt, nameLength, reasonNeedNick, lenNeedNick)
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            findByName(nameAt, nameLength) =: taken
            IF taken >< 0 THEN
                refuse(nameAt, nameLength, reasonNickTaken, lenNickTaken)
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            IF slot >< 0 THEN
                refuse(nameAt, nameLength, reasonAlreadyIn, lenAlreadyIn)
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            freeSlot =: slot
            IF slot = 0 THEN
                % XROUT should have stopped this - the seat count it holds and
                % maxSeats here are the same number. If it ever happens, the
                % two have drifted apart and that is worth refusing loudly
                % rather than overwriting a member.
                refuse(nameAt, nameLength, reasonRoomFull, lenRoomFull)
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            1 =: memberUsed(slot)
            senderMagic =: memberMagic(slot)
            copyNameIn(slot, nameAt, nameLength)

            % AND WHICH ROOM. The Join carries it in the TEXT field,
            % because with one port the room can no longer be told from
            % which port the message arrived on. Everything this member
            % says afterwards is broadcast only to the others in it.
            copyRoomIn(slot, textAt, textLength)

            % The welcome goes only to the joiner; the room hears Joined.
            buildMessage(kWelcome, nameAt, nameLength, 0, 0) =: length
            sendTo(senderMagic, length)

            % ---- WHAT THEY MISSED, BEFORE THE ROOM HEARS THEY ARRIVED -----
            %
            % ORDER MATTERS AND THIS IS THE RIGHT ONE. After the welcome,
            % because the client is not in the room until it has that and
            % would drop anything sent before it. Before the Joined broadcast,
            % so the transcript reads the way it happened: everything said
            % earlier, then "OLAV joined", then whatever is said next. Sending
            % history after the announcement would put old lines below the
            % arrival and read as though they were new.
            %
            % findTopic, NOT claimTopic - joining a room must not allocate a
            % history slot. A room nobody has ever spoken in has no history to
            % replay, and giving it a table entry on a join would let eight
            % people joining eight empty rooms fill the table with nothing.
            findTopic(slot) =: histSlot
            histReplay(histSlot, senderMagic)

            buildMessage(kJoined, nameAt, nameLength, 0, 0) =: length
            broadcast(length, slot, slot)        % their room, not them
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % ---- THE TWO THINGS A PERSON WITH NO SEAT MAY ASK ------------------
        %
        % Both are READ-ONLY and both are what somebody actually needs at the
        % moment they have no seat: they have just left, or been refused a
        % nickname, and the next question is always "what is there" or "who is
        % about". Answering silence to that is the client's worst behaviour.
        %
        % kRooms WAS SUPPOSED TO BE ALLOWED HERE ALREADY, and was not.
        % DOC/protocols/chat-wire.json has said since it was written:
        %
        %   "Allowed while UNJOINED, unlike Who: 'what rooms are there' is
        %    exactly what somebody asks after being refused a name, and the
        %    server needs no seat to answer it."
        %
        % The handler sat BELOW the membership guard, so /list from somebody
        % with no seat was dropped without a word. The registry described the
        % intention and the code never matched it - found 2026-08-22 by reading
        % the registry entry while building the /who case beside it.
        %
        % THE GUARD'S REASON STILL STANDS FOR EVERYTHING ELSE. Silence for a
        % stranger is deliberate: answering confirms the port is a chat server
        % to something that never joined. These two answers give up exactly
        % that one fact and nothing else - no seat is spent, no state changes,
        % and a roster is not a secret in a chat for one building. Saying and
        % joining and renaming stay behind the guard.
        IF kind = kRooms THEN
            buildRooms =: length
            sendTo(senderMagic, length)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % /map belongs here for the same reason /list does: it is READ-ONLY,
        % costs no seat, and is exactly what somebody asks when they cannot see
        % anyone - "is the other machine even there?". Refusing it to a person
        % with no seat would refuse it at the one moment it explains something.
        IF kind = kMap THEN
            buildMap =: length
            sendTo(senderMagic, length)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kWho THEN
            IF slot = 0 THEN
                % No seat means no room to filter by, so the answer is the
                % whole house. A DIFFERENT KIND, because the client labels a
                % kWho answer with the room it thinks it is in.
                buildAllWho =: length
                sendTo(senderMagic, length)
                RETURN
            ENDIF
        ENDIF

        IF slot = 0 THEN
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kSay THEN
            % The speaker is named by the ROOM, never by what the sender put in
            % the message - otherwise anyone could speak as anyone.
            buildFromSlot(kSaid, slot, textAt, textLength) =: length
            broadcast(length, slot, 0)            % their room, speaker too

            % PHASE D: AND ON TO THE OTHER MACHINES.
            %
            % AFTER the local broadcast, deliberately. The people in the room
            % here are the ones waiting on this keystroke; a peer that is slow
            % or absent must not delay them. It also means a forward that fails
            % costs the local room nothing.
            %
            % forwardSaid reads inBuf, which still holds the arrival - the local
            % broadcast above only wrote outBuf.
            forwardSaid(slot, textAt, textLength)

            % ---- AND REMEMBER IT ----------------------------------------
            %
            % LAST, after the room and the peers have both been served, for
            % the same reason the forward goes after the broadcast: the people
            % waiting on this keystroke come first, and a disk write must never
            % sit between somebody pressing return and the room hearing it.
            %
            % claimTopic, despite the name - it is the room table, and history
            % shares its slots. It allocates on first use, so the file for a
            % room is created the first time anybody speaks in it rather than
            % when the room is invented, and a room nobody ever speaks in costs
            % nothing at all.
            %
            % A FULL TABLE RETURNS 0 AND histSave DOES NOTHING. Nine rooms
            % means the ninth keeps no history; it does not lose its messages.
            claimTopic(slot) =: histSlot
            histSave(histSlot, slot, textAt, textLength)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % A DIRECT MESSAGE. A SEAT IS REQUIRED - not because the target needs
        % to be in a room, they do not, but because the SENDER has to have a
        % name for the message to be from. This sits below the slot = 0 test
        % with kSay for that reason.
        IF kind = kDirect THEN
            handleDirect(slot, textAt, textLength)
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kRename THEN
            IF nameLength = 0 THEN
                refuse(nameAt, nameLength, reasonNeedNick, lenNeedNick)
                RETURN
            ENDIF
            IF sameName(slot, nameAt, nameLength) = 1 THEN
                RETURN                            % already your name: not news
            ENDIF
            findByName(nameAt, nameLength) =: taken
            IF taken >< 0 THEN
                refuse(nameAt, nameLength, reasonNickTaken, lenNickTaken)
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            % Renamed carries the NEW name and the OLD one as its text, so a
            % transcript on somebody's screen can be connected up. Built before
            % the slot changes, while the old name is still there.
            buildRenamed(slot, nameAt, nameLength) =: length
            copyNameIn(slot, nameAt, nameLength)
            broadcast(length, slot, 0)            % their room, asker too
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        % /who - answered to the ASKER ALONE, never broadcast. Everybody else
        % asking would then get a list they did not want, and the room would
        % spend a message on each of them.
        IF kind = kWho THEN
            % slot, not senderMagic: the answer is filtered to the ASKER'S
            % ROOM, so buildWho needs a seat to compare against.
            buildWho(slot) =: length
            sendTo(senderMagic, length)
            RETURN
        ENDIF


        % /list is NOT here any more - it moved ABOVE the membership guard,
        % where the registry always said it belonged. See the note there.

        % /topic - ask with an empty text, set with a non-empty one.
        %
        % A SET IS BROADCAST, AN ASK IS NOT. Everybody in the room needs to see
        % a topic change as it happens - it is a change to the room, not an
        % answer to one person - while an ask is nobody else's business.
        IF kind = kTopic THEN
            IF textLength = 0 THEN
                buildTopic(slot) =: length
                sendTo(senderMagic, length)
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            % TRUNCATED, NOT REFUSED. The length arrives from the peer and
            % PLANC checks no array bound, so a long topic would write straight
            % through the rest of the table. The client already stops at 64, so
            % this only ever bites a peer that is not our client - which is
            % exactly the case a bound has to survive.
            IF textLength > maxTopicLen THEN
                maxTopicLen =: textLength
            ENDIF

            claimTopic(slot) =: taken
            IF taken = 0 THEN
                % The table is full. SAY SO rather than dropping it: a topic
                % that silently does not stick is the kind of failure somebody
                % retries for ten minutes. refuse() sends for itself.
                refuse(nameAt, nameLength, reasonTopicFull, lenTopicFull)
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
                inBuf(textAt + i - 1) =: topicText(taken, i)
            ENDFOR
            textLength =: topicTextLen(taken)

            buildTopic(slot) =: length
            broadcast(length, slot, 0)        % 0 = leave nobody out
            RETURN
        ENDIF

        IF kind = kLeave THEN
            buildFromSlot(kLeft, slot, 0, 0) =: length
            0 =: memberUsed(slot)
            broadcast(length, slot, slot)         % their room, not them

            % Give the seat back to XROUT, or the room fills up permanently and
            % XROUT stops forwarding joins long after anybody left.
            xmpinfc(0, myPort, 1, 0) =: returnStatus
            RETURN
        ENDIF
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % The program.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    PROGRAM : chatServer
        XMMSGIDENTIFIER : msgIdent
        INTEGER : i

        % INISTACK RUNS EVERY START, and it must - the stack does not survive
        % the terminate. Everything below it is guarded instead.
        INISTACK stack

        % ---- THE HISTORY TABLE, CLEARED BY HAND -----------------------------
        %
        % PLANC DOES NOT PROMISE A MODULE VARIABLE STARTS AT ZERO, and this
        % file already carries one scar from assuming it did - see the note by
        % typedLen in the client. Three of these would be quietly fatal rather
        % than merely wrong:
        %
        %   histDev   a stale non-zero would be taken for an OPEN FILE and
        %             handed to MN120 as a device number;
        %   histSize  a stale ZERO is a DIVIDE BY ZERO the first time anybody
        %             speaks, because the ring is id MOD size;
        %   histNext  a stale value writes the first message into the middle of
        %             the ring and replays blocks that were never written.
        %
        % Cleared here, before anything can be received.
        FOR i IN 1:maxHistRooms DO
            0 =: histDev(i)
            0 =: histNext(i)
            histDefault =: histSize(i)
        ENDFOR

        % ---- NO FLAGS AT ALL. NOT XFWTF, AND NOT XFWAK EITHER. --------------
        %
        % XFWTF was never right here: a task already blocked in XFRCV is ACTIVE,
        % and RT-starting an active program does not queue a second run, so a
        % wake-up arriving during a block is simply LOST.
        %
        % BUT XFWAK IS ALSO WRONG NOW, AND IT WEDGED THE SERVER. Measured on
        % D100, 2026-08-21, with one client trying to join:
        %
        %   X-C LIST-PORTS
        %    No  Address  Owner-task   Qhead  Qlen  Chain WAK
        %     6  160057 152616 BAK06  161762     1      0   0   <- the ROOM port
        %    14  160147 152616 BAK06       0     0      6   1   <- the ADMIN port
        %
        % A message SITTING on the room port, and the task parked on the admin
        % port's wake-up instead. The server had logged its two START lines and
        % then written nothing more; the client waited for ever at "not in a
        % room yet". XFRCV CANNOT WAIT ON TWO PORTS - arming a wake on each does
        % not make one call watch both, and the wake-up does NOT deliver the
        % message even when it fires.
        %
        % XFWAK WAS LEFT OVER FROM A DESIGN THAT NO LONGER EXISTS. It belonged
        % to the original loop, which RETURNED when both ports were empty and
        % relied on the next message RT-starting the program again. That was
        % replaced by the MON 267B sleep below - the program now STAYS ALIVE and
        % goes round - and once it stays alive there is nothing for a wake-up to
        % do. Keeping it bought nothing and cost the whole server.
        %
        % Flags ZERO is XMSG's own no-wait: the call returns at once whether or
        % not a message was there. Both ports get polled every pass, and the
        % one-second sleep when both are empty is what keeps it from spinning.
        0 =: waitFlags
        2**XFSEC =: sendFlags

        % ---- OPEN ONCE, NOT ONCE PER START ---------------------------------
        IF NOT portsOpen THEN
            FOR i IN 1:maxSeats DO
                0 =: memberUsed(i)
                0 =: memberDead(i)
            ENDFOR

            % A CONNECTION port: XROUT holds the free-connection counter and
            % turns the joiner past maxSeats away for us, before this program
            % is woken. That is the whole reason no client knows the number.
            % ONE PORT, CALLED *CHAT. Every service on this machine is starred
            % and short - *FA-SERVER, *XFTRA, *COSPO, *TADADM - and the chat
            % server is one of them.
            %
            % IT USED TO BE CHAT-LOBBY, and that was two mistakes in one name.
            % CHAT-LOBBY is a ROOM name doing duty as a SERVICE name, and the
            % client built a DIFFERENT port name per room - "CHAT-" plus the
            % room - so every room needed its own registered port. That made
            % the set of rooms XROUT's data rather than the server's, with no
            % call to read it back; it made a room expensive to create; it
            % fragmented the seat count per port; and it turned a federated
            % room into the SAME NAME registered on two machines, which is a
            % collision rather than shared state.
            %
            % Now: one port, and the ROOM TRAVELS IN THE MESSAGE. LOBBY goes
            % back to being an ordinary room. See the_room_model in
            % DOC/protocols/chat-wire.json.
            xmpopcn(0, '*CHAT', uniqueName, maxSeats, myPort) &
                =: returnStatus
            IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL29', 'CHATSV: cannot open the room$')
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL11', 'SV: opn st=')
                OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
                RETURN
            ENDIF

            % The command port. XMPOPNM - a NORMAL named port, so an operator
            % costs the room nothing. See the declaration for why that matters.
            xmpopnm(0, admName(0:lenAdmName - 1), adminPort) =: returnStatus
            IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL28', 'CHATSV: no command port, st=')
                OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
                % NOT fatal. A server that will not start because nobody can
                % ask it for STATUS is worse than one that runs unattended.
            ENDIF

            % The trunk port. XMPOPNM for the same reason the command port is -
            % a peer that is switched off must cost the room nothing however
            % long it stays away.
            xmpopnm(0, trkName(0:lenTrkName - 1), trunkPort) =: returnStatus
            IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL26', 'CHATSV: no trunk port, st=')
                OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
                % NOT fatal, same judgement as the command port: a server that
                % refuses to serve its own room because it cannot federate is
                % worse than one that serves the room and cannot federate.
            ENDIF

            % Our own magic number, so an arrival can be tested against it.
            xmpfp2m(0, myPort, myMagic) =: returnStatus
            IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL18', 'SV: p2m failed st=')
                OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            ENDIF

            % ...and our own SYSTEM number out of it, so the trunk can tell our
            % own bounced letters from a peer saying hello. See mySystem.
            xmpfm2p(0, myMagic, senderPortNo, mySystem, &
                senderRtInfo, senderExtra) =: returnStatus

            % ONE BANNER, AND IT STAYS. An operator has to be able to tell a
            % server that is waiting from one that never got its room, and the
            % port number is what LIST-PORTS is matched against when it does
            % not. It prints on the FIRST start only - this program is now
            % started again by every arrival, and a banner per message would
            % bury the machine.
            0 =: nEmptyLet
            0 =: nRelayed
            0 =: nDupes
            0 =: myLineId
            1 =: seenAt
            0 =: seenUsed
            0 =: nBounced
            0 =: nRefused

            OUTPUT(1, 'AL24', 'CHATSV: room open, port ')
            OUTPUT(1, 'I6', myPort)
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL9', ' command ')
            OUTPUT(1, 'I6', adminPort)
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL7', ' trunk ')
            OUTPUT(1, 'I6', trunkPort)
            OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
            logLine('SV START room prt=', 18, myPort)
            logLine('SV START admin prt', 18, adminPort)
            logLine('SV START trunk prt', 18, trunkPort)

            TRUE =: portsOpen
        ENDIF

        DO
            % WAIT. XFWTF is what makes this block rather than spin; an RT
            % program that polls burns the machine for nothing. Without the
            % flag the status would come back MXNEXTM when nothing is waiting.
            % ---- DRAIN BOTH PORTS, THEN TERMINATE --------------------------
            %
            % The lobby first, because that is the busy one. If it is empty,
            % the command port. If BOTH are empty, both wake-ups are now armed
            % and there is nothing to do: RETURN, and the next transmission to
            % either port RT-starts this program again.
            %
            % HOW "EMPTY" IS TOLD FROM "GOT ONE", which is the whole hazard of
            % dropping XFWTF and is MEASURED rather than reasoned:
            %
            %   XMOK (0)        a SUCCESSFUL receive, with the type in the
            %                   msgType out-parameter. Vendor Appendix G tests
            %                   "IF RETURNSTATUS >< XMOK THEN bad status", and
            %                   D100 printed "SV: rcv st= 0 t= 2 len= 25".
            %   XMTNO..XMTPS    also a successful receive.
            %   XMXENTM (16896) an EMPTY port. Neither of the above.
            %
            % The manual's "a zero status is returned" for an empty port
            % describes the RAW MON call. XMPFRCV remaps it - the library's
            % empty answer is 16896, not 0 - so zero is unambiguous here. Had
            % that not been so, this loop could not have told an empty port
            % from a message and would have terminated on live traffic.
            FALSE =: fromAdmin
            FALSE =: fromTrunk
            xmpfrcv(waitFlags, myPort, msgType, remotePortHash, msgIdent, nBytes) &
                =: returnStatus

            IF returnStatus = XMXENTM THEN
                TRUE =: fromAdmin
                xmpfrcv(waitFlags, adminPort, msgType, remotePortHash, &
                    msgIdent, nBytes) =: returnStatus

                % ---- AND THEN THE TRUNK PORT ------------------------------
                % Third in the round, and last on purpose: people in the room
                % come before an operator, and an operator before a machine
                % saying hello. Trunk traffic is the least urgent thing here.
                IF returnStatus = XMXENTM THEN
                    FALSE =: fromAdmin
                    TRUE =: fromTrunk
                    xmpfrcv(waitFlags, trunkPort, msgType, remotePortHash, &
                        msgIdent, nBytes) =: returnStatus
                ENDIF

                IF returnStatus = XMXENTM THEN
                    % ---- BOTH EMPTY: SLEEP, DO NOT END ------------------
                    %
                    % THIS USED TO "RETURN", AND THAT WAS REFUTED ON THE
                    % MACHINE. MEASURED on D100 2026-08-20: @RT CHATSER ran,
                    % drained both empty ports, returned - and
                    % LIST-RT-DESCRIPTION then said PASSIVE with 0 CPU units
                    % while LIST-NAMES showed NEITHER CHAT-LOBBY NOR
                    % CHAT-ADMIN. SINTRAN CLOSES A TASK'S PORTS WHEN THE TASK
                    % TERMINATES, so the XFWAK wake-up had nothing left to
                    % arrive on and the server could never be restarted.
                    %
                    % The manual's wake-up mechanism is real, but it assumes a
                    % task that STAYS ALIVE to be woken. Both halves of that
                    % were already written down here and were not put together.
                    %
                    % So: sleep and go round. MON 267B suspends the program for
                    % a given time and continues after the call - no spin, no
                    % termination, ports stay open. Unit type 1 is basic time
                    % units of 1/50 second, so 50 is one second: long enough to
                    % cost nothing, short enough that an operator typing STATUS
                    % does not notice the wait.
                    %
                    % A BARE LOOP WITH NO SLEEP IS NOT AN OPTION. When XMSG
                    % died under this server the receive stopped blocking and
                    % this loop burned the machine until somebody pressed ESC.
                    50 =: sleepUnits
                    1 =: sleepKind
                    MONITOR_CALL('TimeOut', sleepUnits, sleepKind, sleepWhy)

                    % ---- ONE SECOND HAS PASSED: AGE THE TRUNKS -------------
                    %
                    % THE IDLE SLEEP IS THE CLOCK, and it is the only one this
                    % program has. Doing it here rather than on every pass round
                    % the loop means the tick is a SECOND, not "however fast
                    % messages happen to be arriving" - a busy room must not
                    % make peers time out faster.
                    peerTick
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
            % A RECEIVE IS A MESSAGE ONLY WHEN THE STATUS IS A MESSAGE TYPE.
            % Not "= XMOK" and not "> 0". The library remaps statuses: 0 is OK
            % for other calls but is NOT a message type, so comparing to it
            % here discards every message; and 16896 (XMXENTM, nothing there)
            % and every error are positive, so "> 0" accepts an empty port.
            % The constants file settles it - "Message types: Returned as
            % successfull status from XFRCV", XMTNO=1 through XMTPS=6.
            % PRINT THE STATUS OF EVERY RECEIVE, OUTSIDE THE TEST.
            %
            % This line is here because the test below threw the message away
            % and every diagnostic was INSIDE it, so the server looked as if it
            % had never woken at all. MEASURED 2026-08-18: CHAT-LOBBY went from
            % 8 free seats to 6 across two joins, which proves XROUT accepted
            % both letters and forwarded them here - "XROUT will decrement the
            % counter and forward the whole message to portName" - while this
            % program printed nothing and went straight back to waiting.
            %
            % A gate must never be the only thing that can tell you it closed.
            % This printed on EVERY receive during bring-up. It now prints only
            % when the receive is NOT one this server accepts - which is the case
            % the original comment was really about. A message that arrives and is
            % handled says so by what it does; a message thrown away by the test
            % below still has nothing else to speak for it.
            % ---- KNOW WHEN TO STOP -------------------------------------
            % MEASURED 2026-08-18, and it is why this block exists at all.
            % XMSG went down under a running server. XMPFRCV then stopped
            % blocking - there is nothing to wait ON - and returned 16933,
            % XMXENRU, "XMSG not running", instantly, for ever. This loop
            % printed that line as fast as the terminal could take it and
            % burned the machine until somebody pressed ESC.
            %
            % A WAIT THAT CANNOT WAIT IS A SPIN. The XFWTF flag is what makes
            % this loop cheap, and it quietly stops meaning anything the moment
            % the thing it waits on is gone. Any program built around a
            % blocking call needs an answer for that, and "print and go round
            % again" is not one.
            %
            % Two ways out, and they are different on purpose:
            %
            %   XMXENRU        STOP. Nothing this program can do brings XMSG
            %                  back - an operator has to restart it - so
            %                  retrying is not persistence, it is a busy loop
            %                  with no end. Say so plainly and leave.
            %
            %   anything else  RETRY, but not for ever. An unknown status may
            %                  well clear; the same one arriving maxBadRuns
            %                  times running has not cleared and will not.
            %
            % A GOOD RECEIVE CLEARS THE COUNT, so the limit is on a RUN of
            % failures rather than on failures for the life of the program. A
            % server that has been up for a week and sees twenty scattered odd
            % statuses is healthy; one that sees twenty in a row is not.
            IF returnStatus = XMOK OR &
               (returnStatus >= XMTNO AND returnStatus <= XMTPS) THEN
                0 =: badRuns
            ELSE
                IF returnStatus >< XMXENTM THEN
                    badRuns + 1 =: badRuns

                    % Print the first few and then stop shouting. The whole
                    % fault above was a message repeated without limit, and a
                    % diagnostic that fills the screen hides the one line that
                    % matters.
                    IF badRuns <= maxNoisy THEN
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL23', 'SV: receive refused st=')
                        OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
                        logLine('SV rcv refused st=', 18, returnStatus)
                    ENDIF

                    IF returnStatus = XMXENRU THEN
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL31', 'CHATSV: XMSG is not running - $')
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL31', 'CHATSV: stopping, restart XMSG$')
                        RETURN
                    ENDIF

                    IF badRuns > maxBadRuns THEN
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL30', 'CHATSV: too many bad receives$')
                        OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
                        RETURN
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ENDIF

            % ACCEPT XMOK TOO - Appendix G's SERVER does exactly this.
            %
            % The old test was the range XMTNO..XMTPS alone, defended by a
            % comment claiming 0 "is NOT a message type, so comparing to it here
            % discards every message". The vendor sample says otherwise: after
            % XMPFRCV it tests "IF RETURNSTATUS >< XMOK THEN ... bad status" and
            % only THEN looks at the MSGTYPE out-parameter to check for XMROU.
            % So XMOK is a successful receive, and the message type belongs in
            % msgType, not in the status.
            %
            % Both forms are accepted here rather than one being swapped for the
            % other, because the print above will say which one actually turns up
            % and guessing is what put the range test here in the first place.
            IF returnStatus = XMOK OR &
               (returnStatus >= XMTNO AND returnStatus <= XMTPS) THEN
                % The FULL magic number of the sender - XMPFRCV hands back only
                % a hashed port, and we must be able to answer.
                % A "got t= len=" line printed here to separate "never woke"
                % from "woke, replied, reply lost". It said to remove it once the
                % join works, and the join works.
                % THE RECEIVE'S OWN nBytes, KEPT BEFORE ANYTHING OVERWRITES IT.
                % For an ordinary message it is the length; for a RETURNED one
                % the guide says it is something else entirely - "If msgType is
                % XMTRE, msgLengthOrStat contains the error status" - and
                % xmpfmst below writes over it either way.
                nBytes =: receiveStat

                xmpfmst(0, msgIdent, msgType, senderMagic, nBytes) =: returnStatus

                % WHICH MACHINE SENT THIS. A magic number is a port and a system
                % packed together, and XMPFM2P is the library's own way of taking
                % it apart - "Take a magic number apart into port number and
                % system number" (xmp-api.json, transcribed from XMP-B02:DEFS).
                %
                % The TRUNK needs it and nothing else does: a peer is identified
                % by system number, and an unsolicited HELLO from a machine we
                % have never been told about has to be answerable without any
                % configuration having happened first. Reading it from the magic
                % means there is nothing to remember and nothing to go stale.
                0 =: senderSystem
                xmpfm2p(0, senderMagic, senderPortNo, senderSystem, &
                    senderRtInfo, senderExtra) =: returnStatus

                % CLAMP BEFORE READING. nBytes is the sender's message size,
                % handed back by XMSG - not a size this program chose. Passing
                % it straight in lets any peer that sends more than inBufSize
                % bytes write past the end of inBuf. That is a WRITE overflow
                % and it happens before a single field is parsed, so none of
                % the careful field checks in handleMessage can save it.
                nBytes =: readAmount
                IF readAmount > inBufSize THEN
                    inBufSize =: readAmount
                ENDIF

                xmpfrea(0, 0, &
                    ADDR(inBuf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS, &
                    0, readAmount, readLength) =: returnStatus

                xmpfrel(0, msgIdent) =: returnStatus

                % A 48-BYTE RAW DUMP OF EVERY ARRIVAL STOOD HERE, and it is the
                % single diagnostic that solved this program. It is worth saying
                % what it did before it goes, because the lesson outlives it.
                %
                % It began at 8 bytes and printed nothing useful for the arrival
                % that mattered. Widened to 48 it showed the join in full:
                %
                %   123  65  0 12 | 255 10 'CHAT-LOBBY' | 1 5 'RONNY' 0 0
                %
                % Byte 0 was 123 - the SERIAL NUMBER this program's own client
                % hands to XMPBLET. So what arrives starts with the XSLET LETTER
                % HEADER, and handleMessage had been reading that header as if it
                % were a chat message all along. The header skip below is the fix,
                % and the layout under it is read off those bytes.
                %
                % The general form: a dump of what actually arrived beat every
                % re-reading of the code, and it beat the manual too - the manual
                % was right and was being applied to the wrong offset.

                % A "fromSelf YES/no" line printed here. It earned its place once:
                % the answer came back YES, the server's own reply arriving
                % straight back at this port, which no amount of reading the guide
                % would have suggested. That is recorded rather than printed now.
                % THE SENDER'S MAGIC NUMBER AND OUR OWN were printed side by side
                % here, to answer one suspicion: the welcome was being sent with
                % status 0 and never arriving, so was senderMagic - taken from
                % XMPFMST on a letter that came via XROUT - an address we could
                % actually send BACK to?
                %
                % IT WAS. The two numbers were consistent and the reply address
                % was never the bug. What that ruled out is what sent the search
                % to the CLIENT's receive test, where XMPFRCV was returning XMOK,
                % a "1..6 only" test was rejecting it, and the welcome was being
                % dequeued and dropped in silence.
                %
                % A diagnostic that clears a suspicion is worth as much as one
                % that confirms it, and this one cost a push-and-compile round
                % trip to add. That is why it is written down instead of deleted.

                % ---- A LETTER ARRIVES WEARING ITS XROUT HEADER --------------
                % XROUT forwards THE WHOLE MESSAGE, header included. So a letter
                % that came via XROUT has the XSLET header in front of the chat
                % message, and the chat message has to be found behind it.
                %
                % ND-60.164.3 EN COSMOS Programmer Guide, appendix B, "XROUT
                % Message Format", states the layout - this is read from the
                % manual, not inferred from one capture:
                %
                %   byte 0     serial number, echoed back unchanged by XROUT
                %   byte 1     the service number (XSLET = 65); XROUT overwrites
                %              it with the return status on a reply
                %   bytes 2-3  LENGTH OF THE REMAINDER OF THE MESSAGE in bytes,
                %              "followed by a sequence of parameter blocks"
                %
                % So the parameter blocks occupy exactly the bytes that length
                % counts, and OUR data starts right after them:
                %
                %   payloadAt = 4 + the length word
                %
                % The manual is explicit that the user data is untouched: "The
                % remainder of the message will not be looked at by XROUT ... thus
                % the user data can be coded in any format legible to the
                % receiving (server) task."
                %
                % MEASURED 2026-08-18, the 25-byte join, which agrees:
                %   123  65  0 12 | 255 10 'CHAT-LOBBY' | 1 5 'RONNY' 0 0
                %    ^serial ^len=12   ^param 1: string, 10 bytes  ^payload at 16
                %
                % DERIVED, NEVER HARD-CODED. 16 is only right for a ten-character
                % room name; the parameter block moves with the name, and a
                % constant here would break the moment /join names a longer room.
                %
                % The alternative reading - 6 + the name length - fits this one
                % capture just as well and is WRONG in general: it cannot see a
                % second parameter block, and XSLET has an optional third.
                %
                % The payload is slid down to offset 0 so that handleMessage
                % parses one layout and one only. Every other route into it -
                % an ordinary XMTNO message straight from a magic number - has no
                % header at all, and this is the single place that knows that.
                % ---- A MESSAGE OF OURS THAT COULD NOT BE DELIVERED ----------
                % This is what XFSEC bought, and it has to be caught BEFORE
                % handleMessage: the bytes in inBuf are our own outgoing Said or
                % Joined coming home, so parsing them as an arrival would have
                % the room answering itself.
                %
                % THE QUESTION THIS BRANCH WAS BUILT TO ANSWER, AND THE ANSWER.
                %
                % A returned message carries what WE wrote, and what we wrote
                % names the SPEAKER, not the member it could not reach - so it
                % was not obvious that anything here identifies the destination.
                % XMPFMST hands back a magic number for an ordinary arrival; the
                % guide never says what that field holds on a returned one.
                %
                % MEASURED 2026-08-18 on D100, and it IS the destination:
                %
                %   SV: returned msg, st=  16915
                %   SV: it names slot          2
                %   SV: reaped dead slot       2
                %
                % 16915 is XMXEIMA, "Invalid magic number" - the reason the
                % message came home. Note where that code turned up: as the
                % REASON ON A RETURN, not as the status of the send. The first
                % attempt at this fix tested XMPSEND's own status for exactly
                % that number and never fired, because XMPSEND answered XMOK and
                % handed the message to a port that was already gone.
                %
                % The slot print stays. It costs nothing - a member has to die
                % before it runs - and it separates "one of ours bounced" from
                % "something bounced", which is the first thing an operator
                % needs to know and the one thing the reap line cannot say.
                IF msgType = XMTRE THEN
                    nBounced + 1 =: nBounced
                    logLine('SV bounced msg st=', 18, receiveStat)

                    findByMagic(senderMagic) =: wasMember

                    % PRINT ONLY WHEN IT IS ONE OF OURS. The note above says the
                    % point of this line is to separate "one of ours bounced"
                    % from "something bounced", and only the first is worth
                    % waking an operator for - it means a member has died. The
                    % other case is now just a number in STATUS, because it was
                    % arriving often enough to bury everything else.
                    IF wasMember >< 0 THEN
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL22', 'SV: returned msg, st= ')
                        OUTPUT(1, 'I6', receiveStat)
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL22', 'SV: it names slot     ')
                        OUTPUT(1, 'I6', wasMember)
                        OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
                    ENDIF

                    IF wasMember >< 0 THEN
                        1 =: memberDead(wasMember)
                        1 =: deadFound
                    ENDIF
                ELSE
                IF msgType = XMROU THEN
                    % THE SERVICE BYTE, TAKEN BEFORE ANYTHING MOVES. The payload
                    % is slid down to offset 0 below, so by the time the seat
                    % rule runs inBuf(1) is chat data, not the header. Reading
                    % it there is how the feedback loop went unnoticed in
                    % review - the test looked right and was inspecting the
                    % wrong bytes.
                    inBuf(1) =: letterService

                    4 + inBuf(2) * 256 + inBuf(3) =: payloadAt

                    % A letter with nothing behind the header is a connect this
                    % program has no answer for. Dropping it says so; the seat is
                    % already spent either way. Note this is >=, not >: a payload
                    % that starts exactly AT the end is an empty payload.
                    IF payloadAt >= readLength THEN
                        % COUNTED, NOT PRINTED. This is an EXPECTED arrival, not
                        % a fault: XROUT's answer to a seat return comes back on
                        % this very port as a routed message with no payload, so
                        % there is one of these per seat returned. Printing it
                        % each time buried the terminal - STATUS reports the
                        % count, and a count that climbs while the room is quiet
                        % is the feedback loop described below coming back.
                        nEmptyLet + 1 =: nEmptyLet
                    ELSE
                        % (the seat for this arrival is settled after
                        %  handleMessage, by the one rule below)
                        readLength - payloadAt =: readLength
                        FOR i IN 1:readLength DO
                            inBuf(payloadAt + i - 1) =: inBuf(i - 1)
                        ENDFOR

                        % ---- SEAT ACCOUNTING, and it belongs HERE ----------
                        % The seat is spent by the ARRIVAL of a routed message,
                        % not by any particular kind of message inside it. Doing
                        % it per kind is what the C# side got wrong: a letter
                        % carrying anything but a join, or a letter too damaged
                        % to decode at all, never reaches a handler - so no
                        % handler can give its seat back, and the room silently
                        % loses a seat for good.
                        %
                        % XROUT decremented its free-connection counter to get
                        % this letter here, so a seat is already spent by the
                        % time this line runs. There are now exactly two places
                        % that give one back, and between them they cover both
                        % ways a member can stop being one:
                        %
                        %   handleMessage, kLeave   they said goodbye;
                        %   reapDead                they did not, and a send to
                        %                           them came back saying the
                        %                           port is gone.
                        findByMagic(senderMagic) =: wasMember

                        handleMessage
                    ENDIF

                    % ---- THE SEAT BELONGS TO THE ARRIVAL, SO SETTLE IT HERE ----
                    % MEASURED 2026-08-18 by a load run: twenty C# clients joined
                    % at once and CHAT-LOBBY went from 16 free seats to 2 and
                    % stayed there, even though every welcomed member left again
                    % cleanly. Fourteen seats gone.
                    %
                    % The comment a few lines up has described this hole all
                    % along - "a letter carrying anything but a join, or a letter
                    % too damaged to decode at all, never reaches a handler - so
                    % no handler can give its seat back" - and named it as
                    % something the C# side got wrong. It was wrong HERE too. The
                    % code only ever returned a seat on a clean /leave or a reap,
                    % so an empty letter, a join refused because the name was
                    % taken, and a letter from a stranger each cost a seat for
                    % good. Under a burst that is most of them.
                    %
                    % ONE RULE, APPLIED TO EVERY LETTER: XROUT spent a seat to
                    % deliver this, so if the sender is not sitting in the room
                    % once we have finished with it, nobody is holding that seat
                    % and it must go straight back.
                    %
                    % This does NOT double-count with the /leave and reapDead
                    % paths. A leave arrives as an ORDINARY message, port to
                    % port, and never comes through this branch at all - only the
                    % JOIN is a letter. So each member's seat is returned exactly
                    % once, by whichever of the three applies.
                    %
                    % KNOWN GAP, said plainly rather than left to be discovered:
                    % a letter from somebody who IS already a member keeps its
                    % seat and should not. Clients send port to port after
                    % joining, so it does not arise in normal use, and guessing a
                    % fix for a case never seen would be worse than naming it.
                    % ---- ONLY A REAL LETTER SPENT A SEAT --------------------
                    % MEASURED, and it is the worst kind of bug: a FEEDBACK
                    % LOOP that this very block created. Four users joined and
                    % left, and CHAT-LOBBY went from 16 free seats to 3470 while
                    % the terminal filled with "DROP empty letter" for ever.
                    %
                    % Here is the loop. Returning a seat means sending XMPINFC
                    % to XROUT. XROUT ANSWERS, and its answer arrives back on
                    % this very port as a routed message with no chat payload -
                    % which the code above drops as an empty letter, and which
                    % this rule then read as "a letter whose sender is not a
                    % member", so it returned ANOTHER seat, which produced
                    % ANOTHER answer. The note at the foot of this file has
                    % warned since the first version that the XMPINFC reply
                    % "comes back later on our own port as an ordinary message"
                    % and that ignoring it was "safe by accident until somebody
                    % says so". It stopped being safe the moment this rule
                    % started acting on every routed arrival.
                    %
                    % THE DISCRIMINATOR IS BYTE 1. A client's letter carries the
                    % XSLET service number, 65. XROUT OVERWRITES that byte with
                    % a return status when it answers - the header comment in
                    % the receive loop says so, and it is the same test the C#
                    % load client uses to spot a refused letter. So a letter
                    % with 65 in byte 1 came from a client and spent a seat;
                    % anything else is XROUT talking to us and spent nothing.
                    IF letterService = XSLET THEN
                        findByMagic(senderMagic) =: nowMember
                        IF nowMember = 0 THEN
                            xmpinfc(0, myPort, 1, 0) =: returnStatus
                            IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
                                OUTPUT(1, 'AL22', 'SV: seat return failed')
                                OUTPUT(1, 'I6', returnStatus)
                                OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
                                logLine('SV seat back st=  ', 18, returnStatus)
                            ENDIF
                        ENDIF
                    ENDIF
                ELSE
                    % No header on this path: an ordinary message sent straight
                    % to our magic number is exactly the bytes the peer wrote.
                    handleMessage
                ENDIF
                ENDIF

                % ---- AND NOW BURY ANYBODY WHO TURNED OUT TO BE GONE ---------
                % One place, after everything the arrival caused has finished,
                % because that is the only depth at which outBuf is free. Both
                % branches above lead here on purpose: a member can be found
                % dead by any broadcast, and the routed and direct paths both
                % broadcast.
                IF deadFound >< 0 THEN
                    reapDead
                ENDIF

                % ---- AND STOP, IF AN OPERATOR ASKED ------------------------
                % Here rather than where the AdminStop arrived, so the reply
                % path and the reaping above finish first. A stop that abandons
                % a half-sent message is not a clean stop.
                %
                % This RETURN is different from the one on an empty drain. That
                % one goes to sleep and is woken by the next arrival; this one
                % ends the program for good - the ports go with it and SINTRAN
                % clears the names, measured 2026-08-18.
                IF stopWanted THEN
                    OUTPUT(1, 'AL17', 'CHATSV: stopping$')
                    RETURN
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDDO
    ENDROUTINE

ENDMODULE

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% STILL NOT DONE, and worth saying rather than leaving to be met:
%
%  1. THIS ITEM USED TO READ "Nothing has been compiled", and it is kept here
%     in corrected form rather than deleted, because everything it worried about
%     has now been settled by the compiler itself rather than by argument:
%     INTEGER4, two-dimensional BYTES ARRAY, the implicit extra ARRAY keyword on
%     a BYTES declaration, BOOLEAN := TRUE, and the XMMSGIDENTIFIER and
%     XMUSERADDRESS names out of XMP-B02:IMPT. All of it compiles and runs.
%
%     The one thing the list did NOT anticipate is the one that cost the most:
%     a letter arrives with its XSLET header still in front of it. No amount of
%     checking declarations against a manual would have found that - only the
%     raw dump did.
%
%  2. The seat returned on Leave uses XMPINFC(flags, portNumber, extraConn,
%     serialNumber). Its reply comes back later on our own port as an ordinary
%     message, which handleMessage will see and not understand. Decide what to
%     do with it - ignoring an unknown kind is already the behaviour, so this
%     is safe, but it is safe by accident until somebody says so.
%
%  3. Each reason string carries its length as a separate constant, because
%     PLANC has no LENGTH routine and SIZE's meaning for a BYTES parameter is
%     not established. A string and its number must be changed together.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
